import type { V205BookData } from '../types';

export const DATA: V205BookData = {
  book: "2 Samuel",
  display: "Second Samuel",
  chapters: {
    "1": {"chapter":1,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 01"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously this book of the Bible takes place after the events in 1 Samuel. Though it is not Samuel who is writing the events here (he died in our last book), it was Samuel who initiated the rise of the kingdom of Israel. Samuel had put Saul in charge of the kingdom, and Saul squandered his position and did everything humanly possible to go against the commands of the Lord. While it was the people who chose this Saul character, God wanted to give them what they wanted, and in the end they regretted their decision. God desires a king that would seek HIM, and so He sets up this young man, David, to take over the thrown in the future. David knows this, but at this particular time (as we will pick up in 2 Samuel) David probably doesn't think becoming king would even be possible."},{"kind":"para","text":"Last we left off with David he was living away from Israel in the land of their enemies \"Ziklag.\" Ziklag was a Philistine territory, and he and his men have been hiding out here for quite some time do to Saul's continuous search and desire to kill David. Israel likely looks at David as a traitor at this point, and so David has kept his distance due to the fact that he and his family are Israelites, and he doesn't want to cause any trouble with those that he loves."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we pick back up after Saul had died a quite brutal death, and we see David's character when he finds out about what has happened to his king and the kings family."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":10,"title":"The News Of Saul","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that this man went out of his way to find David on purpose. We must remember that David was a war hero. He was a mighty used man of God. He wasn't particularly a \"mighty\" man (he faced off with mighty men like Goliath), but he was certainly an anointed man, and that was proven when he took down mighty men left and right. This man of Saul knew where David was, and he knew WHO David was, and the defeat of Saul was a distraught and terrible occurrence for the children of Israel."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So, David knows that there was a battle happening between Israel and the Philistines. He was almost sent into battle against the Israelites in chapter 29 of 1 Samuel."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then said the princes of the Philistines, What do these Hebrews here? And Achish said unto the princes of the Philistines, Is not this David, the servant of Saul the king of Israel, which hath been with me these days, or these years, and I have found no fault in him since he fell unto me unto this day? And the princes of the Philistines were wroth with him; and the princes of the Philistines said unto him, Make this fellow return, that he may go again to his place which thou hast appointed him, and let him not go down with us to battle, lest in the battle he be an adversary to us: for wherewith should he reconcile himself unto his master? should it not be with the heads of these men? Is not this David, of whom they sang one to another in dances, saying, Saul slew his thousands, and David his ten thousands?","ref":"1 Samuel 29:3-5"},{"kind":"para","text":"It seems even the Philistines knew the tales of David. They must have forgotten (or maybe just didn't know) that David was the young man who killed their most valiant warrior so many years prior to this. David knew there was a fight taking place, but I'm sure he suspected the fight to turn out quite differently (though Samuel had warned the whole nation of Israel that Saul would die and the kingdom would be given to someone else)."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So, we pick up this chapter with David coming back from quite a battle (rescuing the wives and children of his men (including his own wives)). He was on a victory high. On the third day, he was brought low rather quickly with this news. Jonathan was David's best friend. Saul may have hated David, but David wanted what was best for Saul. He never wished any ill on Saul."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And when the men of Israel that were on the other side of the valley, and they that were on the other side Jordan, saw that the men of Israel fled, and that Saul and his sons were dead, they forsook the cities, and fled; and the Philistines came and dwelt in them.","ref":"1 Samuel 31:7"},{"kind":"para","text":"Notice it says that Saul’s \"son(s)\" were dead. It was not just Jonathan that died this day. Saul had a few sons, and he lost more than one. Jonathan, here, was prominent because David truly loved Jonathan. He felt one in the same with Jonathan (Of course, not in a homosexual way as some may try to twist it). David loved Jonathan like a brother. Like a soldier loves a soldier. He loved him like the Lord loves us. No doubt (as we will see) he was saddened by the loss and death of Saul, but he was far more distraught by the thought that his great encourager (Jonathan) might also be killed in a battle that he shouldn't have been in, too."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I think David is facing a little bit of denial here. Again, he is coming off a victory high. Everything was looking good for him at the moment. He had no worries about the battle that was going on with Israel and the Philistines because he knew that Saul was great at leading them to victory. I believe the Lord was with Israel every time they faced a victory (just look to the book of Judges for that proof), but we know that God was not with Saul in this battle."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And when Saul saw the host of the Philistines, he was afraid, and his heart greatly trembled. And when Saul enquired of the LORD, the LORD answered him not, neither by dreams, nor by Urim, nor by prophets. Then said Saul unto his servants, Seek me a woman that hath a familiar spirit, that I may go to her, and enquire of her. And his servants said to him, Behold, there is a woman that hath a familiar spirit at Endor.","ref":"1 Samuel 28:5-7"},{"kind":"para","text":"Saul didn't take that seriously, however, and it cost him his life. David didn't want to believe that Saul and Jonathan were dead. He wanted proof. He didn't want to just take this random guy’s word for it (though he fit the part quite well with his ripped clothes and the fear in his eyes)."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Saul seems to have a small amount of life left in him at this moment. Now we might look at this and think, okay, this is great, Saul can give a final testimony here. But imagine the amount of pain and agony that Saul is facing at this moment. He has tried to kill himself with his own spear, and yet he is still alive. How is it possible that he is talking through so much pain and anguish? He seems to see this young man and calls him over to talk to him about something..."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Hold the phone! David's attention peaks right here. David just had a victory over the Amalekites (as they were the ones that stole their women and children and burned their houses), and this guy (not knowing that David had killed the Amalekites just two days ago) admits he's an Amalekite! So many thoughts had to be going through David's head right at this part of the conversation."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Saul asks this random Amalekite stranger to finish him off (it is obvious that Saul couldn't do it on his own, and now he is just sitting there suffering, or this guy is lying). Saul shouldn't have taken his own life, though. It would have been better for him to be killed by the enemy or tortured, knowing that David would be used of God to do something about Saul."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There's the proof that David needed. Of course, they didn't have cellphones back then to take pictures of the occasion, so he brought proof with the king’s crown and bracelet. David had a victory high killing the Amalekites, and it was a low defeat hearing that it was an Amalekite that killed (supposedly) off his king. Seeing the bracelet and crown had to throw David off."}]}]},{"start":11,"end":16,"title":"Weeping Over the Lost","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David has lost a lot in his time through running from Saul these past few years. He lost his first wife (he marries two more against his better judgement), he lost his homeland, he lost the chance to go visit his brothers and family, he's lost connection with the house of God and worships God in caves and secretly in the land of the Philistines, and now he has lost his best friend and king."},{"kind":"para","text":"This was a good time for David to weep. This, obviously, really hurt David. This hurt the rest of the men as well. I think the rest of the men were more hurt by the fact that David was finally broken. After all the David had experienced and gone through, I don't think there was much crying taking place (other than with Jonathan just before David runs out of Israel for his life). This is the first time that his men are seeing their leader cry. I don't know if you've ever seen a strong, faithful, Godly man cry, but when they do it's not easy to shun your emotions from them."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David didn't forget that this man was an Amalekite. I think he wanted to be sure he didn't mistaken what he heard. The man tells the truth..."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Is David justified in this action here? I believe so. David (by God's sovereign leading before all of this) was now the anointed king of Israel (whether the Israelites knew it or not). God tasked Saul before with killing off all the Amalekites in the land, and Saul didn't do it. He went against the commands of God, and it ended up costing much more than his own life in the end. David was justified to kill this man because this man killed God's anointed (at least so he says; I have reason to believe this guy just made up a story after seeing Saul’s dead body so he could gain some clout). Either way, he took responsibility for killing Israel’s king! This man would have been better off just steering clear altogether and walking away, but he let his curiosity get the better of him. If the story was fake, this man got himself killed for no reason. The story could have been true; it just doesn’t match up to what we read in the last chapter of 1 Samuel."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":27,"title":"A Lament","entries":[{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Lamentations are much like eulogies. At a funeral, you want to say good and wholesome last words for the family and friends of the deceased to remember him by. David's lamentation for Saul and Jonathan is a very heartfelt one."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If you don't remember, during the time of Saul's entrance as king, the Israelites didn't have iron weapons. That was all controlled by the Philistines. Saul, however, taught people how to shoot with a bow and arrow, and he was very good at it. David uses that gift from Saul as a memorial to his legacy continuing from them."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David's lamentation is not just a eulogy for the loss, it's a poem for the testimony of Israel's leadership. The first king of Israel was beautiful, and he was killed."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Gath and Askelon were Philistine places. David didn't want the Philistines to feel victorious over this cause."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Saul was anointed by Samuel with oil (even with Samuel's disdain for putting a king in Israel). Saul was a provenly anointed man. But God also couldn't affect his heart. Saul was a hardhearted warrior, and he chose that path for himself. Whether anyone liked it or not, God put Saul on the throne, and in all honesty, it was God who took Saul off the throne. He was anointed with oil, that's for sure, but he had no anointing of God's spirit for he rejected it every time."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jonathan and Saul were courageous and valiant men. They were not to be looked at as weak or worthless. They did more for Israel than anyone else in their time, and they deserved respect for the things they had to put up with. Saul might have been somewhat of a monster, but he was the leader of God's chosen people, and he didn't quit doing that (even though there might have been many ample reasons to do so). That's quite a feat in and of itself."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jonathan honored his father (though Saul almost got Jonathan killed several times, and Saul took a lot of credit for Jonathan’s innovation as well as his boldness in facing off against the Philistines). But Jonathan honored his father, and that was proven even down to their untimely deaths..."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Say what you want about Saul (and there is plenty of truth to be said), Saul did bring prosperity to the Israelites. He made them stronger. He made them richer. He started them off quite well for what is to come. David is not going to have to \"build a kingdom\" when he takes over. He is going to inherit the one that was already started."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The first part of this lamentation was about Saul. David gives Saul his due diligence. Now he wants to focus on his beloved friend, Jonathan..."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I believe David could have given up all the love in the world to have the love of Jonathan again. The kind of friendship that David and Jonathan had is an extremely rare one to find today. If you find a friendship with this kind of love, hold onto it for dear life and don't let go. Chances are, you might not find a friendship like David and Jonathan. You will likely find some great friendships, but nothing like this."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now I want to address the fact that (again) the homosexual crowd has used this verse to twist it into their personal agenda (I've talked very deeply about this in my book \"Born of Adversity\"). The type of love that David and Jonathan had was much deeper than a surface-level love. David says, \"The type of love that Jonathan and I had was better than a fleshly or worldly love with women.\" Better translated would be, \"I loved Jonathan, I lust women.\" Jonathan and David had a deep connection, and David will always miss out on that for the rest of his life."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jonathan was a mighty man. He was a strong man. He was a warrior. He was a friend. He will be missed."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":27}]},
    "2": {"chapter":2,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 02"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we see David receive the news about Saul's death, and it really burdens him. He was sad about Saul's death, and he was even more sad about hearing that Jonathan died with him. This was a very big loss to David, and it was an eyeopener for him as well because he knows that he is to be king in Israel."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, he talks to the Lord about his next steps in taking kingship of Israel. He doesn't want to just go in and take over, he wants to do this the right way so that everyone in Israel is on the same page. This is not what takes place, however, and drama begins to arise in the land of Israel."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":7,"title":"King of Judah","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Israel is currently without leadership, and David knows this. He also knows that he's supposed to be the next king of Israel, but he doesn't just want to go in and take over as if all of Judah knew this (though they did). Rather, he wanted the transition to be casual and formal. So he does the best thing any leader can do: He goes to God and waits for directions. Instead of God saying, \"The land is yours, go and take it,\" God says, \"Go to Hebron.\" Hebron was a city that was close to him at the time. David was living in Philistine country, if you don't remember, and so Hebron was just north of his current location, right on the border of Israel and his current dwelling."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, we talked about when he took two wives, why this was wrong, but we notice that these two wives have more of a story than just David marrying them. He's taking them with him, and this will become a problem later."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David and his people are slowly moving back into the land they belonged to. They are trying to leave the Philistine company and are trying to get back to what they know and are assured of. They really didn't belong with the enemy of Israel. They didn't belong with that kind because they were purchased and possessed by the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"You will never feel comfortable if you have been redeemed to stay in a place that is not with others who are redeemed. I understand why David had to go there and I know his thoughts towards the move, but it was still not the right place for him. Moving back was the right direction for David to go, and it was probably a sigh of relief as the men followed, knowing that they were going back to their proper territory."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems the Israelites in Judah were welcoming to David returning and were willing to make him king. They knew they needed a leader, and they knew that David was next in line. I don't think they fully comprehended the type of leader that David would make. I don't think even David fully understood how God was going to use him. I think he was a humble king at this moment. I feel he didn't know what he was doing. But we will see that in the coming chapters. Jabesh-gilead took Saul and his family and buried their king with them."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Most people in the land would probably find reason to be upset with Saul. Saul made a lot of very bad decisions, and a lot of very innocent people suffered for it. But, nevertheless, he was their king. He was their leader. They could not disrespect the Lord's anointed."},{"kind":"para","text":"We are very spoiled in our country today. We don't like our leader, and we make it very well known. There are many who curse and cry about what our leader is doing. I think there is reason to certainly be concerned about what your leader thinks and desires. I am concerned myself. But I do not wish that he make bad decisions. When the leader makes bad decisions, the people suffer (we have seen that in our own country, and we will see that in the people of Israel as well). I do not stand with those that wish ill upon our leadership. No, I pray for the better. I pray that our leader would become a Godly man, only leading by how the Lord entreats him. If he follows after the faith of Christ (which is love, joy, peace, longsuffering, gentleness, goodness, faith, meekness, and temperance), then he will lead a successful land. If he follows after the world’s spirit (which is unrighteousness, fornication, idolatry, adultery, effeminate, abusive, thieves, covetous, drunks, revilers, and extortionists), then he will lead the land into evil every time."},{"kind":"para","text":"David is anointed king, and they are hoping that he will make the right change in the right places so that they would see prosperity and not have to try and anoint another person to take David's place. David's oath to these people here (willingly being kind to them because they showed kindness to a king who was not very nice) is something that speaks volumes about the character of David and the character that a child of God should have. Jabesh-gilead was also the first place in which Saul protected as soon as he's made king in 1 Samuel 11."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Judah wasn't just replacing their king quickly. They were obeying the command of the Lord quickly. They knew that David was next to be in charge. They knew that Saul would die, and it was Samuel that let it be known unto everyone around him. The day that Saul messed up in not killing the people (the people in which eventually kill him, mind you), Samuel told Saul that the kingdom would be taken from him quickly and abruptly. Surely, this is the evidence of that happening..."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":11,"title":"Israel Divided","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ner doesn't seem to like David and makes a very stupid mistake. All of Israel was to be joined together with their leadership, and Ner splits up the land and tribes, taking nearly all of the rest of them with him, placing Saul's son as the king of Israel. Judah did what God bid them. The rest of Israel did what Ner bid them. This will not be a good thing, as we will see in the future chapters."},{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, Ner (perhaps) knew that David was to be the king. He also knew that if he stopped David from being king (understanding that Saul did not want this either), then he could possibly gain the trust and power of the remaining tribes. He knew he had more than David does, and so he was thinking there was much more power in his numbers because of this, and it wouldn't take much to take the little that David has so that everyone can be on HIS page."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ishbosheth doesn't reign very long. Two years is still plenty of time to do some damage, but it seems something happens to Ishbosheth that requires him to stop reigning. Then there's a gap between David's reign over Judah and the end of Ishbosheth’s reign over Israel. Five years, it seems, that Israel spent without a leader. We will, of course, get more of this story as we read through the chapters, but I thought it was an important note to make here."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":17,"title":"A Battle of Peace","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don't think Ishbosheth (nor Abner for that matter) wanted any conflict with David. I don't think their goal was to become more powerful or to be more prosperous than the land of Judah. But when land is separated (like is being shown here), there is nothing that can prevent bitterness in the hearts of the people. You can look back to any civil war to see how they played it out, and why. This land was divided, and there was no way around their future conflict while it was in this shape."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"When I first read this, I'm going to be honest, I understood it as they were going to play some music, or they were going to just have a good time together. Abner takes twelve of his men, and Joab takes twelve of his men, and they meet on this field. Low-and-behold, they do not bring instruments with them. \"Play\" here means \"to make sport\" or \"to laugh mockingly.\" They were going to fight each other to the death. Their goal here was to settle a matter, and this was how they did it in those days. It seems a bit barbaric, honestly..."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Helkathhazzurim\" means \"field of swords.\" A lot of men were dying this day, and it seems it was a play on power. Israel had the numbers, that's for sure, but David had the tactics. He was not just some young man running from Saul anymore. He had experienced some things while he was out of Israel, and he learned how to lead in battle. These men were sacrificing themselves together to show they were bound to peace. We will soon see how that will play out for them..."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David proved that his tactics were improved. David has proved that over and over again since we first met him. Even with Goliath, David has shown and proven that God has a much bigger and better plan than the people. David learned a lot from the Lord about how to lead and how to have victory."}]}]},{"start":18,"end":23,"title":"Working a Treatise","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Asahel is Joab's brother. Joab is David's captain. Abner is Ishbosheth's captain (captain to Saul prior). This is important to remember because of what is to happen next..."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Asahel wanted the big prize. He wanted the honor of killing Abner himself. He wanted to take down Israel's strength."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abner is giving this young man a chance to turn away and fight someone else. Abner doesn't want to kill Asahel. Perhaps it's because he was making a very important treaty to Joab (Asahel's brother) through this battle. If Asahel dies today, Abner didn't want to be the one who did it because that would be disgraceful to Joab. Joab, likely, had a lot of confidence in Asahel's ability to battle, and probably didn't think much of it when sending him in. But I don't think he was prepared for Abner to be the one that would fight him..."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Everyone stopped at this moment. Abner didn't have much of a choice but to stop Asahel, and so he did. It would have been better if Asahel had gone after someone else. He was trying to stop Asahel from being killed, and he ended up killing him anyway. The moment this happens, the whole lot takes notice, and it is not a good thing..."}]}]},{"start":24,"end":29,"title":"Joab Pursues","entries":[{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joab and Abisahi were obviously upset. This was not the way things should go, and so Joab wants revenge against the one who killed his brother. This was supposed to be a simple battle to commemorate unity, and now it has turned into a civil war. The battle was to be fair, and now they are under the assumption that it was no longer fair. Losing someone that was respectable and important to them. Abner and his men gather together on a hill to say something..."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abner says, \"There is no point to this war. What happened has happened, and even if you take revenge on me, what difference will it make in the end? You will still feel bitterness towards me no matter what!\" Abner did try to warn Joab's brother, and he didn't listen. Abner felt sorry for having to kill him, but it was either kill or be killed."}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joab puts a stop to this battle at this moment because they needed a better plan than to just keep fighting over bitterness and hurt. This became a serious occasion quite quickly..."}]}]},{"start":30,"end":32,"title":"Asahel's Funeral","entries":[{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Twenty men were killed that day. Asahel was the most prominent one because he was the brother of David's right-hand man in battle. It is also said that Asahel was David's nephew. In verse 18, it says that these brothers are \"Zeruiah's\" sons."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Jesse begat his firstborn Eliab, and Abinadab the second, and Shimma the third, Nethaneel the fourth, Raddai the fifth, Ozem the sixth, David the seventh: Whose sisters were Zeruiah, and Abigail. And the sons of Zeruiah; Abishai, and Joab, and Asahel, three.","ref":"1 Chronicles 2:13-16"},{"kind":"para","text":"This was a terrible loss for sure. And it's one that keeps this battle waging..."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David lost twenty men, but Ishbosheth lost three hundred and thirty men! That was quite the defeat for the Israelites. They had numbers, but David had skill."}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They had a funeral and a long journey back to their home. What a sad ending to this chapter. There's a lot of sadness that will come next because of the division of the people in Israel. There's always sadness when the house is divided."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And if a house be divided against itself, that house cannot stand.","ref":"Mark 3:25"},{"kind":"para","text":"A lot of churches today fail because they are so divided. Some churches limp along with division, and they eventually heal with a limp. They gain a great number of dividers in their congregation and develop an unchristlike culture of hate towards this world. It just creates bitterness and war against their own people. They had just lost their king to people who are so blatantly against their God, and now they are losing numbers to each other. What a sad and terrible story for God's chosen people…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":29},{"start":30,"end":32}]},
    "3": {"chapter":3,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 03"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: Asahel, In our last chapter we got a look at a divided Israel. The major portion belonging to Saul's son Ishbosheth, who was set as king by Abner (Saul's right hand), and the minor (Hebron specifically) belonging to David who was accepted as king nearly instantly after Saul's death. There came a dual between Saul's men and David's men as a means of display in power, and David was having quite a victory until Abner was left with no choice but to kill Asahel (Joab's brother) when Asahel wouldn't leave Abner alone. He was warned several times to pursue after someone else because Abner didn't want to kill this young man, but he wouldn't let up. When this happened, Joab (David's seemingly right hand) becomes enraged and pursues after Abner to kill him. The battle gets extremely heated and a war breaks out because of it. This little duel turns into an all out civil unrest for the nation of Israel for quite a long time."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now we come to this chapter and we see the progression of this civil war, the betrayal of Abner, and the poor decisions of a king as well as his men. This chapter is packed with information, and on the surface it seems kind of unnecessary to us who are reading it, but it's how David comes to start gaining control over Israel, slowly but surely."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":1,"title":"Saul Vs. David","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This has been the theme since David and Saul first met. Saul's house continually tried to destroy David's house, but God continually lifted up David and his household. Why? Did God really hate Saul that much? Why didn't God just change the heart of Saul and the people that followed him? I believe God tried, but He doesn't have a track record of forcing people to turn to Him. He gives us the choice, and we always choose wrong. David chooses right, and God knows that David will lead through the Lord (as was promised by Moses in Deuteronomy 17). The king of Israel was not one that establishes himself. The king of Israel would be one that GOD chooses for it. Saul's house could never win this battle, and it shouldn't have begun initially anyway."}]}]},{"start":2,"end":5,"title":"David's Sin","entries":[{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David seemed to find some time during this war to have children. Worst yet, he seems to have children with more than one woman, and while it seems to just be glossed over here, I assure you it was not something that God approved of, and we will see that come to fruition in David's life later on. God still prospered David's household even though David was doing some stupid things here with his flesh."},{"kind":"para","text":"One particular child to note is Absalom \"son of Maacah of Geshur...\" His name might already be familiar, but it will be him who leads a rebellion against his own father later down the line. David takes special interest in Absalom because he is the one that David wants to follow him as king next. Of course, that doesn't happen, but that's a story for a later time. It suffices to say that what David was doing here would serve to be a very tragic moment in David's life later on, and God makes sure that David realizes his sin."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":11,"title":"Abner The Double-Crosser","entries":[{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abner was probably a strong force for the army of Saul, and he likely wasn't someone that people wanted to mess with. It was Abner that got Saul's son onto the throne to begin with, and that is an issue in and of itself. But a new issue arises for Abner..."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So, supposedly in this time and era, it was common for the right hand of the king to be with the concubines after the king is deceased. It seems Ishbosheth rejected this from Abner, and instead of Abner being content with the decision, he gets upset with the king. Instead of being rational, he starts to play over into David's victories, double-crossing his own king..."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"At least Abner wasn't quiet about what he was going to do. He told the king outright that he was going to abandon him and help David with overthrowing Ishbosheth. Abner sets this young man up as king, and then tries to destroy his kingship because he can't have his way. What a disgraceful act someone can do, even if it's on the wrong side of history."},{"kind":"para","text":"I've seen this in churches, honestly. There are churches that fight against each other today. They have power in high places in the church house, and the leaders are at war with one another. One church grows more than the other, and it seems as if God is working mightily in one side’s favor over the other. To be honest, I don't know if God is working much at all in a situation like that. He knows what is taking place, but He allows it to play out how it should, correcting things here and there to bring an ultimate purpose in the end. In these churches I've seen people trade sides because they can't get their way. I've even seen leaders completely outcast and rejected because the main leadership is upset and hurting. This is not an easy war to be had, and it's even more sad when you think about the fact that they are fighting against their own people..."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":16,"title":"Abner Joins With David","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So Abner wants to join forces with David, and David wants to join forces with Abner. But there was a condition. That condition was, David had to see his wife Michal again. Of course, I don't know if they were still married at this point. It seems likely not, seeing as Saul was the one that took her away from him to begin with. Why does he want to see her? Possibly to relive old flames? I don't know, but I imagine that he wasn't thinking so much with his faith as he was more with his flesh..."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is such a sad scene. Perhaps Michal had moved on at this point. She has a new husband now, and it seems they had a good relationship. There must have been a fight over his wife being taken, and he couldn't win. The leaders of his own people were rejecting his proclamation. They were married, \"what God has joined together...\" and such. Yet, here is a man, putting this marriage asunder, and the husband is following behind crying over this whole situation."},{"kind":"para","text":"I imagine I would be in tears as well. David doesn't really care about Michal. David, at this moment, only cares about David. Perhaps David didn't even know that Michal was married. Maybe her husband was shewed away before David could find out. Michal, no doubt, said something about it to David. David doesn't care though... This fleshly living will get him into more trouble."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":21,"title":"The People Join David","entries":[{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Abner effectively convinces the tribes to switch over and join the side of David. Of course, David was happy about this. David, however, should be very wary of trusting Abner. If Abner can switch sides that easily, over something so small and ridiculous, it wouldn't take much for him to do it again against David. He seemed to have a lot of power and sway over the people, and the people seemed to regard him as a figure who could be trusted."}]}]},{"start":22,"end":26,"title":"Abner Gone With Peace","entries":[{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I'm under the impression that Abner got what he wanted, and so David left him to lead a peaceful life somewhere of his own choosing. David, perhaps, didn't want Abner around just in case he planned on overthrowing or stabbing David in the back."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So Joab doesn't like Abner, and doesn't trust Abner, and it makes sense as to why. He is under the impression that Abner was there to try and trick David, but honestly Abner was there to try and destroy Ishbosheth’s rulership. So Joab sends out messengers to bring Abner back, but David doesn't know anything about this particular plan of Joab."}]}]},{"start":27,"end":30,"title":"Joab Murders Abner","entries":[{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This was not just an attack on Abner simply because Joab didn't trust him. It was revenge. This was planned for quite some time, I'd imagine. Joab held a grudge against Abner for a while."}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David made it clear when it was found out that his hands are clean of this issue. The one that should suffer from this wrongdoing would be Joab and his household. David thinks this was a bad call from Joab, and it wasn't fair for him to overthrow David's authority when David allowed Abner peace from his position..."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems Joab wasn't alone in this pursuit either. Either Abishai was there, or Abishai was in agreement. Either way, these brothers were together getting even for their brothers’ murder by murdering the murderer. Listen, I can hold grudges, but I also know that I cannot retaliate against someone who has greatly sinned against me. Why? Because I'm a pushover? No. Because the Lord wants to enact vengeance on my behalf."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Dearly beloved, avenge not yourselves, but rather give place unto wrath: for it is written, Vengeance is mine; I will repay, saith the Lord.","ref":"Romans 12:19"},{"kind":"para","text":"If I rob the Lord of His vengeance, and I take it into my own hands, I will receive the consequences for my actions. If I allow the Lord to do it, He bears the consequences. He can take the consequences much better than I can."}]}]},{"start":31,"end":39,"title":"David Mourns","entries":[{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This seems quite strange. I don't know what David's relationship was with Abner, but it didn't seem like Abner much liked David to begin with, and even after he switched to David's side, he was only doing it for personal pleasure... Did David really care about Abner that much? Hebron was a city of refuge where a murderer would be safe (according to the commands of Moses). Joab couldn't touch him in that city. Joab took him out of the city gate and killed him there, knowing that he was not able to be touched in the city. Abner was a fool for leaving the city to die. Perhaps that is what David meant when he said he \"died as a fool dieth.\""}]},{"start":36,"end":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David was really distraught over this death. This didn't sit well with him. It might be that he had respect for Abner for sticking by the side of Saul all this time. Perhaps, he believed Abner came to his senses in the end and thought David's rightful place was over Israel. Maybe David just really respected Abner because he was an older man, or maybe David got to know him quite a bit when David was fighting for Saul. I honestly don't know... Perhaps it was because Abner stepped outside of safety and received death due to it. It is the great image of us today. Christ is our city of refuge. Perhaps this is where Psalm 91 stems from..."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I will say of the LORD, He is my refuge and my fortress: my God; in him will I trust.","ref":"Psalm 91:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"The death of soldiers is not a very welcoming reality, but less welcoming is the death of faithful men..."}]},{"start":39,"end":39,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The people honored this act of the king. They honored the king. They respected David's compassion and vision, and he was giving them a reason to love him more. He had immense care for his people, and for those that were well respected and in high positions. Say what you might about Abner (and surely there is plenty to say), he was a faithful man, and he never did anything behind the backs of anyone. If he was going to do something (including switching sides of the war), he let it be known. He was not quiet or secret about his decisions, even killing Joab's brother. In fact, he warned him over and over again that he didn't want to kill him and to turn aside and go after someone else. Joab doesn't care about that though, and he let his own flesh and hate get in the way of serving properly..."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is a lesson for us all. We should have more compassion (like David) for those that are over us, and less bitterness (like Joab) because we disagree with something they might have done. Of course, we should give place for the Lord to anoint and dictate, but we should be cautious about jumping the gun too quickly on someone we know nearly nothing about…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":30},{"start":31,"end":39}]},
    "4": {"chapter":4,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 04"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: There was quite a lot happening in our last chapter as we saw David and Ishbosheth continue their battle against one another. Of course, I don't think there is any particular ill will between David and Ishbosheth directly, but a kingdom cannot be divided against itself because it won't stand."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And if a house be divided against itself, that house cannot stand.","ref":"Mark 3:25"},{"kind":"para","text":"There is a lot of casualities in war, but when there are people who are quite high up becoming a casualty, it is not a good feeling on either side (unless that man was truly heinous and disgusting; I don't know how many tears were shed for someone like Hitler for instance). This chapter continues with more casualties of war, and it doesn't really end on a great note..."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":3,"title":"Two Captains","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, Ishbosheth would not take kindly to this news. Even though Abner switched sides in the middle of battle (which was a treacherous thing to do anyway), Ishbosheth still cared for Abner, if only for the sake that Saul took extreme care of him. I'm sure Ishbosheth didn't just feel terrible because Abner died, but because he felt he had let down his deceased father. He felt he had lost a bit of honor, and it seemed that all of Israel felt the king’s pain in this area."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Baanah\" means \"in affliction.\" My assumption is that he was born during the time that the Beerothites were “sojourners,\" and so his name was derived from being born during a time of affliction. “Sojourner\" means \"to seek hospitality with.\" They had to split from the tribe of Benjamin for a time for some particular reason that isn't mentioned here. My assumption is that they had to leave due to something Saul had done and were not willing to stay around to see more of his crazy antics come to term. \"Rechab\" means \"rider.\" That isn't useful to anything at the moment, but I figured I would define it, seeing as I've been trying to keep up with that for a while now."}]}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"title":"Jonathan's Son","entries":[{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Mephibosheth\" means \"exterminating the idol.\" I find it fascinating that Jonathan had a son, and it wasn't talked about more beforehand. We get a small glimpse of this fact here, but nothing else is much said of him until later on. David must not have known about this young man, and so he assumed that the rest of Saul's house might have been gone for good."},{"kind":"para","text":"What a name, by the way. It doesn't really roll off the tongue, but it certainly paints a picture. That is, perhaps, his greatest testimony. This lame child of Jonathan will eventually come into the king’s presence, and he will \"exterminate the idol.\" He will give a new thought to the lineage of Saul. More about that later though..."}]}]},{"start":5,"end":12,"title":"Cursing Yourself","entries":[{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Rimmon\" means \"Pomegranate.\" Again, not very helpful, but it might help draw to why they left Benjamin to begin with if you want to dive into his name more appropriately. There is, however, some pretty important significance to a pomegranate and to Christ, but that's best left for the book of Solomon."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Thy lips are like a thread of scarlet, and thy speech is comely: thy temples are like a piece of a pomegranate within thy locks.","ref":"Song of Solomon 4:3"},{"kind":"para","text":"But we get the picture here of the new captains of Ishbosheth coming before their king while he's laying down and relaxing."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They came in as if they were just doing their job, and they attacked their king and killed him right on the spot. It seems they owed a lot of respect towards Abner. It is said that they were officers under the command of Abner in the army of Saul. They believed that David was meant to be on the throne, and judging by Ishbosheth's lack of provision and unity, they took the job into their own hands and killed their king."},{"kind":"para","text":"This was not okay. We know this is not okay for a few reasons. One of them was the life of David. David had plenty of opportunities to kill Saul for the way he was acting, but he never hurt him one time. In fact, over and over again we see David protect Saul from being killed until the last battle with the Philistines (in that battle he had no choice but to walk away). David is not an idiot, and when this news comes before him, he won't be happy about it..."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They disgraced the name of Saul further by doing this. Killing their king is one thing, but beheading him and taking the head to David was a truly heinous act. I don't really understand how they could have thought this was a good idea. What leader could ever trust the people that just killed their own king and beheaded him in such a cruel way? They literally tricked their leader and killed him while he was defenseless. This also goes against their leader Abner, who was upfront with his plan of action. He never went behind the king’s back about it..."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David starts this conversation off with, \"I can only make wise choices in this matter because the Lord has guided me through them.\" The truth is, when David made many decisions, he would pray and wait for the answer of the Lord. He was smart to do so; otherwise, he would have made some grave mistakes any other way. So, even in this same instance, he takes the advice of the Lord on the matter. He has already been through a situation like this at the beginning of 2 Samuel when someone came to him and told him that he killed Saul. Instead of rewarding this man, he kills this man because Saul was David's king. Whether David liked it or not, Saul was still the leader of Israel and was still anointed as king by God. It could not be by the hands of David that the king was overthrown; it would have to be God that sets up the leader."},{"kind":"para","text":"That's a good reminder for all of us today. We cannot force our vision or role in leadership. I've seen pastors and teachers do that a lot, and it starts off a great and striving ministry only to fall later because of the leader’s pride and grip on THEIR ministry. Someone once said, \"A staff-led church is a staff-ruined church.\" That really walks hand in hand with anything. A leader-led class is a leader-ruined class. A king-led state is a king-ruined state. A husband-led house is a husband-ruined house. We must rely upon the Lord to make the right decisions and calls for things that truly matter to us. If we take the matters into our own hands, we will ruin it for ourselves and for everyone else that is involved."},{"kind":"para","text":"David did the right thing in honoring the king. He might not have agreed with Israel's choice to place Ishobosheth as the king, but it was not up to these men to force new leadership either. They attacked and killed their OWN king. What a disgrace to the name of Israel as well as to the Lord they were serving. David knew exactly what to do in this situation, and he does not hesitate to do it again..."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These two men would be on display as traitors. Ishbosheth's head was not a trophy for David. He buried the head quickly and discreetly. If David had had the chance to clean up the remains of Ishbosheth, he would have done that as well before speaking to the people about it. He, however, wasn't in a position to do this, and so the people would have to find out in another way…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":12}]},
    "5": {"chapter":5,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 05"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: The last few chapters has been anything but easy or pleasing. There has been a lot of death. Abner was the most recent, and it throws the king of Israel for a loop when he found out. We did get a small glimpse of light and hope (which will be better touched upon later in this book) that Jonathan had a son, but he was a lame child because he fell while trying to run (perhaps from Israel's enemies the Philistines). David doesn't know about this child yet. Then Ishbosheth's men go and kill the king of Israel (Ishbosheth) while he's sleeping. They take the kings head and present it to David, and David kills these men for what they had done (they went against their own king)."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, David is made king over Israel, and we see him start to establish his place before them. He then faces the Philistines and some battles commence out of it."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":3,"title":"David Made King","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The war was meaningless and useless, and now with the loss of their king, they had no choice but to find a leader, and David was the rightful leader all along. They were coming to him to pay tribute to the fact that they were wrong to have ever considered someone else their leader when it was painfully obvious through Samuel that David was supposed to be the one. Everyone knew it, they just didn't want to accept it. David didn't push it either; he was actually humble about the whole matter. He didn't like the tragedies of this war. Too many of his own kind had to die and sacrifice themselves over something so little and meaningless, only to realize that there could have been a better solution all along (this being one of them)."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Further proof that they knew they were supposed to place David as king. They saw him, over and over again, protect these people and help these people and serve these people, and yet they didn't want to trust the process that God obviously laid out for them. They were arrogant and prideful, and it's exactly the same for Christians today. We might not be searching for a king (we have found one in Christ), but we are constantly searching to put ourselves on top of the situation. We think we have everything under control when it's in our control to have it, and ultimately we mess it all up."},{"kind":"para","text":"We should enjoy the fact that God would use specific men to lead, and we should be encouraging to those that we see God doing a service to. Instead (more times than not), we fight against God's leading for OTHER PEOPLE and try and put them down because it means they might become bigger and better than who you want to be on top. That's not wise in any scenario."},{"kind":"para","text":"Let's take this more politically (we are talking about kings here after all). When we vote for a presidential candidate, we overlook the lesser ones on the ballot and head straight for the two candidates with the most ad money spent. We want the ones that have shown their faces the most. That (honestly) is a poor way to vote. What values do they have? Can they lead by example? Will they do what's right by their people? These are the real questions, and 90% of the time people don't vote for the right person because they don't think about what their leadership really looks like."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this situation with Israel, they didn't want David because he was not a face they saw very much. Actually, he was the face that their previous king hated more than anything. Most of the time, their king was gone on a pursuit to try and kill David, and the people of the land knew this. But they didn't think about what the Lord was doing through all of this, and the wiser choice being placed on trusting in David as their king."}]}]},{"start":4,"end":5,"title":"A Worthy King","entries":[{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Thirty years old is pretty young for a king. It's, of course, not the youngest, but it's still a pretty young age."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Seven years old was Jehoash when he began to reign.","ref":"2 Kings 11:21"},{"kind":"para","text":"I'm currently thirty-one (not to toot my own horn), but I couldn't imagine leading a whole nation at this age. Or how about a seven-year-old king? We would probably think that is cute and funny, but he does what is right before the Lord as a seven-year-old. He was a GOOD king! We have an eighty-year-old president who is terrible being shown up by a seven-year-old with less technology... just saying..."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So this war lasted quite some time. A seven-year war is nothing to wink at, and it certainly isn't something to just skim over. That's a lot of fighting, and it translates to a lot of deaths. It does matter quite a bit more that he reigned over them all for thirty-three years after that though, even though they were the ones that were killing his men. That goes to further prove that he didn't want the war to begin with."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":10,"title":"Zion Is Ruled","entries":[{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It's funny that they thought David would stay away from this place called Zion. It is David's favorite place, and a place he will hang around a lot. I believe he enjoyed it so much because it was the highest point in the city. He could see and control everything from that feat. Perhaps it was a bit closer to Heaven as well in the eyes of David."},{"kind":"para","text":"There will be a New Jerusalem that comes from Heaven someday, and that will be the highest vantage point we will all have. This is a great symbol of a future promise here, and I believe this is why David loved this city so much. Notice it says that he didn't just take over the whole city, but he took over the \"strong hold\" of Zion. His goal was to get on top of this hill, and he did it without much harm."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps David doesn't like the lame and blind here because they have made it difficult for him to come in (even though he is king over them). Maybe there is something else, I couldn't tell you, but the most important thing to highlight here is that David was building his city, and in so doing he grew great, and God was with him. Saul didn't have this same demeanor. If Saul grew great, it was only because Samuel was with him. David was searching, not to be the king and ruler of the nation, but to be the proper leader that God needs the nation to be."},{"kind":"para","text":"Say what you will about a \"Christian Nationalist,\" but we could all benefit from a man of God that desires to love God and serve his people in their leadership. Those that don't believe we can benefit from that kind of leadership must really hate love, joy, peace, patience, gentleness, goodness, faith, and meekness. I would appreciate a leader with these qualities, and they are impossible to find in anyone who doesn't know the Lord."}]}]},{"start":11,"end":12,"title":"David Feels Established","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seemed the moment that Tyre sends over workmen to build David a house in Zion, he understands that God is with him in this endeavor. I don't know if he didn't seek God first before entering this city, or if he just wasn't fully confident that God would bless him. Either way, he knows now that God is with him here."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For the LORD hath chosen Zion; he hath desired it for his habitation.","ref":"Psalm 132:13"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The LORD loveth the gates of Zion more than all the dwellings of Jacob.","ref":"Psalm 87:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"It's important to note, however, that David understands that God is not just providing for the sake of David here. David wants to be a good leader, of course, but he understands that the reason God is providing for him is so that he can provide for \"...his people Israel's sake.\" David was in this position to provide safety and direction for God's people, and it's a very serious thing to be in charge over God's people. Sadly, there are a lot of people who end up messing this up in the coming chapters, but it still even happens today."},{"kind":"para","text":"Many pastors, priests, and laity of the church house are not very serious about their leadership towards God's people. They treat them like dogs rather than like sheep... The leader must know whether or not the Lord is with him before he continues to lead. If he does not feel the presence of God leading him, he should step down from his leadership..."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":16,"title":"David's Flesh","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So he feels established and then brushes it all away with this great sin. This was not okay in the eyes of the Lord. God did not want this for any of His people and especially in His children's lives. This is penned here to point out the fact that David was a sinner just like you and me, and he made some terrible mistakes even while God was providing for him."},{"kind":"para","text":"It is what all of us fall for. We are all sinners in need of God's grace every day. We will always mess up, and we will always fall down, and God will always provide for your good, even if you don't think it's good. I truly feel as if God should not be providing for me all the time. I don't deserve His provision. I don't deserve to be alive today. Yet, He continues to provide above and beyond my needs and it fascinates me daily."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"As in previous tradition, we have been able to define the names given here. These are the children of David, and so it stands to reason that David would have been very specific about the names being chosen. \"Shammuah\" means \"He that is heard; or renowned.\" Perhaps the Lord heard David. Or perhaps David heard the baby. \"Shobab\" means \"rebellious.\" David was quite rebellious towards the Lord having these marriages. Perhaps the baby was also rebellious towards the Lord or towards David. \"Nathan\" means “giver.\" The Lord has provided for David and his wife. \"Solomon\" means \"peace.\" There are a lot of connections we could make about that name here, but I think it's best left for a book like Proverbs or Ecclesiastes. Mind you (and this will be important later), these were the sons given to David through Bathsheba. This wife has not been mentioned yet..."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Ibhar\" means \"Jehovah chooses.\" Or at least we should let Him choose. \"Elishua\" means \"My God is wealth\" or \"God is my salvation.\" \"Nepheg\" means \"sprout.\" Maybe David wasn't there for the naming of this child? \"Japhia\" means \"shining.\" These children's mothers are not mentioned. They are likely children of concubines."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Names with \"Eli\" in front of it likely relate to God in some way (as a rule of thumb). \"Elishama\" means \"My God has heard.” \"Eliada\" means \"God knows.” \"Eliphalet\" means \"God is deliverance.\" With these children, it seems David is telling a story about God's relationship with him. God hears you, God knows the situation, and God delivers you. What a great promise that is somewhat lost because these children aren't mentioned much."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are only two children who make their debut again quite prominently, and that is Nathan and Solomon. The other children will be mentioned but there's not much story to their lives. The first two mentioned in this list don't ever come up again as far as I know. That's either a good thing or a very tragic thing."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":21,"title":"A Quick Battle","entries":[{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"In case you don't remember, David was living in the land of the Philistines for quite a while. When Saul dies, David and his men move back into Israel without saying a word to the Philistines about it. They have been gone for quite some time. Maybe the Philistines assumed that David was planning a takeover. When they hear about David becoming king, they want to know what's really going on. David has the power and might to kill all these people (seeing as he was protected and taken in by the Philistines), and yet David hasn't done this. So David meets with the Philistines (ALL of them, it says) so that he can confront the problem head-on."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Philistines were in battle position. They were ready for a fight if things went south."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David assesses the situation and realizes that the Philistines might not be there just to talk, and before moving forward further, he prays to the Lord about the next move. The way that it's asked is almost equivalent to, \"Will I survive this, Lord, or is today the day you replace me on the throne?\""},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, I think this is a good way to view all of our situations. As a leader in ministry (or in your family), ask the Lord if things are right moving forward. Pray to God to give you the strength and direction you need to make the proper judgment calls, and give light to the idea that God could replace you at any moment. Usually, He won't replace you without warning. If God does decide to replace you (perhaps not in your family; you are not replaceable there, but in your ministry), you will need to pray that God finds a better fit, not for a bigger fall."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Baalperazim\" means \"lord of the breaks.\" It was a victory name."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems the Philistines brought their false gods with them, and they didn't stand a chance. David was wise and burned them instead of holding onto them or just leaving them there to rot. He didn't want anyone coming along and collecting them or using them as a means in the future to worship anything but the real God of the universe."}]}]},{"start":22,"end":25,"title":"A Second Battle","entries":[{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They just don't learn. David didn't even have to try the last time, and they think they could just come back and bully him over and over again. David could run back into battle and just destroy him like he did before, but he doesn't do that. Look what David does..."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David, wisely, prays to the Lord first. If David had been bold enough to just fight them the same way he did before (assuming God had already instructed him and he would just follow the same instructions), David would have been toast. God tells David to go around them and attack from the rear, rather than facing them head-on like he did before. This gives us more reason to pray before every decision."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God was there in the battle with David. It was not David going in alone. This is important for us to understand, because there are many of us who have our minds set on doing something very specific and just telling God what we plan on doing instead of asking God. Granted, I think there are times when God gives us something that is so obvious that we don't need to keep asking Him if it's okay to do. For instance, we probably don't have to ask God every time if we should kiss our wife. Or maybe we don't need to ask God every time if we need to take a shower. Common sense would explain that these things are necessary."},{"kind":"para","text":"I can't stand it when preachers say that \"If the Bible doesn't talk about it, it isn't important.\" That's not true. Cars are important in our day. Showers are important in our day. Lighting (electricity as a whole) is quite important in our day. The Bible doesn't talk about these specific things, but common sense would tell us what we should and shouldn't be doing. There are things where we must come to the Lord and ask God, \"What would you have me to do?\" However, I think there are a lot of Christians who come before God and say, \"God, I am going to do this, and if you are there then give me a victory. If you are not, tear it apart.\" I'm not putting this method of praying down (if it's an urgent matter), but there should be more times where we come before God for direction, rather than for blessing."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sometimes those prayers will take a while to be answered. Sometimes they will take years to ever come to fruition. We must just pray, \"Lord, give me direction here. I will not move until you make it clear.\" And God will make it clear, eventually, in a way that you didn't even think was feasible."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":25}]},
    "6": {"chapter":6,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 06"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, the Israelites finally make David their king, and he rules with a great head on his shoulders. He goes to Zion and takes over, committing some serious sins while he's growing in prosperity (something we will see get in his way over and over again as we continue to follow his journey), and it ends with David protecting his people multiple times from the Philistines who have now learned that David has left their territory and joined their enemy."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, David sets out on a noble task to retrieve the ark of the Lord and bring it to his city to have it close by and to use as a method of worship towards his Lord. He knows that having the ark is a good thing, but he doesn't know exactly how he should do it."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":5,"title":"David Wants the Ark","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David gathers his men for a very triumphant cause. David does a lot of really great and interesting things for the people of Israel, and one thing that he was lacking (at this time and in his land) was the ark that belonged to Israel. David wanted God's presence in the land, and what better way to display that at this time than to have God's ark in their midst?"}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So this is a great idea. However, it is done in a very poor manner. There were specific instructions that were made by Moses about how the ark was to be moved."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But unto the sons of Kohath he gave none: because the service of the sanctuary belonging unto them was that they should bear upon their shoulders.","ref":"Numbers 7:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"What David is doing here is extremely dangerous, and that will be made evident here shortly. This ark was not being carried by the Levites, but rather by cart, and by the sons of Abinadab of Gibeah. It would insinuate that these men were probably Benjaminites, rather than Levites. God's covenant promise is not a thing to play with. It should be a very important responsibility on the shoulders of Christians to take God's covenant gift seriously."},{"kind":"para","text":"We cannot play around with the gospel message when we have been given such amazing truths to pour out on this world. If we are nonchalant with the covenant truth of Christ's salvation, we should not be surprised if we end up cursed or worse... There are many false preachers today, sadly, and they have neglected their duty as followers of Christ to give the message of the gospel with reverence and love. They will have their judgement before God one day. May that not be said of us..."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David’s way of worshipping was a little different. He loved music, and he loved to use his talents to glorify the Lord. I think that is a very fine way of glorifying God. I can't think of any better way to use your talents than to bear it all to God. This was not necessary to do, but it was something that David wanted to present, because taking this ark where it was supposed to go was a very big deal."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":11,"title":"Uzzah Dies","entries":[{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That escalated quickly. You might think that this is a bit extreme, and to us it might seem to be that way. But they were not carrying the ark the right way right off the bat. They were trying to move it without following the customs of the Lord, and perhaps they would have done it decently if the cart didn't start rocking, chancing the breaking or tipping of the ark. If the ark fell off the cart, what would they do to put it back on?"},{"kind":"para","text":"Maybe if it fell off, David would have thought much more carefully about who it is that is supposed to be moving it? Either way, Uzzah tries to steady the ark and that was not okay to the Lord. This is a very sacred and holy covenant they were touching, and Uzzah's hands were certainly not holy enough for this particular cause. Let's see what David thinks of it..."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Perezuzzah\" means \"Breach of Uzza.\" That place will be made a memorial to the memory of Uzzah. We will never visit this place again in Scripture, but it's a nice note to have because of the events that take place here. This circumstance really upsets David. He is distraught by what just happened. It wasn't as if David was angry with Uzzah for touching the ark. He was angry at the Lord for killing Uzzah so easily."},{"kind":"para","text":"David should have known better than to have Uzzah controlling the ark anyway, and Uzzah should have known what to do as well. The law of the Lord was very precise on the matter, and God does warn them continuously throughout the Scriptures (the law that they would have/should have studied every day)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And thou shalt teach them diligently unto thy children, and shalt talk of them when thou sittest in thine house, and when thou walkest by the way, and when thou liest down, and when thou risest up.","ref":"Deuteronomy 6:7"},{"kind":"para","text":"He was either never taught this (highly unlikely) or he never actually took it seriously."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So David stops the trek of carrying this ark because he does not want anyone else to make any other mistake like this. He knew that the Lord was righteous and always right, but this actually scared David. \"Afraid\" means \"terrify.\" We are told several times throughout scripture to \"Fear\" God, and out of all those times, it usually insinuates that we should be giving God “reverence and awe,\" not to be \"terrified\" of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this instance, David was actually afraid of the Lord. He was scared that God would do more than just kill Uzzah, and so he takes this ark and leaves it in a nearby home (the home of Obededom). I don't know what Obededom was thinking when the ark was brought in there, but when the king enters your home and leaves God's ark with you, you don't question it, and you don't reject it. Perhaps he was told what had just happened (that wouldn't make him feel any better about having this in his home). He thought, \"As long as I don't touch it, it should be fine...\" Yet it becomes more than fine..."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Three months of having this \"terrifying\" box, and Obededom was having a lot of blessings all of a sudden. Perhaps his crops grew better (or in more abundance)? Maybe he felt a lot more energy and peace? Maybe everyone around him just seemed to be in a better mood? The Lord's presence will certainly do that to a man that follows Him."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are plenty of grumpy pastors and preachers today, don't get me wrong. I won't deny that they have the Lord in their lives, but they are certainly lacking in the power of the Holy Ghost and it shows by their attitude. Rather than taking a harsh trial with patience and learning to flush it out with joy, they feel burdened and encumbered and they look angry and grumpy. I won't say there aren't times for that, but I will say that if you feel this way more than you feel joyous in the Lord, you might be lacking in the presence of God, and it's a good idea to find that presence (no matter how difficult or how long it might take) than it is for you to force yourself forward without Him. Either way, this man (Obededom) was having the time of his life with the ark in his presence. Possibly the best three months he's ever had."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":15,"title":"The Ark Arrives","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So now David feels confident, once again, to bring the ark the rest of the way because he hears that someone is being blessed by it. Again, David didn't understand that there was a proper way to bring this ark, and so I think he was a bit confused as to why it was happening this way. David should have been studied enough to know (and it's possible that in the back of his mind somewhere the knowledge was lurking). Maybe he has had time to study God's Word to figure out where he went wrong. We see he does it properly in the next verses."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is kind of funny to me. Every six steps, he has a sacrifice to make sure that this ark gets to where it needs to go without any issues. There had to be quite a lot of animals killed this day as they were moving the ark. It certainly seemed to take a village, but he was doing things right this time, and he was going to make sure there were no mistakes made and no other deaths. He was going to get the ark to Zion whether everyone liked it or not!"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is how we know it was the priests that were bearing the ark this time, because David didn't have the right to sacrifice animals without the presence of the priests doing it. We learned that lesson with Saul when he started sacrificing animals before Samuel got there and put a stop to it."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Saul said, Bring hither a burnt offering to me, and peace offerings. And he offered the burnt offering. And it came to pass, that as soon as he had made an end of offering the burnt offering, behold, Samuel came; and Saul went out to meet him, that he might salute him. And Samuel said, What hast thou done? And Saul said, Because I saw that the people were scattered from me, and that thou camest not within the days appointed, and that the Philistines gathered themselves together at Michmash; Therefore said I, The Philistines will come down now upon me to Gilgal, and I have not made supplication unto the LORD: I forced myself therefore, and offered a burnt offering. And Samuel said to Saul, Thou hast done foolishly: thou hast not kept the commandment of the LORD thy God, which he commanded thee: for now would the LORD have established thy kingdom upon Israel for ever.","ref":"1 Samuel 13:9-13"},{"kind":"para","text":"If that wasn't enough, Saul does it again later on even though Samuel called him a fool for doing it before!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Saul said, Disperse yourselves among the people, and say unto them, Bring me hither every man his ox, and every man his sheep, and slay them here, and eat; and sin not against the LORD in eating with the blood. And all the people brought every man his ox with him that night, and slew them there. And Saul built an altar unto the LORD: the same was the first altar that he built unto the LORD.","ref":"1 Samuel 14:34-35"},{"kind":"para","text":"So David is moving the ark in the right progression with the right people, and that is how he is able to get it into his city. Ultimately, he wants it there to bless the Israelites, and that is certainly what God wants to do."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don't exactly know what \"dancing with all his might\" might have looked like, but we can assume that it was quite intense. I can picture it in a sense. The weird part, though, is not the dancing. The weird part is the linen ephod he was wearing. There are plenty of people who attest to the idea that David had nothing else on but this piece of priestly cloth covering only the parts that shouldn't be shown. I don't know how accurate that idea is, but they get this idea because of how Michal reacts to his performance in a moment."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Everyone was out on the streets excited about the arrival of this ark, and it was evident that David was extremely happy to have it finally arrive (especially after what had happened a few months back)."}]}]},{"start":16,"end":19,"title":"Michal Is Disgusted","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is where people get the idea that David was likely not very well kept in his manner of dancing, but I don't think that to be the case here. I believe that David was wearing more than the ephod (a priestly garment). I believe the ephod is mentioned simply because he doesn't usually wear something like that. Honestly, he might have looked a little better with the ephod on."},{"kind":"para","text":"The important thing to note here is Michal’s (David's first wife) disgust in David because of how seriously in love he was with God. She didn't like the idea that David was giving this much praise to God. She might not have made it known openly that she was not thrilled about David's performance, but she certainly harbored hatred in her heart."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, we must remember that God does not really have a place to dwell at this time. Rather, the ark has no home. The Lord is always in Heaven, but for the people to come in before Him, He has a tent. I could argue that it is Solomon that creates the first \"church\" as we would refer to it now, but the purpose of the temple that Solomon builds is to depict a future understanding of how a person dwells with the Lord. The tabernacle was a place for people to go to be with God. The church is a place for God to dwell with His people."},{"kind":"para","text":"The church is not a building, by the way. They had to go to this place to be with God, and that was their place to offer their sacrifices and express their worship. Today, the church is everywhere a group of believers meet (even if it's in a home or underground). We are called (as the church) not just to be in God's house, but to BE God's house."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"What? know ye not that your body is the temple of the Holy Ghost which is in you, which ye have of God, and ye are not your own?","ref":"1 Corinthians 6:19"}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This was a double blessing. The people were blessed to have the ark of the Lord in their midst, but they were further blessed by the offerings of kindness from their king. This is how God's leaders should be displayed to his people. When they are so excited to have the Lord present with them, they will show that outwardly by giving to others around them. It may not be bread, meat, and wine, but it will certainly be a kind-hearted gesture out of a mode of compassion given only from the presence of the Lord. Perhaps David had always wanted to give to his people in this way, but there was really no reason to do so without the Lord's presence giving him the unction."}]}]},{"start":20,"end":23,"title":"A Proper Worship","entries":[{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I know what you are thinking, \"But Jonathan, you said that David was wearing other clothes as well.\" He did! What she is saying here is more on the premise of him not acting very kingly. He was acting anything BUT professional. It's like a grown man who is the president of a country dancing around and being silly in the presence of all the people, and the people are happy about it and egging him on. Today, if we saw our president do this, people would be flipping out. The Democrats would love it (our president currently is Joe Biden who is the candidate for the Democratic party) and the Republicans would be in the shoes of Michal mocking him. However, if our president (no matter his political affiliation) was dancing around the spirit of the Lord like this, I would appreciate him much more. He would earn my respect. Not Michal's though. Not those that don't see the value that the Lord brings to the people that are willing to be influenced by Him. Perhaps she thought that worship to the Lord was to look very different than the way that David was doing it. David was playing music and dancing before the Lord, and it wasn't very \"kingly\" of him."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sometimes (and I admit I am guilty of this as well), we make the mistake in thinking that we have to fit into an idea of church worship and limit ourselves off from true worship. We can't sing this song, or wave this hand, or sway this way, or preach this verse, or pray this prayer, because it doesn't fit the \"Baptist\" conduct, or the \"Catholic\" conduct, or the \"Methodist\" conduct (if they have one?) But God doesn't want a tradition of worship. He wants a HEART of worship."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"God is a Spirit: and they that worship him must worship him in spirit and in truth.","ref":"John 4:24"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Sing unto the LORD; for he hath done excellent things: this is known in all the earth.","ref":"Isaiah 12:5"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Speaking to yourselves in psalms and hymns and spiritual songs, singing and making melody in your heart to the Lord;","ref":"Ephesians 5:19"},{"kind":"para","text":"Our mode of worship today is much like Michal’s, and over and over again throughout Scripture, the Lord rebukes such worship for Him. He rejects it and turns away. He wants your heart more than your actions. Don't get it mistaken. He wants your actions too. But action without heart is not really worship."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That's really what you should be asking yourself when it comes to worship. What are we doing it for? Your method of worship will be very different from my method, but our worship should be in unity towards one Lord. I might enjoy (for example) worshipping with my hands by my side. You might like worshipping with your hands in the air. Just because our methods of worship are different doesn't draw from our heart for it."},{"kind":"para","text":"What David was doing was not provocative, and it was not sinful. His method of worship was holy before the Lord, and he was willing to \"dance with all his might\" to prove it. What really frustrates me is those people who complain about your method of worship are usually people who don't do much worshipping themselves..."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord leaned in on David's method of worship over Michal's method. David will be blessed; Michal will not be. I heard about a service that D.L. Moody gave about going to reach the lost with the gospel. One particular member of the congregation raised her voice and said, \"I don't like the way you share the gospel with others. I find it to be rude and intrusive.\" \"You're right.\" he said, \"Quite frankly, I don't like the way I do it either. How do you do it?\" \"I don't.\" She said. He responded with, \"Well I like my way of doing it much better than your way of not doing it.\" That is the best method of ministry moving forward. Don't worry about how everyone else wants to do it, dance to the Lord with all your might (or whatever gift you might have), and give Him your best method of worship, even if everyone else is scoffing at you for not fitting into their idea or \"bubble\" of worship."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":23}]},
    "7": {"chapter":7,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 07"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, things got a bit hectic as David tries to bring the Ark of God back to his dwelling. He wants the ark in his presence and so he tries to retrieve it with difficulty. Someone ends up dying, and they have to leave it for a time, and then come back for it later on being careful again. We ended the chapter with Michal (David's first wife) being disgusted in David's delivery in the ark, and it causes strife and turmoil between the two, and it made Michal childless..."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, David has the unction to build the Lord's house, and he desires to give a permanent dwelling place to place the ark and for the people to come and worship the Lord. Something stops him, however, and he has to wait for the Lord to give a promise."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":3,"title":"Desires to Build God's House","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems David was constantly thinking on the Lord during this time. He was concerned about the Lord's dwelling. He had been given so much, and the Lord had been treated with so little for so long. David wanted the Lord to have a proper place of worship and dwelling, and I find that to be an extremely noble cause. He went to Nathan (the prophet of the Lord at this juncture) to see if there was something that could be done, and Nathan responded..."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Nathan seemed to find this act wholesome as well and gives the green light to David. He lets it be known that the Lord will be with him as well when he goes on this venture to build God a dwelling place. There's a lot of great symbolism here."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are men of God today who are called and compassionate about building a house for the Lord, and the Lord allows it, and it takes them years to build it, but the longer they stay committed to it, the more blessed the people are. Then there are people who have a desire to build the Lord's house, but only for selfish gain. They want to be blessed, sure, but they want it by their own means. This is not the proper way to build the Lord's house."},{"kind":"para","text":"David was already blessed, and so he wasn't looking to build the house of the Lord to gain blessings. Rather, he wanted to build the Lord's house to bless the Lord. May we seek the same mindset when building the Lord's house."}]}]},{"start":4,"end":17,"title":"God Stops the Building","entries":[{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Let it be clear, it is not as if God doesn't WANT a house to dwell in. Obviously, the Lord has a home in Heaven, above everyone and everything, and so this house is something very small to Him (for He dwells in eternity and not on Earth), but He shows adoration towards this cause. He seems to be pleased with the idea that David thought on Him, especially because no one else thought on this idea before (of course, they were traveling a lot in the wilderness, so they couldn't give God a permanent dwelling until they came into the promised land)."},{"kind":"para","text":"God wants a permanent dwelling with mankind. That is what He will have in eternity, and that is what this world is gearing towards in the end. But David... David wasn't the man for the job... Nathan jumped the gun, it seems, by insinuating that the king should do what he wanted to without the permission of the Lord first. Now Nathan has to pick up the pieces..."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God has taken care of His people, and He has certainly taken care of David. He has a plan for His house in due time, but for right now things need to be established with the Israelites who were not where they were supposed to be yet. They haven't gone the distance for the Lord, and so there was still much to be done before a permanent dwelling could be established."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"A very interesting note to make here about God's \"Will\" for the Israelites. God says, \"I will appoint a place for my people\" and \"I will plant them\" and \"They may dwell in a place of their own and move no more...\" yet, that still hasn't happened. This is a promise that God will make good on, and we know that it will come to pass as He has promised."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That desire is not lost on the Lord, but God has to establish David's house first before anything can be done for the Lord. He will not let this go unnoticed, however. It is interesting that God rejects this blessing from David. Of course, the rejection is mostly in the fact that David's hands were drenched in violence, but the Lord could easily turn all of that around. The Lord wants to be a blessing more than be blessed, however, and that is proven over and over again throughout Scripture. The problem is, His children often reject the blessings of the Lord, and end up in their own suffering and pain because of it. It is a lesson to be learned, though. We should desire to be a blessing more than to be blessed."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I find it interesting that the child in which God is talking about here will be a child gotten through a very wicked time in David's life. While, right now, David is looking forward to the promises of God, and has Him centered in his view, later on David's flesh will get the best of him, and he will do something extremely stupid. Out of it, however, the Lord provides for this promise, and uses David's sin to show God's mercy. But it drives even deeper than that."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is about the kingdom of Christ. His kingdom will be established forever. It is obviously not Solomon's kingdom (spoiler alert) for he dies, and his kingdom with him. Christ's kingdom is eternal, and that is the one in which God is dealing with here. David probably won't understand it that far, but it’s what God is guaranteeing here."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You read this and think, “Well, Christ didn't have iniquity, so how can this possibly be talking about Jesus?\" There's a twofold answer to this. One (and least likely) God could be talking specifically about Solomon (and that might be how they would understand it). Or two (most likely) the translation here isn't quite enclosed correctly. \"If he commit iniquity...\" means \"if there is iniquity found on Him...\" and \"I will chasten him with the rod of men...\" means in the Hebrew \"When guilt is laid upon Him, I will chasten Him, and I will be His Father, and He will be my Son.\" God's mercy is displayed on the resurrection of Jesus Christ. We see the wrath of God poured out on His own Son, and we see the mercy of God poured out THROUGH His Son."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It is true. David's kingdom is still established. We might not see it in the same way, but his name is infamous, and the Lord still reigns. David's obedience and commitment are unmatched, and it is one that will forever go down in history as being righteous and dominant."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So Nathan stops David to tell him the news. I don't know how Nathan's demeanor is at this moment. He might be coming to David and thinking, \"I hope he's not mad at me for giving him the go-ahead just to stop him now...\" Whatever he was thinking, he knew he had to go through with telling David the truth, and God made it clear that David would have to wait to have the house built."}]}]},{"start":18,"end":29,"title":"David's Prayer","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David is humble about this. God just told him that He wants to build David's house first, and David wonders why God would want to do that at all for him. It seems like the Lord is being too gracious to David, and David wonders about it before the Lord."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David says, “It wouldn't have been that big of a deal to me to build the house. It would have been pretty quick and painless, actually.” He has everything at his disposal because the Lord has given it to him already. He says, \"You (the Lord) have been speaking about the One that is to come for a long time, and you are saying that it will be through my lineage?\" David is astonished at the idea that God would use David in this manner. To David, it is a much bigger deal to know that the Messiah will come through him than it is for him to build the house of the Lord."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"At this point in time, there are no people like the Israelites. Most of the people are worshipping idols, and the Israelites were trickling into that same mode of worship. But God keeps pointing them back in the right direction, and it is a lot like the Christian church today."},{"kind":"para","text":"We get sidetracked quite easily, just like the Israelites of old, and we end up wrestling with God over the direction we are supposed to be going. But God builds them, and gives them to us as an example of things to come, which is revealed to David in part, but to us as a whole! We have seen the Savior; David was just promised one."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David would have to wait for some time, but the promise could be trusted. David understood that God would keep His promises, and God certainly does keep His promises. It is through David that the seed comes. You might say, “Well, how do we know that for sure?\" We have two proofs of this."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The book of the generation of Jesus Christ, the son of David, the son of Abraham. Abraham begat Isaac; and Isaac begat Jacob; and Jacob begat Judas and his brethren; And Judas begat Phares and Zara of Thamar; and Phares begat Esrom; and Esrom begat Aram; And Aram begat Aminadab; and Aminadab begat Naasson; and Naasson begat Salmon; And Salmon begat Boaz of Rachab; and Boaz begat Obed of Ruth; and Obed begat Jesse; And Jesse begat David the king; and David the king begat Solomon of her that had been the wife of Urias; And Solomon begat Roboam; and Roboam begat Abia; and Abia begat Asa; And Asa begat Josaphat; and Josaphat begat Joram; and Joram begat Ozias; And Ozias begat Joatham; and Joatham begat Achaz; and Achaz begat Ezekias; And Ezekias begat Manasses; and Manasses begat Amon; and Amon begat Josias; And Josias begat Jechonias and his brethren, about the time they were carried away to Babylon: And after they were brought to Babylon, Jechonias begat Salathiel; and Salathiel begat Zorobabel; And Zorobabel begat Abiud; and Abiud begat Eliakim; and Eliakim begat Azor; And Azor begat Sadoc; and Sadoc begat Achim; and Achim begat Eliud; And Eliud begat Eleazar; and Eleazar begat Matthan; and Matthan begat Jacob; And Jacob begat Joseph the husband of Mary, of whom was born Jesus, who is called Christ.","ref":"Matthew 1:1-16"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Jesus himself began to be about thirty years of age, being (as was supposed) the son of Joseph, which was the son of Heli, Which was the son of Matthat, which was the son of Levi, which was the son of Melchi, which was the son of Janna, which was the son of Joseph, Which was the son of Mattathias, which was the son of Amos, which was the son of Naum, which was the son of Esli, which was the son of Nagge, Which was the son of Maath, which was the son of Mattathias, which was the son of Semei, which was the son of Joseph, which was the son of Juda, Which was the son of Joanna, which was the son of Rhesa, which was the son of Zorobabel, which was the son of Salathiel, which was the son of Neri, Which was the son of Melchi, which was the son of Addi, which was the son of Cosam, which was the son of Elmodam, which was the son of Er, Which was the son of Jose, which was the son of Eliezer, which was the son of Jorim, which was the son of Matthat, which was the son of Levi, Which was the son of Simeon, which was the son of Juda, which was the son of Joseph, which was the son of Jonan, which was the son of Eliakim, Which was the son of Melea, which was the son of Menan, which was the son of Mattatha, which was the son of Nathan, which was the son of David, Which was the son of Jesse, which was the son of Obed, which was the son of Booz, which was the son of Salmon, which was the son of Naasson, Which was the son of Aminadab, which was the son of Aram, which was the son of Esrom, which was the son of Phares, which was the son of Juda, Which was the son of Jacob, which was the son of Isaac, which was the son of Abraham, which was the son of Thara, which was the son of Nachor, Which was the son of Saruch, which was the son of Ragau, which was the son of Phalec, which was the son of Heber, which was the son of Sala, Which was the son of Cainan, which was the son of Arphaxad, which was the son of Sem, which was the son of Noe, which was the son of Lamech, Which was the son of Mathusala, which was the son of Enoch, which was the son of Jared, which was the son of Maleleel, which was the son of Cainan, Which was the son of Enos, which was the son of Seth, which was the son of Adam, which was the son of God.","ref":"Luke 3:23-38"},{"kind":"para","text":"You might notice that both of these lineages are a bit different. One is the lineage of promise, the other the lineage of man. Without getting too deep into it (I will in future chapters, probably in Luke or Matthew specifically), no matter which account you choose, they both lead back to David's line! Either way, the promise has been kept!"}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David's prayer is himself just being open and honest to the Lord. He says, \"If this is all true about what you told Nathan, then I want you to do it and show it to me. I want to be a part of it, and I want you to make it obvious. Make me an instrument towards it.\" David has an interesting heart. You read through the book of Psalms, and you can see his heart continually growing and breaking."},{"kind":"para","text":"Some people have pure hearts from an early age, and it is broken enough to callous you and make it hard for you to feel. Some people have a broken heart from an early age, and their heart hardens and stiffens with time. Some people are safe from experiencing the hurts of a heart, and so their hearts remain tender forever. Then we have David. His heart was broken, and mended, and expanded, and confused over and over and over again. Yet it was not his own heart that he was simply concerned about; it was God's heart that he desired…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":29}]},
    "8": {"chapter":8,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 08"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we saw David have the urge to build a house for the Lord. He couldn't understand why he should be worthy to have a place to dwell, and yet God has no permanent dwelling among His people. David understood that the desire of God was to be with His people, but it couldn't be David that built this house. It would have to be someone that comes into his lineage that does it. Of course, we know that Solomon builds the temple, but it goes even deeper than that. We now (through the church) have a place of dwelling with the Lord, and He has provided His Spirit in order for His people to worship Him in truth and in Spirit."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But the hour cometh, and now is, when the true worshippers shall worship the Father in spirit and in truth: for the Father seeketh such to worship him. God is a Spirit: and they that worship him must worship him in spirit and in truth.","ref":"John 4:23-24"},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see David building the kingdom and enlarging the borders. God makes his way prosperous and David uses the knowledge that he has learned from the Lord in the last chapter to give him a new burst of energy to expand and developing Israel."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":2,"title":"David Prospers","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now that God has made His covenant with the house of David, David has a newfound drive to prosper. He was already prosperous before, but knowing that the Lord will be with him, and understanding the amount of power and prosperity his future holds, he is going after the Philistines (Israel's constant enemy). \"Methegammah\" means \"mother city.\" I'm assuming this place was the Philistines’ main city of operation. I haven't seen or heard the Bible make mention of this place before, but based on the name I assume that it's a very important place for the Philistines, and yet they lost it."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, the Moabites are an interesting people. We have spent a great length of time talking about them in the book of Ruth. In fact, David's great-grandmother is Ruth, and Ruth comes from the land of Moab. David destroys a good portion of Moab, but he leaves one lineage alive. Your guess is as good as mine as to which lineage he chose to save."}]}]},{"start":3,"end":8,"title":"Israels Coasts Enlarge","entries":[{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, I find it interesting that David is getting rid of nearly all the horses here. He could have kept them for himself and to grow his supply (especially as his armies grow), but the Lord was specific about not gathering and multiplying their horses."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But he shall not multiply horses to himself, nor cause the people to return to Egypt, to the end that he should multiply horses: forasmuch as the LORD hath said unto you, Ye shall henceforth return no more that way.","ref":"Deuteronomy 17:16"},{"kind":"para","text":"It would seem as if David is trying to follow the commands of God here. However, he is also commanded not to multiply wives either..."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Neither shall he multiply wives to himself, that his heart turn not away: neither shall he greatly multiply to himself silver and gold.","ref":"Deuteronomy 17:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"But we won't talk about that part..."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David was growing Israel in ways that they didn't even know were possible. In fact, this was their job to begin with. We get a picture in the book of Joshua of God providing for His people this land through Joshua, and Joshua does a great job at growing it. But when Joshua gets old, he can no longer help lead these people to take more land. Their job was to destroy the people and take the land. Instead, after Joshua's death, they take land and leave the people. They start to connect with the people, which is all well and good, but after a while, the people of that land started to influence the Israelites, and so they were drawn away from the Lord (leading to the book of Judges). It's all a very sad account, but the saddest part of it is the fact that they stopped taking the land that was promised to them and eventually just gave up on what the Lord called them to do."},{"kind":"para","text":"David is fulfilling more of what God had already promised, and he is not just establishing his own kingdom; he is establishing the Israelites ruling. The problem is, the Israelites are influenced only by their king, and that was not how things should have been run..."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":14,"title":"David's Name Was Great","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David took all the spoils he had, and instead of building his own home with it, he dedicated it all to the Lord. Now, I assume this means he gave it to the Levites for the work of the Lord, or perhaps he was storing it to the side for the house of the Lord (which he knew would be built in time to come), but either way, he wanted the Lord to do what only he could do with it."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think that is a very fair and important way to view all of our belongings. When the Lord provides you with wealth, dedicate it all to Him so He can prosper you. David didn't just help Israel prosper, he was helping other nations prosper as well (apparently Hadadezer had a scuffle with a few other people as well and it was about time for someone to put him in his place)."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David is making a name for himself. People would know David. People would remember David. I find it funny that a good portion of people today reject the presence of the Lord in this world. They reject the idea that Jesus Christ existed (there are some scholarly atheists who don't stoop as low as the stupidity of rejecting proof of the man, but not all atheists can be so keen). But you know what they don't reject? They don't reject the existence of David. Actually, they have an easier time believing that David (the son of Jesse) came before a giant and cut off his head, than for God to have given His own perfect life to save everyone from their sins."},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, I find it harder to believe that David was a small young man when he did that, than I do that Jesus lived a perfect life and died for our sins. However, I dare not argue scripture because scripture has always had a way of proving me wrong beyond reproach. David made a name for himself, but you know where his name is mentioned the most? In the same book that points to Jesus Christ. Yes, David's name was huge, but it was to bring to assurance of what God had planned for the future of the world through the Savior. What a great lineage David represents!"}]}]},{"start":15,"end":18,"title":"David Led Correctly","entries":[{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Israel was powerful at this point. More powerful than it ever had been before. They came from being slaves to being the most powerful nation in the entire world. Of course, that was all through the gift and perseverance of God and His faithful few. The thing that is noted with David (and will be noted with many other kings to come) is that he was good at giving judgment and justice to his people. He knew where to point people too and how to give the people what they needed. There will be bad kings who don't know how to execute judgment and justice, and it will really show forth by their language and hate for the people that follow them. We would live in a much more prosperous era if we had leaders who could execute judgment and justice correctly. However, we live in an era that mocks what is right and promotes what is wrong."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Woe unto them that call evil good, and good evil; that put darkness for light, and light for darkness; that put bitter for sweet, and sweet for bitter!","ref":"Isaiah 5:20"},{"kind":"para","text":"Interestingly enough, Isaiah is talking about Israel here. It seems that in the future, Israel will not be calling out proper judgment (likely because of their leadership), and they will fall into all sorts of mischief and suffering because of it."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David was placing the proper people into the proper places. He knew who would be good at their positions and who wouldn't be. He didn't elect these people based on skin color, or based on gender, or even based on wealth or honor. No, he chose these people because he knew these people, and he knew their strengths and weaknesses. He would have been a fool to put Jehoshaphat as a priest, and Ahimelech as a recorder. It would have been unreasonable to place Benaiah as the scribe and Seraiah as the ruler over the Cherethites."},{"kind":"para","text":"We live in a very strange era where we don't place people based on their strengths and weaknesses. All we care about is their ethnicity and their sex. If they are good at what they do, and if they love it is irrelevant as to whether or not they should be in the position we want them to be in. This is why we have a failing democracy, and it's why we see other nations starting to prosper over ours. We don't want to be wise about who we are putting where, because we don't think (ultimately) that it matters nearly as much as just trying to include the minorities. David prospered because he led by the charge of the Lord, and the Lord showed David who is fit for what position. If only we had leadership who was led like this today…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":18}]},
    "9": {"chapter":9,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 09"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: We have seen David's kingdom growing immensely since he has come into power, and the Israelites are being blessed because of it. He seems to have a new \"pep\" in his step knowing that God has promised a Savior and provision for his future and through his lineage. It's a wonderful reality to know that the Savior will come to you."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, David doesn't just want to honor the Israelites or the Lord here... He is searching for ONE man, and it's such a great picture of Christ. I find this chapter to be one of the most warming chapters probably in all of scripture."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":8,"title":"David Honors Mephibosheth","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"After all this time, David is still thinking about his old friend Jonathan. Jonathan made a strong impact on David, not just in his life, but in his faith. I truly believe that if Jonathan were not in the life of David, we wouldn't be getting the same picture of David here that we are getting. David wouldn't know how to prosper himself in the Lord, or how to encourage himself in the things of God. Jonathan showed him how to do that, and Jonathan was being missed at this moment. David is under the impression that all of Saul's kids are dead, and there is nothing that David can do to pay homage to Jonathan, or to the household of Saul."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Ziba\" means \"statue.\" I don't know how that's important here, but I figured I would share this odd bit of information, seeing as we have done our best to interpret names. This statue man seems to have been lurking in the background, and David enquires some information from him to see if there is a way to bless Saul's house (specifically through the line of Jonathan, but Ziba doesn't know that). The best news that Ziba brings is that JONATHAN (specifically) has a son! How did David not know about Jonathan's family? I know David was moving around a lot, and there was no way to continuously hold conversation with Jonathan because his father was consistently trying to kill David, but still... I would think there would have been some mode of conversation about it at some point. Either way, what joy David must have felt knowing that Jonathan had a son at this moment."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, if you don't remember Mephibosheth was in hiding previously."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Jonathan, Saul's son, had a son that was lame of his feet. He was five years old when the tidings came of Saul and Jonathan out of Jezreel, and his nurse took him up, and fled: and it came to pass, as she made haste to flee, that he fell, and became lame. And his name was Mephibosheth.","ref":"2 Samuel 4:4"},{"kind":"para","text":"This servant likely knew that he was in hiding (specifically because he was of the house of David, and the Philistines still hated that house), yet he reveals this hiding place. I assume that Ziba trusts David at this point, and that is perhaps the only reason he has let this information go."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Mephibosheth\" means \"exterminating the idol.\" There's a lot of deep connection I could make here, but I think we get the picture. I want the scene of David's compassion to be highlighted here. Mephibosheth has likely heard about David, if not through his father, then through the events that were currently taking place in Israel. He has no idea what David is about to do with him. In fact, he might think that because his hiding place had been found out, he was going to be killed or treated even worse. Perhaps his grandfather had more of an influence on his understanding of who David was. If that is the case, then Mephibosheth was likely thinking that David was ready to kill off the last of his grandfather's heritage at this moment..."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Mephibosheth doesn't think very highly of himself. David is giving him above and beyond just his grace. He's restoring land and prosperity to Mephibosheth and his family. He's giving him preservation and longevity by having him sit at the king’s table. He's doing what Saul did not do for him, and that was treat him with kindness and respect, simply because he truly loved his friend Jonathan. Jonathan set up a legacy for his family even after his passing, and David was going to be sure to honor him the best way he could. What better way to honor someone you love than by treating their kindred like royalty?"}]}]},{"start":9,"end":13,"title":"Accepted Instantly","entries":[{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Mephibosheth will have quite a bit of people serving and living with him. Fifteen sons and twenty servants is a lot to take care of this one man. Mephibosheth would likely never have to really work again in his life, and he would always have people to provide for him. I'm sure (even with his handicap) he had been trying to live as normal of a life as possible, staying under the radar so that no one came looking for him. Things changed in a blink of an eye for the rest of his life. It's such a great picture of salvation, and a really strong look at an Old Testament example of how Christ sets us up in this life if we would have Him. We sit before Christ as \"dead dogs,\" and yet Christ does not see us that way. He sees us as royalty because of who our Heavenly Father is! He provides for us and gives us a place at His table. What a great Savior."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Mephibosheth is not simply treated like another member at the table either. It's not as if this man is simply going to be eating out every night at a fancy restaurant in the king’s palace. Rather, he will be treated like family. He will be treated as if he is one of David's own children. He will not just sit and eat at the king’s table. He will sit and consistently eat WITH the king. What an honor for us all!"}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David doesn't ask questions about Mephibosheth's lame feet. That didn't seem to matter to him. What mattered to him was honoring this man and his lineage (specifically because of Jonathan's sake), and he wanted to show grace and love to him by providing for his needs and treating him like family. It should be recognized that there would be others sitting with them at this table as well. David had other children and friends, and servants, and they would all be sitting along with him eating and enjoying the fruit of their kingdom. None of them seem to say anything about Mephibosheth's lame feet either. Why? Because it didn't matter. The writer points it out here, making sure that the reader understands that Mephibosheth had an obvious and recognizable handicap, but nobody focused on it except for the writer. What we SHOULD be focusing on is the same, that all of the house of David was focusing on, and that was extending grace to the lame and needy."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's about spreading love and fellowship with your neighbor and kindness to those that need it most. I don't know how Mephibosheth's life was before this, but I don't think he felt very deserving of it, or needed by the people around him. People probably often looked down on him because he was lame in his feet. That would no longer be his life anymore because of the love of David."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Charity suffereth long, and is kind; charity envieth not; charity vaunteth not itself, is not puffed up, Doth not behave itself unseemly, seeketh not her own, is not easily provoked, thinketh no evil; Rejoiceth not in iniquity, but rejoiceth in the truth; Beareth all things, believeth all things, hopeth all things, endureth all things. Charity never faileth: but whether there be prophecies, they shall fail; whether there be tongues, they shall cease; whether there be knowledge, it shall vanish away.","ref":"1 Corinthians 13:4-8"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":13}]},
    "10": {"chapter":10,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 10"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we see David try and stretch forth grace and kindness for the house of Saul, in hopes that there was someone he didn't know that he can serve for the sake of Jonathan (whom he loved dearly, and seemed to be missing at this moment). Thankfully, he finds out that Jonathan has a son, and better yet, his son has a handicap, and David treats this man and his family like royalty."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, David tries to continue his streak of kindness when he hears that the king of the Ammonites is passed away. He wants to be a friend, and perhaps he is desiring to lead the example that his friend Jonathan left him."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":5,"title":"David Continues to Console","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David has been on a kindness streak lately. He has been trying to make alliances, not enemies. He wants to serve, and he wants to help, and the Ammonites were going through a difficult time at the moment with the loss of their king. The Ammonites didn't really care for the Israelites, though. The real question here is, when did Nahash or that family show kindness to David? Did David have any real tradeings or dealings with the Ammonites? If anything, Nahash seems to be a formidable foe to Israel."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Nahash the Ammonite answered them, On this condition will I make a covenant with you, that I may thrust out all your right eyes, and lay it for a reproach upon all Israel.","ref":"1 Samuel 11:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps Nahash helped David when he was on the run from Saul? Maybe he took shelter with him once, or maybe there was some provision made for David at some point. I really don't know, and I can't seem to find the answer to it. Whatever it was, it doesn't seem to be worthy of writing, and so we must just assume that David knew something that we didn't. After all, the people of Scripture lived a whole life outside of the pages of Scripture, not everything could be written down."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And there are also many other things which Jesus did, the which, if they should be written every one, I suppose that even the world itself could not contain the books that should be written. Amen.","ref":"John 21:25"}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So apparently the children of Ammon didn't take very kindly to this gesture. They seem to think that David has an ulterior motive. They think David is just trying to spy out the land to destroy them. Honestly, David didn't need to spy anything out to take them down. They were without a king, and they already knew what David and his army were capable of. It's kind of strange that they went to this assumption right off the bat, but maybe it's part of their grieving process? This is a great reminder for us as well."},{"kind":"para","text":"It doesn't matter what side of the line someone may be on, death is a very serious and sad occurrence because it will affect the people who were around that person. I knew of a man who was very difficult to be around if you were on his bad side. One particular person did this man wrong, and this man would never forgive this person. The person lost his father a couple of years later, and the man actually felt sad for him. I said, \"Why does it matter? I thought you didn't like him anyway?\" He said, \"It doesn't matter who you are or what you've done, it's not easy losing a father.\" I still don't know if he meant it to this day, but it's an interesting trait that society is missing today."},{"kind":"para","text":"Today, we want the worst kinds of punishment for the people we hate. No compassion or care for their families or their loved ones as long as they are hurt in the process. Christian men and women should be compassionate even to our enemies. This man was not a Christian, and yet he had more compassion for the loss of this guy’s father than most Christians have for their own enemies..."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This didn't seem like a grieving process... This was more personal, and they wanted David to know it. The more disturbing part is the fact that they were willing to cut off their garments straight down the center."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These men likely didn't allow this to happen with a bit of pushback. I don't imagine they just rolled over and allowed these men to do this. I think they probably put up a fight and squirmed at the process. Instead of David becoming furious with them, he just tells them to wait for their hair to grow back normally, and then they will be sent to the same people."},{"kind":"para","text":"I liken this instance to Paul, who didn't seem to know when to give up when it came to preaching the gospel. They could stone him nearly to death, and yet he would still find reason or excuse to go back to them and talk to them some more. It should be the same with every Christian man or woman. When the King sends you on a work, there should be no reason for you not to listen, no matter how humiliating it might be."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":8,"title":"The Ammonites Prepare For Battle","entries":[{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps I should have referenced some of the other chapters that this leads into. Chronicles has a little bit of a deeper account of these fights and conflicts, and so if you want to study more deeply about these fights, you can visit 1 Chronicles 19. But it seems that the Ammonites knew that they had put themselves as direct enemies of David, and so they were preparing for battle because they thought that David would come to attack them when the time was right. I don't think David had any intentions of harming any of them at all, even after they mistreated his servants, but now David had to tread lightly and make some important decisions here."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They were no match for the army of David. Joab knew how to prepare for battle, and there was nothing that this army could do to disrupt Joab's smarts. This is also not to mention that this battle was not even necessary."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":14,"title":"A Wasted Effort","entries":[{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Israelites are surrounded, and so they keep the enemy’s unity at a distance. As long as the enemies didn't clump together, it wouldn't be too difficult to take them on if it came down to it. We may also remember that it is unwise (every time) for the Israelites to go into battle without first consulting the Lord. They do this nearly every time. David has displayed the importance of prayer to them over and over again before a battle, and so they needed to take that example seriously in this situation, and yet they don't seem to be doing that."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord will always do what is good, but I think it's wise of us to ask Him what is the right direction before entering into a situation like this. They only had to take a few minutes to sit and pray to the Lord for direction."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Trust in the LORD with all thine heart; and lean not unto thine own understanding. In all thy ways acknowledge him, and he shall direct thy paths.","ref":"Proverbs 3:5-6"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Call unto me, and I will answer thee, and shew thee great and mighty things, which thou knowest not.","ref":"Jeremiah 33:3"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Thus saith the LORD, thy Redeemer, the Holy One of Israel; I am the LORD thy God which teacheth thee to profit, which leadeth thee by the way that thou shouldest go.","ref":"Isaiah 48:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"They, perhaps, knew that God was on their side, and that He was ready to give them victory (especially knowing that this battle was over nothing more than David trying to be kind to a selfish and foolish people). It's still wise to take a couple of minutes in prayer before the battle."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don't know if anyone died this day. It doesn't seem like there was a single drop of blood shed. The enemy just suddenly got scared and ran away. It seems to be that the enemy had a lot more men and power, and yet something caused them to get scared, and it scared the rest of the armies that were unifying with them. It's a little funny, honestly, when you read it like this, because I just imagine Joab getting closer to the enemy, and them just screaming, \"RUN!\" and everyone just scattered. Joab might have felt a little disappointed, honestly. If I were Joab, I would have been like, \"Aw...\" These people just spent so much money and time to get these armies from these other cities just to... run away?"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And when the children of Ammon saw that they had made themselves odious to David, Hanun and the children of Ammon sent a thousand talents of silver to hire them chariots and horsemen out of Mesopotamia, and out of Syriamaachah, and out of Zobah. So they hired thirty and two thousand chariots, and the king of Maachah and his people; who came and pitched before Medeba. And the children of Ammon gathered themselves together from their cities, and came to battle.","ref":"1 Chronicles 19:6-7"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is just silly..."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":19,"title":"A Second Try","entries":[{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This chapter's funny. David doesn't seem to be too worried about these people. I think David was still willing to make nice if they were willing to make nice, but the Syrians think they are able to take down the armies of David. Remember, it was the Syrians that fled first in the battle, and now they think they are able to get bold enough to destroy the Israelites. David is not personally involved because he doesn't want the Syrians to attempt anything stupid on their own land. So the Syrians set themselves for battle and actually fight against the Israelites. And you can guess what happens next..."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They didn't stand a chance. They never did. They knew they never did, and yet they still tried. Honestly, all they had to do was apologize and beg for forgiveness, and David was in a mood of forgiving, but they lost their chance."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Such a funny chapter! What a great ending! The remaining people actually DID beg for forgiveness, and Israel accepted their forgiveness. This is how it should have been from the start, but they chose pain instead of allegiance, and so the deaths of so many men are on their own heads."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's a great realization for us today, when we go out to bring peace to the hearers of our city. We preach to them about the peace and love of Christ, and they (instead) take it as a threat and desire to mistreat us. Our King is still forgiving to them, even though they are not willing to accept forgiveness. They set themselves in battle formation against the Lord and His army, but they won't win. In the end, our King has the final say, so, and they won't have a choice but to either serve Him or die in the battle."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":19}]},
    "11": {"chapter":11,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 11"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: We have been seen victory after victory coming from David and his kingship. He has made wise decisions, and has taken God's word for each move that he makes. Because of this, the kingdom is growing and prospering and everyone is enjoying luxuries that they haven't had in a very long time (if ever). Saul gave them plenty of luxuries, but not like how David provided. If only it stayed this way forever."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we start to see the downhill sliding of David in his life. We have seen the rise of David and the great strength that the Lord has provided for him, but from this chapter forward it seems that David makes mistake after mistake. I believe it's because he has so much. He has relied on his own strength and his own provision, and neglects the Lord. That is how we all face our downfall."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":1,"title":"The Kings Battle","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Apparently, the purge was not an original idea? I guess they had a season back then when war and fighting were treated kind of like sport. It was a way to pass time and prove your strength. It seems strange, but perhaps that is the type of entertainment we might be facing today if we did not have modern technology. The Hunger Games gives a pretty interesting example of this. Gladiator movies are dramatic, but they weren't too far off from reality. The world was quite barbaric in the old days. Either way, David sent out Joab and Joab's men to go into the battle for him, while David stayed behind at home. Perhaps David didn't feel like fighting in this Hunger Games today."}]}]},{"start":2,"end":5,"title":"The Lust of the Flesh","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David was alone. We should all be aware that Satan likes it when you are alone. I think there is no more dangerous time for a child of God than when he is by himself. Remember, in those days this is the Israelites hang-out spot. They don't have a front porch to walk around. They had a roof balcony to pace back and forth on. David is seen contemplating and he is likely thinking about the war that is taking place. His mind was surely busy on a lot of things... at least until he sees this woman..."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Hittites were not part of the Israelites’ original lineage. So it seems they were in close proximity to some foreigners, and it also seems that her husband was not at home during this time. Matthew Henry puts a very good look at this situation here in 3 easy steps towards sin. He says; \"1. Neglect of his business. He tarried at Jerusalem. When we are out of the way of our duty, we are in temptation. 2. Love of ease: idleness gives great advantage to the tempter. 3. A wandering eye. He had not, like Job, made a covenant with his eyes, or, at this time, he had forgotten it.\" He goes on to further point out that once you start on that downhill path it is a very difficult thing to stop. Newton's law, right? An object in motion tends to stay in motion. So when we are in the motion of sin it's a very difficult thing for our brain to process a stop to it. David was doing everything right up to this point. From here, we see a lot of things that David starts to do wrong and it starts with this very simple and unreasonable sin."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It took only one night of stupidity to change David's life forever. This will certainly change his life, and it will lead to a very terrible disaster. It is strange that Bathsheba chose to have her windows open while bathing this day. I can't place the entire blame on her, because how could she know that the king would be walking about his roof at that particular time? Maybe she thought all the men were gone out to war. That is where David was supposed to be anyway."},{"kind":"para","text":"When she was taken from her home, she likely didn't know what was going on. She was brought before the king, and the king took advantage of his power. She didn't put up a fight, however, and that is where the story gets dicey. If she had put up a fight, David would be in an even worse place at this moment, and no charge would be laid on her account. She didn't, and so now they are both guilty of a very terrible sin that has destroyed a great deal of men in our age today."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are a lot of broken homes due to this sin for various different reasons. I won't get into all of them in this setting, but we know that adultery of any kind leads to terrible decisions, and broken hearts on both parts. There are no winners in adultery. Everyone loses in that sin. The wives lose because they have broken their trust with their husbands. The husbands lose because they have broken their trust with their wives. The pastors lose because they have to give the honest truth to a broken family. The children lose because they don't understand why there's a split in their family. The church loses because they have to pick sides. Friends lose because they feed the lust. And the adulterers lose because they are entrapped by this sin and it's not easy to get out of."},{"kind":"para","text":"While Israel was fighting a battle for strength against these Godless nations around them, David was fighting a battle against his own godlessness. He never addressed this sin before, and now he is going to have to face the consequences."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":13,"title":"Uriah is Honorable","entries":[{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David forms a plan here that will (hopefully, at least in David's mind) get him off the hook. This is a ridiculous idea as we will soon see..."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David wants to get Uriah home so that he can relax, hopefully be with his wife, and enjoy his time out of the war. Everything seemed to go well with Uriah, and so David sends him a whole buffet of meat and provisions so he can relax at home. If I were Uriah, I would question the kindness of the king. Not because David was not kind, but because he was only being kind to Uriah. But David just hopes he'll take the bait, sleep with his wife, and then it will all just look like it was the reward of war."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Uriah was too tired and too defeated to go home. He wanted to be presentable before he did, and perhaps he was raring to go back out and fight for his family. What a sad moment for David. It wasn't what David expected, and it puts David in his place rather quickly. While this man was out fighting in a war to protect his country and family, David was neglecting his duties and abusing his power. This whole scene is messed up, and it is even more sickening to see it coming from David."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"While his people and fighters are all still in the battle, and the image of God's ark still living in tents, Uriah doesn't feel he deserves to go home and be with his wife. He belongs back in battle, standing arm in arm with the other soldiers. This should really put David to shame, but David doesn't take this image like this, and he makes everything worse..."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David tells a half truth here..."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David is trying to get him drunk enough to go home, or at least stumble so that he will make a mistake. Maybe even get him drunk enough not to remember what he was doing the night before, and perhaps David can just take him home and make him believe that he went home on his own. That doesn't seem to work though, and Uriah ends up sleeping back with the servants waiting to go back to battle."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":21,"title":"Uriah is Killed","entries":[{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David... Mighty man of war... Writes to Joab saying, \"Put Uriah in the middle of the harshest battle and then leave him there alone so that everyone can kill him.\" Why? Because DAVID made a mistake, and so he continues to make more mistakes instead of coming clean. Perhaps Uriah would have felt better dying in war than to find out that his wife was sleeping with the king. Perhaps it would have been easier to take a spear to the heart than the affliction of adultery in his household. David doesn't know that, though, and David makes a very foolish mistake by murdering an innocent man who has done nothing but provide and protect his country better than even his own king..."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Not only did it get Uriah killed, but David lost a few of his own men because they weren't prepared for this battle. I believe, in fact, that these men lost because of the sin of David. Had David not sinned and been in the battle with them, they would have likely defeated everyone without a scratch on them. But David (as leader of these people) provided several casualties more than was necessary for the lust of the flesh. Might I say that when you fall into temptation, it is a damaging thing to everyone around you? Your sin doesn't just afflict you, it afflicts everyone closest to you."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joab was giving a code back to David here. He couldn't just outright tell the messenger what happened, because then the news would get out about the fact that David purposely had Uriah killed. That would start to raise a lot of questions in the camp. Joab knew there was something up, but instead of asking questions, he simply did what he was told. I think that is a great sign of a faithful man."},{"kind":"para","text":"We should be in the king’s business as such. I am so thankful our king today doesn't do things based on His own flesh. Otherwise, we would be killing innocent people like David instructs in this chapter... The messenger will come back with the news and just think that Uriah was killed because he accidentally put himself into harm’s way without thinking, when in truth, he was placed there by his own country specifically to die..."}]}]},{"start":22,"end":25,"title":"David's Comfort","entries":[{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Even this messenger was more obedient and truthful than David... The foreigner was putting David (supposed to be a child of God) to shame in innocence in this chapter. David's military leader puts David to shame in obedience in this chapter. And this messenger puts David to shame in truth in this chapter. David is not looking good, and while he may feel a little better about the fact that Uriah will never find out, and his wife will think he was just killed in an accident, their conscience will always be aware..."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now Joab is given permission to take down the city that killed Uriah and a few other men. In all honesty, if I were Joab, I would be a little wary about going in to attack this city specifically because of what he had just done. However, he doesn't know the sin that David has caused in all of this. He is just following orders, and his king is supposed to be a man of God. God will provide the victory for these men, even though David is doing a poor job in his leadership at the moment. God will deal with David."}]}]},{"start":26,"end":27,"title":"The Light","entries":[{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God didn't like this. Uriah was protecting this king’s sin, and God was not going to just sit idly by and watch evil triumph. That is something you can always guarantee. When we see evil beginning to reign, and it seems like it has the advantage, God will always be there to take it down and destroy it in the end. God will never let evil reign, and we can bank on the fact that God already has the victory set. Even when good Christian \"Godly\" men do evil things, God will address them."},{"kind":"para","text":"This whole scene was not lost on God, and He will not just simply let it slip away. David, perhaps, thinks he got away with his deeds, and Bathsheba maybe thinks that things will get better, but they were both responsible for this terrible sin. David took it way too far, and will have to be dealt with. \"Bathsheba\" means \"daughter of an oath.\" I find that fascinating because she did not stick to her oath towards her husband. Perhaps they got married outside of a Godly matrimony, but she was married nevertheless to Uriah and didn't stick to her vows. \"Eliam\" means \"God is kinsman.\" Her father (probably) had a relationship with the Lord and thought of God as a very close family member. Perhaps he dedicated his daughter to God and his daughter was the oath to the Lord. Maybe that oath was broken somewhere? Or maybe Bathsheba just wasn't living up to her name."},{"kind":"para","text":"Here's the saddest part of the story though... \"Uriah\" means \"Jehovah is my light.\" David snuffed out a light this day all for the sake of his own sin. He was willing to douse the fire of God so he can experience the pleasures of sin for a season. We will find out in the coming chapters that it was not worth it. For now, David has his victory. It won't stay forever though…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":25},{"start":26,"end":27}]},
    "12": {"chapter":12,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 12"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: Our last chapter was a disaster to say the least. It was sad and burdensome. David committed adultery with a random woman while he was suppose to be out to battle with the Israelite army, and she became pregnant. In order to hide what he did, he tried to bring her husband home and make him think he was the one that got her pregnant, but he wouldn't fall for the trap. So instead, David has this man killed in battle (intentionally), and David turns around and marry's this woman so that the other people won't look down upon her or connect some dots that don't really add up."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we find out that David's trick and sin doesn't go unnoticed, and there are real and dire consequences for what he has done that he will have to face, not only now, but in the days and years to follow..."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":6,"title":"A Parable of Theivery","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, we understand that Nathan is a prophet of the Lord."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"That the king said unto Nathan the prophet, See now, I dwell in an house of cedar, but the ark of God dwelleth within curtains.","ref":"2 Samuel 7:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"David knows that it is likely not a great thing when a prophet comes and starts a conversation off like this, but David isn't thinking too deeply into it (as we will see in a moment). Nathan, perhaps, doesn't want to offend David, but when you have a word from the Lord, you don't have too much of a choice but to offend someone. Preachers and pastors don't try and go out of their ways to offend people (I pray to the Lord that it's not the case), but there is no doubt that someone will be offended if you decide to speak the truth. Two men in a city, one rich and one poor."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That, in those days, was what justified the rich in that class. If you had a lot of animals and herds, you were considered rich. That was their currency. Today, it would be equal to how many houses and how much money one man has. If he has a big house and a lot of money, people think of that man as rich."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This lamb, in the eyes of this poor man, was not currency. This lamb was his wealth, but for a far different purpose. I believe there are shepherds (still today) who love their sheep. I believe there are those who don't just grow them to slaughter them. I believe they are more than just currency to these types of people. This man LOVES his one sheep. It was all he had, and it seems he had a family as well to feed. It doesn't look like he was willing to trade this lamb for anything, and his family loved this lamb. The lamb was MORE than currency to him."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The rich man had plenty to give, but because there was a random traveller, he decided, \"You know what, I don't want to lose what I have. This poor man doesn't have much, and he probably won't even know that his sheep is missing. I'll just take his and kill it and feed this traveller.\" It's probably likely that this traveller was also rich. The traveller here likely symbolizes someone of importance. There is another story about a rich man and a poor man in scripture that I think could be useful to this overall context."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"There was a certain rich man, which was clothed in purple and fine linen, and fared sumptuously every day: And there was a certain beggar named Lazarus, which was laid at his gate, full of sores, And desiring to be fed with the crumbs which fell from the rich man's table: moreover the dogs came and licked his sores. And it came to pass, that the beggar died, and was carried by the angels into Abraham's bosom: the rich man also died, and was buried; And in hell he lift up his eyes, being in torments, and seeth Abraham afar off, and Lazarus in his bosom. And he cried and said, Father Abraham, have mercy on me, and send Lazarus, that he may dip the tip of his finger in water, and cool my tongue; for I am tormented in this flame. But Abraham said, Son, remember that thou in thy lifetime receivedst thy good things, and likewise Lazarus evil things: but now he is comforted, and thou art tormented. And beside all this, between us and you there is a great gulf fixed: so that they which would pass from hence to you cannot; neither can they pass to us, that would come from thence. Then he said, I pray thee therefore, father, that thou wouldest send him to my father's house: For I have five brethren; that he may testify unto them, lest they also come into this place of torment. Abraham saith unto him, They have Moses and the prophets; let them hear them. And he said, Nay, father Abraham: but if one went unto them from the dead, they will repent. And he said unto him, If they hear not Moses and the prophets, neither will they be persuaded, though one rose from the dead.","ref":"Luke 16:19-31"},{"kind":"para","text":"I've learned a great rule of thumb that makes proper sense to me while reading scripture. If it's a parable, there is not a name mentioned. If it's a true story, names are mentioned. Now, I can't know for sure, but I can likely connect this story to the story that Jesus gives in Luke 16. I link them because Jesus can only speak the truth, and He uses God's Word every time He preached to the people. Everything Jesus said can be linked directly back to scripture. I like to think that this is the full story."},{"kind":"para","text":"The rich man (perhaps) was a real man (who isn't named here either). Maybe the poor man here is Lazarus. \"Lazarus\" means \"whom God helps.\" I like to think that this poor man in this story receives justice in the end for eternity’s sake. He lived a poor life and lost everything he had (even having his prized possession stolen from him by the rich man), and now he sits in Heaven with the Father, while the rich man suffers for his sin."},{"kind":"para","text":"Nathan doesn't just bring this story up for no reason, of course. And when David hears of this story, David takes it seriously. Nathan said it in a way that made David believe it was a real circumstance (and perhaps it is, and maybe that was Nathan's way of subtly bringing this up to David, or perhaps that is just the message that God wanted Nathan to give him). Either way, the story doesn't sit well with David."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Rightfully so, what this rich man did was disgusting to say the least. He has everything, and there was no reason for him to take a poor man's only loved sheep when he had plenty specifically for the occasion. But greedy men are greedy, and they are easily enticed into sin."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David thinks there must be some justice served for this poor man. The rich man has to pay him back. The rich man has the sheep, so what should he give to the poor man now that he has stolen his one and only sheep? Even if the rich man paid \"fourfold\" back to the poor man with sheep, it was still nothing compared to his love for his one and only sheep. It was as if he had lost a child. You can't just replace your children with more children. It doesn't work that way."}]}]},{"start":7,"end":15,"title":"You Are the Man","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David didn't realize that his sin was noticed. He didn't liken his sin to this situation. Nathan, maybe, didn't know exactly what had happened, but he certainly knows that the Lord doesn't lie about these things. Maybe God told Nathan what happened verbatim, I don't know, but David knows (especially at this moment) what he had done wrong... It's fascinating to me that Nathan says this from the Lord in this last part, \"I would moreover have given unto thee such and such things...\" There were more blessings that God wanted to pour out on David. What more could David even ask for or receive? It seems he had everything, and yet God had more in store if he had just stayed the course."},{"kind":"para","text":"It is a matter of fact that God wants to bless His children. More often than not, we will receive blessings from the Lord, and we imagine that there is not much more He can bless us with, so we become satisfied with what He has given us (which is good and it's what we should do), but then start living as if God is done with blessing us. We live against Him, and we spoil His good gifts to us, and we cry and complain that everything is falling apart, when ultimately it's our own faults because we have limited God's resources in our lives."},{"kind":"para","text":"What David did, he did only because he was bored and lonely. He didn't go out to battle with the men, and if he had been busy with the Lord that day, he wouldn't have fallen into the sin that he did. We can feel a little grief for David here, because we are wired the same as him. It might not be adultery, but it can be a number of other types of sin that we fall into simply because we are bored, and are not concerned about being blessed by the Lord."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Well... it seems that Nathan has been told the whole story. David did what he did to try and suppress the truth, and yet the Lord didn't allow the truth to be hidden. Today, we have a lot of truth suppressors in this world. They try their hardest to reject the Lord, and to get everyone to agree with them as much as possible. Instead of looking to the love of God, they look to the hate of the Christian and the bad Earthly examples who have self-proclamations of Christianity."},{"kind":"para","text":"The world is full of bad Christian examples, I will give them that, but when they use the bad examples to label God's Word as false, they aren't denying the examples as much as they are rejecting the truth. Even still, no matter how hard they try and reject the truth, and how hard they try to suppress the truth, God's truth will always be revealed, and it is only by personal ignorance and stupidity that it can be rejected. David was learning that the hard way at this moment..."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ugh, my heart would sink. Of course, my heart would have sunk far before this, and before the incident with Bathsheba, but in his particular case, God is displaying His sovereign justice right within David's own household. It won't even be about the people on the outside causing a riot; it will be about his own children! We will see this happen soon enough."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David's secret is not a secret anymore. I don't think Nathan was the type of person to take this situation and gossip about it everywhere else, but David's servants will surely know, and it will be a very trying time for everyone that is around David in this season..."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David is weakened here. We can almost hear it in the words here. He seems distraught by his actions and has a repentant heart. Because of his heart of repentance, God forgives him immediately. It seemed as if everything was lost to David, and God may have told Nathan, \"If he repents, let him know I forgive him already...\" David repented, and God instantly forgave. This was not a false or fake repentance. David was truly repentant for his actions. Not just because his actions were found out, but because he had brought on a whole slew of new problems due to his sinful acts. Now, the Lord will spare David's life, and that is great for David, but he will still have to live with the consequences, and God cannot just let it slip by with a slap on the wrist..."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The enemies would seemingly use this occasion to say, “Well, their God must be pretty weak and evil if He is not willing to keep His own law on the matter.\" The enemies of Israel would use this happening to find reason to reject the Lord or continue in their disbelief of God's justification. They already sought reasons to reject God. God, by doing this, is leaving the example to the whole world that He enacts justice and perfect judgement on everyone, not just those that are against Him. It's still the skeptic today."},{"kind":"para","text":"We have plenty of men who will read their Bibles (half-read them rather) and try to find reason to blaspheme the Lord. They can look at a man like David and say, \"How can God choose this man? God is not just.\" They might even look at Abraham, or Solomon, or Jonah, or any of the disciples (for that matter) and say the same thing. They try to look for the flaws in Jesus as well, comparing Him to the bad examples of Scripture. There are no flaws in Jesus though, so they have to make up their own ideas of where Jesus must be flawed. David's child will suffer the consequences of this action, and it had to be a very problematic time in David's life. In all honesty, if I were David (who deserved death for his crime), I would have rather died over seeing my own child die..."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I'm glad God didn't take His time in enacting punishment here. How weak and weary I would be if I weren’t sure when the child would die. It would have been quite miserable to wait until the child was about five years old to start to see my reaping."}]}]},{"start":16,"end":23,"title":"The Child's Death","entries":[{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David thought that he could perhaps have God change his mind. He did not want to see his child die over his own stupid decisions, but God had to let this one happen. He had to allow this circumstance in order for the world to know that God will provide justice for what is good and right. Some can read this story and say, \"God is unfair, this child is innocent.\" The child is innocent, they are absolutely right. They can read the story of Abraham and Isaac and say, \"God is unfair, Isaac has done nothing wrong or deserving of death.\" and you would again be right. Isaac was not innocent, but he didn't deserve to die in that way. We can look to the cross of Christ and say the same thing, \"God is unfair, Jesus didn't do anything deserving that kind of heinous murder!\" You are right, once again, Jesus doesn't deserve it. All three of these examples (and there are many more that I can display) were given unjust punishments from the crimes of unjust people."},{"kind":"para","text":"We cannot blame God for these acts, when it is our own faults and doings. If the world were repentant and desirous of the Lord, perhaps this young baby wouldn't have died here. If the world turned itself around and came to the Savior, we wouldn't be waiting so long for His return. God wants the world to turn to Him, and sadly it requires some innocent people to die. Why? Because it leaves our sin without excuse."},{"kind":"para","text":"This child will be with the Lord this day. That is a great understanding (and we will see this as true in a moment). Christ is with the Lord. They are in better and far more capable hands than in this world. It's interesting to me that people would see this circumstance in scripture and have reason to complain about God's justice system, and then turn around and go out to the streets to preach a message about how important abortion is. God could have aborted this baby, I suppose. He could have allowed this baby to never see the light of day. He could have ripped the child to pieces taking it out of the mother’s womb. Yet, even God is not that barbaric..."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The servants were actually worried that the knowledge of the child being dead would make David spin even further into his depression. For seven days, David fasted and lamented over this child. Seven days straight, he was occupied in only praying and begging for the life of the child. God didn't change his mind. That hope that David had should be a spark of hope for all of us."},{"kind":"para","text":"We don't know if God will change his mind on the matter. When Eva (my wife) had a miscarriage, our heads were spinning. I didn't want to believe it was true (neither did Eva, of course). I didn't want to think that God would allow this thing to happen. I prayed that He wouldn't. Perhaps I should have fasted like David here. In the end, though, it was up to the Lord in what to do with this situation. All we could do was pray and hope that He would change His mind and a miracle would occur. There was no miracle, though, only sad news. Our happenstance was in far different terms than David's was, but I can sympathize with David in this moment here."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These servants dared not to lie to David. They knew it would be their heads if they chose to lie here, so they just came out with the truth right then and there (even though they were hesitant about it a few moments ago)."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David was not done with his sorrow, but there wasn't anything left to do. He had his answer from the Lord about what would happen with the child, and so he had to get up and move forward from here. I don't imagine that his meal was very joyous here. I don't know if he even tasted what he was eating, but he knew he had to eat something, and he knew that he had to continue his life."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David loved the Lord, and it was obvious that God loved David. He was hopeful that God would turn from this and gift David with this child, even though David understood and knew that he 100% didn't deserve it. David deserved to be in the place of that child. Yet God had plans still with David. We praise the Lord today that David was not killed off for this action. We praise the Lord that David still had other children with Bathsheba. One of those children will become very important to the lineage of Scripture, as well as the Messiah."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These are the most comforting verses I can bring to anyone that has lost a child. The child cannot come back to us, but we will certainly be able to go to them when eternity comes. Even still today, I pray to the Lord for my child in Heaven. I don't know what they look like, or if it was a boy or girl (my wife and I assume it was a girl; we just feel that fits properly), but I know that God listens to prayer, and I know that I can keep continuous communion with her because of Him. I thank the Lord we experienced this under His watch, because if we hadn't, we would be very lost and hurt people. Those that don't have the Lord and have experienced the loss of a child will never function properly in this life again."},{"kind":"para","text":"Scripture is a redemption story. That is likely what David was understanding at this moment. God was a God of redemption and salvation. That's what he thought would happen with his child, but he also understands that God has made a promise of redemption through his family and lineage that will come about as promised. This child's death was not going to stop God's promise from taking place."}]}]},{"start":24,"end":25,"title":"Peace is Born","entries":[{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Solomon\" means \"peace.\" Again, you cannot replace a child. It's important to note this particular child because he will ultimately provide some grand influence on the Israelites once David is off the throne. Bathsheba and David did not have peace at that moment, but this new child brought them peace. The Lord loved Solomon, and David might not have thought too deeply on the significance of Solomon, but it brought them peace in knowing that God was not done with them yet. Solomon, at this time, was David and Bathsheba's peace, and there will be quite a lot of peace when Solomon is on the throne, but the ultimate peace doesn't come until much later in the lineage of Solomon (of course through Jesus Christ who is our ultimate peace)."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Nathan didn't seem to care about the name \"Solomon\" and instead named him \"Jedidiah.\" \"Jedidiah\" means \"beloved of the Lord.\" It's likely God that told Nathan to call him that. I don't think anyone else really ever calls Solomon Jedidiah, though. They keep his name Solomon as far as I know. I do think Jedidiah fits him better, however."}]}]},{"start":26,"end":31,"title":"A Battle of Victory","entries":[{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We might forget that there is war happening in the background of all of this. While David goes through this trial, there are people putting their lives on the line. Joab takes a strong city and wants David to place his name there (David is the king, and so he deserves the honor)."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David finally gets back out to his work, and it would have been far better that he were there rather than delving deeper into his sin back at home. He continues to expand his city just like the Lord had prospered him to do, but this does not negate the fact that God will and must provide justice for his sin."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It's a victory that we end on in this chapter. It starts off with a story (an interesting story), and we get a rollercoaster of emotions regarding David's sin and the death of his child, and we end off on a high victorious note. However, when we get into our next chapter, we see God prove His point and David is confronted with a much larger issue (which ultimately stems from his own sin). God will keep to His Word on the matters to come, and it won't be an easy task for David nor for his children…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":25},{"start":26,"end":31}]},
    "13": {"chapter":13,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 13"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we saw what came of David's sin after killing off the man in whose wife he stole. It doesn't end well with David, and requires his newborn child to lay sick and deathly until David loses him. Through it, we see David learn some tough lessons, and yet there is still plenty for David to learn. Solomon is born right after this (which will become important later on)."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see that David still doesn't know how to deal with his family properly, and it really shows through the way he addresses these situations. This is a very difficult chapter to speak on, and it's not a fun one to read either."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":14,"title":"A Dysfunctional Family","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Alright, things are about to get pretty messed up here, and there's no easy way to talk about it. I'd like to skip this happening altogether, but there is no way around it if I want to properly and \"rightly divide\" the word of truth. Sometimes it is required that we talk about the heartaches as well as the blessings of scripture. I feel there are a lot of people who steer clear of these types of stories because it's not an easy one to speak on. But this is reality. This is history and truth, and we have to address these issues because David has made some poor choices."},{"kind":"para","text":"Amnon (David's son) was in love with Absalom's (David's son) sister. He did not \"love\" this woman in a godly way. \"Love\" here means \"lusted.\" Let’s get a bit of a backstory of these characters here before addressing the issue any further. Amnon is David's firstborn. His mother is Ahinoam. \"Amnon\" means \"faithful.\" We will find out pretty soon here that he is not faithful to anyone. Tamar is a \"half-sister\" to Amnon. Same father, different mother. \"Tamar\" means \"palm-tree.\" That doesn't seem to matter in this setting, but I figured it's nice to know the meaning behind names (perhaps bringing a little levity to our impending study)..."},{"kind":"para","text":"This opens up some of David's past mistakes now. Firstly, he married more than one wife. Yes, that was a mistake, and that will haunt him here. Secondly, he murdered an innocent man, and stole that man's wife for himself. That will now hurt his reputation and break some familial bonds right here. God will take note, and will not leave this unchecked... but I'm getting ahead of myself."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Amnon had a conscience. He knew that he was not going to be able to touch his half-sister. Tamar was pure, and she was protected. His flesh was telling him that there had to be some way to get to her, but his conscience was saying, \"You best sit this one out.\" There was probably something in his brain that thought of her as a sister as well (for that is what she is, whether he wanted to admit it or not)."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Jonadab\" means \"Jehovah is willing.\" Jonadab will definitely not live up to his name. He will squander his title. All bad decisions can be traced to that opening line, \"But Amnon had a friend...\" You might be thinking, \"How can my bad decisions be caused by my friends when I don't have any?\" Great question (and I'm kidding, of course)!"},{"kind":"para","text":"When we make wrong decisions, we are listening to our friend, the Devil. He is a friend that likes to whisper in our ears about what is good and not good. Jonadab is not a man serving Jehovah. He is serving Satan. Jehovah is not willing in this situation..."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jonadab noticed there was some conflict in his friend, and he did what any good friend would do and asked him what the problem is. Perhaps Jonadab wants to help his friend out. There are many qualities of a good friend today, but the best of friends are those you can talk to about your hangups. Better friends are those that can give you sound doctrinal advice. Friends, nevertheless, should be willing to hear the problems of those they claim to be friends with so they can help address the issue and find a solution. Jonadab will find a solution to Amnon's problem right now."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Just like Satan, Jonadab is very \"subtle.\" That was the description given to him in verse 3."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Now the serpent was more subtil than any beast of the field which the LORD God had made. And he said unto the woman, Yea, hath God said, Ye shall not eat of every tree of the garden? And the woman said unto the serpent, We may eat of the fruit of the trees of the garden: But of the fruit of the tree which is in the midst of the garden, God hath said, Ye shall not eat of it, neither shall ye touch it, lest ye die. And the serpent said unto the woman, Ye shall not surely die: For God doth know that in the day ye eat thereof, then your eyes shall be opened, and ye shall be as gods, knowing good and evil.","ref":"Genesis 3:1-5"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Be sober, be vigilant; because your adversary the devil, as a roaring lion, walketh about, seeking whom he may devour: Whom resist stedfast in the faith, knowing that the same afflictions are accomplished in your brethren that are in the world.","ref":"1 Peter 5:8-9"},{"kind":"para","text":"When you see a subtle person in Scripture, you can bet your money Satan has a foot in that man's life. It's still the case today. God will not be subtle about His presence and Will. God always makes it clear and focused, so when we have doubts, and we have confusion, and we have fleshly compassions, we can bank on the fact that it's Satan giving it to you."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For God is not the author of confusion, but of peace, as in all churches of the saints.","ref":"1 Corinthians 14:33"},{"kind":"para","text":"What Jonadab requested was not anything particularly lustful or dangerous, but at the same time, when your friend is this emotionally damaged by something like this, it's best to get him away from that thing, not draw him closer to it. If I had a friend who was addicted to meth, the last thing I'd want to do to him is bring him more meth. That is essentially what Jonadab is doing here..."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David didn't think anything deeper of it. He just wanted to make sure his son was well. I would have questioned why he wanted Tamar specifically for this occasion. It seems like a strange request. I would have probably questioned a little deeper about the situation, but David left it at that and did what his son asked. In fact, we notice that David doesn't really seem to be concerned at all with the lifestyle of his children. This is the first time we see David and his sons interact, and we have been on the life of David for quite some time. What a sad family dynamic. While David was trying to rule his kingdom with the Lord's prosperity, he let his most important ministry fall into shambles."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Tamar was happy to oblige for her brother. She did what she knew to do best and made some food for her brother so that he would feel better."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Amnon took the lead from there and sent all the men around him out. I assume these men were there on the order of the king to provide protection for him while he was sick. Obviously, of course, he isn't sick."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Tamar was just being kind to her brother out of sympathy for his illness. She wanted to help her brother feel better. She could never have expected what would come next..."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Tamar is making a lot of valid points. If Amnon did anything to her right now, he would not just ruin his own reputation, he would ruin her's and his father’s reputation as well. Actually, Tamar says, \"Just ask the king if we can be together, I am sure he will give you the okay that we marry first!\" Perhaps this was a last-ditch effort to escape so that he didn't force anything, or maybe she was trying to keep herself pure and she wouldn't mind marrying this man (though he is about to show why he's not worth marrying). Either way, she (even in this situation) was being level-headed. He (being overtaken by Satan already) could not think."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What a cruel, sad situation. It's an important reminder (I think) here that God does not approve of this in the least bit. He hates this situation and finds it diabolical. He (in His sovereignty) has given laws to the Israelites about this specific type of situation in Deuteronomy."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But if a man find a betrothed damsel in the field, and the man force her, and lie with her: then the man only that lay with her shall die: But unto the damsel thou shalt do nothing; there is in the damsel no sin worthy of death: for as when a man riseth against his neighbour, and slayeth him, even so is this matter: For he found her in the field, and the betrothed damsel cried, and there was none to save her. If a man find a damsel that is a virgin, which is not betrothed, and lay hold on her, and lie with her, and they be found; Then the man that lay with her shall give unto the damsel's father fifty shekels of silver, and she shall be his wife; because he hath humbled her, he may not put her away all his days.","ref":"Deuteronomy 22:25-29"},{"kind":"para","text":"Amnon has brought death to himself, and it's a well deserved one."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":19,"title":"Forever Ruined","entries":[{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is even worse! He didn't just rape her, he USES her. He scratched an itch, and now it's time to get rid of her. He's a despicable human being and deserves more than death. I'm glad that the Lord shows no pity for men like this in eternity. They will be spending forever in a place of torment and torture. This is why it's vital that we read this account."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are religions that attest (the Jehovah's Witnesses being our primary candidates) that there is no place called Hell. Hell is a bad word for those that don't know it. But God created this place for a purpose, and it's for those that certainly don't belong in Heaven. None of us (if we are honest) belong in Heaven. But the most evil of people don't belong there. The people that belong in Hell are people like this. They are disgusting, manipulative, and heinous."},{"kind":"para","text":"I spoke to some staff and their wives a few years back on the issue of lust. The topic of adultery came up, and the women seemed to agree that pornography was worse than actually cheating. Every man would seem to disagree with that. We all were under the impression that it would be worse to cheat on your spouse than to look at pornography. Truth be told, they are both equally heinous, but it seems the women find watching worse than acting. While I believe there is forgiveness for both (if they are willing to confess and repent), there is certainly a chasm of difference between lustful thoughts and lustful actions. Let's take Amnon and Tamar as an example here (seeing as they are the example of this chapter). If Amnon raped Tamar (which he certainly did; there's no question about it), would that be better than having lustful thoughts of Tamar?"},{"kind":"para","text":"Let's put it into easier terms. If there was a man who loved alcohol, would it be better for him to drink alcohol or think about drinking alcohol? Repenting from a thought is far easier than repenting from an action. The problem with Amnon is he isn't willing to repent at all, and that will not help this situation..."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"She understood that him sending her out after being treated like that was worse. At least if he continued to love her, there could be further conversation and a hope for renewal (perhaps a relationship). He didn't want that though. He didn't love her. He actually HATED her. \"Hated\" here means \"to treat like an enemy.\" She wasn't just disliked, she was disdained. He would have had no problem killing her after this act; that's the type of hate he has towards her."},{"kind":"para","text":"She didn't want to be hated. She didn't deserve to be hated. She didn't deserve any of this. This is the exact representation that atheists try to use to describe God's injustice. They say, \"If God is so good, why does He allow rape?\" He doesn't allow it. He hates it. He hates rape as much as Amnon hates Tamar (even more in fact). He considers it an abomination!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Now the works of the flesh are manifest, which are these; Adultery, fornication, uncleanness, lasciviousness, Idolatry, witchcraft, hatred, variance, emulations, wrath, strife, seditions, heresies, Envyings, murders, drunkenness, revellings, and such like: of the which I tell you before, as I have also told you in time past, that they which do such things shall not inherit the kingdom of God.","ref":"Galatians 5:19-21"},{"kind":"para","text":"God provides justice for these situations, and He will provide justice in a moment. God doesn't allow rape. He repays the rapist for their evil deeds. \"Why didn't God stop it then?\" You might ask. Let’s turn the question around for a second. People (no matter who you are or where you are from) have a problem in this world. It's called \"sin.\" It's a problem we all struggle with. When we sin, it is not willed by the Father, but rather by our own flesh. We are sinners. He is not. We make the free will choice to go against the Lord. He does not force our choices."},{"kind":"para","text":"Even our choice to LOVE Him is not forced. We have to make that decision ourselves. We, sadly, don't make good decisions. This is why it's important to have a relationship with the Lord who ALWAYS makes good decisions. He will always do what is right, even when we do what is wrong. God doesn't stop this from happening because God allows us to make our own decisions. We learn the hard way."},{"kind":"para","text":"There is an overall purpose for this as well that God knows will come to fruition which we will view shortly. I think the important thing to note (here) is this situation is not going to be just left between Amnon and Tamar. This is going to escalate past that, and it will become a familial issue. Sin ripples in its effect."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We should all feel terrible for Tamar. When I was in high school, there was a young girl who had a purity ring. She wore it because it was a gift from her father and a promise that if she took it, she would remain pure until marriage. Little did her father know that she was a fornicator (I don't know many in our school who weren’t; purity was not a topic of discussion). We all knew that she and her boyfriend were not staying pure because he would openly talk about it, and she didn't deny any of it. That ring was a sham worn by a phony person."},{"kind":"para","text":"Tamar could no longer wear this coat. It was a special coat only made for the pure daughters, and that was robbed from her. It was not by her own doing, but by her disgusting brothers’ doing. She was absolutely pained by the fact that this took place, and it seemed that no matter where she looked, it would remind her of this situation. There was nothing she could do. She was robbed of her purity..."}]}]},{"start":20,"end":22,"title":"Absalom Is Angry","entries":[{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Absalom finds out what happened, and he is trying to ease this pain. The first thing he does is take his sister home and try to comfort her. He says, \"Wait here, I will deal with this.\" It seems he goes to David (or David was there when the conversation takes place with Absalom), and it does not make him happy."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David is angry. So what does David do? Nothing... David does absolutely nothing in this situation. Godly leaders should not be ignorant to their ungodly sons’ actions. Yet it's usually the pastor's or ministers’ kids who are the most uncontrolled children in the church. I don't know why that is, it's just a common fact. I think a good portion of it has to do with the fact that these pastors and preachers are so busy in the lives of everyone else that they have neglected their own lives (and that of their families). They have been too disconnected from their family ministry, because they are too connected to their church one. Their church family becomes more of a priority than their own flesh and blood. David's kingdom was far more important to him than his son's issues, and this is why things like this happen..."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Absalom certainly wanted to say all sorts of mean and hateful things to Amnon, but he was controlling himself for the moment so as not to make a bad decision here. Perhaps he was hopeful that his father would do something about it."}]}]},{"start":23,"end":29,"title":"Absalom Avenges","entries":[{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"TWO YEARS! Two years pass and nothing happens... David is inexcusable here. There was no reason for David to just let this pass. Absalom doesn't let it go. He holds a get-together with all of his brothers (these are the sons of David), knowing full well that Amnon would be there."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Absalom invites his father to this event and I think desires the whole family to come along. There was intent (however) to still get back at Amnon. Tamar (no doubt) has not forgotten this incident, and David couldn't protect Amnon forever. Two years is a long time to fester on something. David probably thought that Absalom harbored no ill towards Amnon anymore. David rejects the invite, which I find extremely disheartening."},{"kind":"para","text":"When I was in full-time ministry, the Lord knows I did my best to unify with the church and the leadership. We (that is the teens ministry) held a lot of get-togethers, and we tried to sell and direct certain things that the whole church could be a part of. The problem is, the leadership didn't want to connect, and so because of it we were often left to fend for ourselves. This is not how leadership should be done. I think if David had gone to this dinner with the rest of the family, things could have been very different. David doesn't want to go though, and Absalom can't do anything about that."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Maybe this was his desire all along. Maybe he wanted Amnon to go and for his father to stay. I don't think so. I think Absalom has waited for something to happen, but David was not giving any concern or care for the situation for the better part of two years. Now he has the opportunity to pour into his kids (they will all be there at this time), and David still rejects it. So Absalom will get his father’s attention, and he will deal with a problem that's festered for far too long."}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They all saw what happened, but none of them stuck around to talk to Absalom about the killing that just took place. This family is a mess. All the sons of David probably heard the story of what happened to Tamar, so I don't think it was much of a surprise that this took place here. That is perhaps the reason they all leave rather than talk about it."}]}]},{"start":30,"end":33,"title":"News Comes to David","entries":[{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Rumors spread quickly. I don't know who this person is that spread this rumor, but he jumped the gun a little too soon. Instead of saying, \"Absalom killed Amnon,\" this man says, \"Absalom killed ALL of your sons!\" There's a big difference between those two statements (David has a lot of sons, let’s not forget)."}]},{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jonadab is speaking the truth here. I can't make this guy out to be a good guy, but he is making sense. A broken clock is right at least twice a day. Which side is Jonadab on? I think he likes the drama. The king’s life and family were just a telenovela to this man."}]}]},{"start":34,"end":36,"title":"The Boys Weep","entries":[{"start":36,"end":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We will address Absalom's running in a moment, but I want us to notice that these brothers were saddened by the death of Amnon. Perhaps they were just in shock by the suddenness of this killing. They couldn't have expected anything less. Maybe none of them knew about the issue that Amnon had with Tamar. Either way, they all came to their father and wept. The servants wept. David wept."},{"kind":"para","text":"David weeps, but he could have done more. He weeps, but he didn't even try to connect with his sons. David weeps over something he could have helped prevent. David weeps over the death of another son, but this death could have been prevented if he had taken more concern in the matters of his children."}]}]},{"start":37,"end":39,"title":"Absalom Flees","entries":[{"start":37,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Absalom is in direct relation (through his mother) to the king of Geshur. David married a good number of women, and one of them came from a nation outside of the promise. This was dangerous in a few ways, but this is now coming back to bite David. Absalom found refuge with David's in-laws and David's enemy."}]},{"start":39,"end":39,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I think David cared about Absalom. David now realizes his mistakes in not being there for Absalom, and not interceding with the situation that Amnon put himself in. David wants to bring Absalom back, and let him know that he is sorry, and that he forgives Absalom, but David doesn't make any move towards reconciliation. He just wants it to happen without him doing anything to make it happen."},{"kind":"para","text":"I believe David thought of Absalom as the perfect fit to be the next king of Jerusalem, and I think that is what David wanted more than anything. Yet he wasn't gearing his children for this, and I don't think his children really understood what their father wanted. They just saw that Dad was busy in his duties, and everyone else was left to their own devices. It's a real shame that David neglected this opportunity to seek out Absalom. It's a shame that David didn't get more involved with his children to teach them some lessons that he learned the hard way."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":29},{"start":30,"end":33},{"start":34,"end":36},{"start":37,"end":39}]},
    "14": {"chapter":14,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 14"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we see a lot of drama ensue with the family of David. His oldest commits a cruel and terrible act which causes another one of his sons to end up killing him (because David doesn't do anything about it), and that son runs from his home to protect himself from his father. David was upset and undone about the whole situation, and couldn't figure out whether or not he wanted to forgive Absalom for killing his oldest son, or to condemn Absalom. So he just leaves the whole thing alone and tries to ignore it."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see that David can't ignore it and it's ruining his leadership in nearly every area. David needs to do something, and yet he isn't taking the advice of anyone else on the matter, so Joab (David's right hand) devises a plan to get the attention of the king in this matter so that amends can be made (prayerfully)."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":3,"title":"A Hired Mourner","entries":[{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that David was feeling pretty terrible at this point in time and was not sure how to proceed forward. He was likely not trying to proceed forward. He just wanted things to work out and nothing really was. He feels bad about Absalom and seemingly wishes he would have done more to mend his son’s relationships (or at the very least listened to Absalom a little deeper). Joab takes it upon himself to try and cheer up David, and sends a mourner over to get David's attention. It looks like Joab might have known this woman from before. Either way, it was pretty common practice to hire people to mourn with others, but this woman isn't going to mourn with the King. She is going to get the King to do a task to set the King’s priorities straight."}]}]},{"start":4,"end":7,"title":"A Sob Story","entries":[{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This seems a little cruel. She is telling David about this made-up story of her family, and David is tasked with giving the proper judgment of the situation. She gives a story that is nearly identical to David's with his two sons. Now, I don't know about you, but if I heard this story happening to someone else in the same way it was happening to me, it wouldn't make me feel better, and it wouldn't make the pain go away. Actually, it would just make me remember, and I would feel even worse. Perhaps, that is what David needed. He isn’t addressing the situation, so he needs to know how to address it."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":11,"title":"The Son Protected","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This son is granted immunity. He's granted the pleasure of forgiveness. This is how David should be viewing his relationship with his son. I believe that is the ultimate goal here (as we will see in a moment)."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":17,"title":"David Needs To Grow","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The woman asks, \"Don't get mad at me here, but why are you not doing the same thing for your son? Why don't you go after him and bring him home? That is what a father is supposed to do, is it not?\" Yes, a good father goes after his children, like a good shepherd goes after his sheep."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he spake this parable unto them, saying, What man of you, having an hundred sheep, if he lose one of them, doth not leave the ninety and nine in the wilderness, and go after that which is lost, until he find it? And when he hath found it, he layeth it on his shoulders, rejoicing. And when he cometh home, he calleth together his friends and neighbours, saying unto them, Rejoice with me; for I have found my sheep which was lost. I say unto you, that likewise joy shall be in heaven over one sinner that repenteth, more than over ninety and nine just persons, which need no repentance.","ref":"Luke 15:3-7"},{"kind":"para","text":"Moreover, the people of Israel were suffering because of the negligence of David in this matter. They were being mistreated, and the woman was playing the part of what life was like in Israel. They likely heard what Amnon did, and they probably were okay with Absalom's actions because what happened was despicable. It should have been taken care of a long time ago, and now David loses TWO sons over his negligence. David should make the plea for Absalom to return to where he belongs, and that is what this widow is implementing in this conversation here."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is (ultimately) the king’s job. He is to make wise decisions and right decisions over the people he is supposed to be leading. He is supposed to give them the proper judgement and proper ruling, and at the moment, David couldn't even think about his kingdom or the people in it because of everything happening in his life. Honestly, he might have needed to take a step back and allow someone else to lead for a little bit."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think that is important for a leader to do. I've seen pastors stay the course and are noble for the cause, but turn up being discouraged and angry with everyone and everything all the time. They don't become beneficial to the ministry as much as they become overburdened with it. If they learned to take a step back for a season, they would know how to refuel their purpose."},{"kind":"para","text":"I've also run into the exact opposite of this example. Pastors who take too much time off and the people suffer for it. David was kind of in the middle here. He was their king, and he was \"in charge,\" but he also wasn't doing anything and letting the place be controlled by nothing. He needed to take care of his familial issues and let someone else take charge in the meantime."}]}]},{"start":18,"end":20,"title":"David Connects the Dots","entries":[{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David seemed to know right away where this was all coming from. He understood that there was no reason for this woman to know so deeply about this matter concerning the king. Joab was the only one who was around to know it, and he was the only one that would be willing to get the king’s attention in this way. Others were, perhaps, a little more afraid to do anything because they didn't want to upset the king. Joab was trying very hard to get the king to turn around and make things right."}]}]},{"start":21,"end":24,"title":"Almost There","entries":[{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David sends to bring back Absalom. He seems to want to have a conversation. I believe that David truly wanted to forgive Absalom. I think he wanted to make things right. I also think he didn't know how..."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David STILL doesn't make it right. He still neglects his duty as the father of Absalom! All this is doing is driving Absalom further away from his father's leadership. Honestly, Absalom was justified to be upset with his father. He might as well have stayed at Geshur if his father wasn't going to speak to him anyway. Absalom is still treated as an enemy, and he was summoned by his father to begin with."},{"kind":"para","text":"In the story of the prodigal son, we see that when the son returns home, the father doesn't turn him away and send him to his own house. Rather, the father opens his arms wide and is happy to see his son return (even though his son squandered his life)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he arose, and came to his father. But when he was yet a great way off, his father saw him, and had compassion, and ran, and fell on his neck, and kissed him. And the son said unto him, Father, I have sinned against heaven, and in thy sight, and am no more worthy to be called thy son. But the father said to his servants, Bring forth the best robe, and put it on him; and put a ring on his hand, and shoes on his feet: And bring hither the fatted calf, and kill it; and let us eat, and be merry: For this my son was dead, and is alive again; he was lost, and is found. And they began to be merry.","ref":"Luke 15:20-24"},{"kind":"para","text":"If David had known this parable, he might have changed his tone."}]}]},{"start":25,"end":27,"title":"Absalom's Beauty","entries":[{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Absalom was liked by the people. Even his hair was worth great value to the Israelites. It seems that Absalom cared so much about his sister that he even named his own daughter after her. It would be another reminder to David (if he cared at all) that his granddaughter's name matters. It would be a reminder that David didn't do anything about the issue."}]}]},{"start":28,"end":33,"title":"Absolom Gets Davids Attention","entries":[{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Two more years of the king’s negligence. This adds up to a total of four years (perhaps more) of the king deciding not to address the issues with his son. David was not being a wise steward of his relationships with his family."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Absalom is trying to make the move here so that his father would pay attention and speak to him. If David wasn't going to do it, Absalom would be the one to get his attention somehow. This might seem extreme (setting Joab's field on fire), but the provisional loss that Joab would face would certainly get David's attention."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I laughed at this. I don't think it was necessarily meant to be funny, but it comes off a little funny. Joab says, “Hey man, what's with the fire that you caused to my field? What did I do to you?\" Absalom got the attention of Joab, who would likely get the attention of the king."}]},{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We will see in the coming chapters that David can't seem to ever fully forgive Absalom. David's heart (as a father) was not set correctly. He was certainly not mimicking the example of his Heavenly Father in this area. Sadly, there are many fathers today who are hung up on their pride and hurt and don't see it fit to forgive their sons for anything. Even smaller things than Absalom. Absalom KILLED another man (his own brother)."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are sons who (perhaps) got an F on their report card, and that sets their fathers over the edge. There are sons who can't learn to drive a car that has a broken stick shift, and that sets their fathers over the edge. Whatever it is, God is giving the example of forgiveness, and though our fathers on Earth may not forgive us, we can be confident that the Lord has and will."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":27},{"start":28,"end":33}]},
    "15": {"chapter":15,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 15"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: We have been getting a bleak look through this section of 2 Samuel about the falling and negligence of David and his family. It seems (as the reader) that David has not paid very close attention to his children, and even after all the time of rejecting to forgive (finally having the chance to talk to Absalom again in our last chapter) David still doesn't look too deeply into his family affairs."},{"kind":"para","text":"We will see, in this chapter, that David should have paid a better look to his son Absalom because there are things that start to take place that will put David in a very weak and terrible position. This chapter doesn't end on a very good note, but there is plenty to look into and think about as we read."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":6,"title":"Absalom Grows a Following","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Absalom knows what it's like to have his petitions ignored by the people he respects and looks up to. It was his own father that let the situation go. In this instance, he is trying to hear out the matter of people's pains. He wants to use what he has gone through as a means of serving those that need the same help. I believe he wanted to give them a fair shot at justice and new life. But there was a catch to his help..."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He's trying to overthrow his father's authority. He is under the impression that he would do a much better job if he were made ruler and judge over the land. He's playing on the hearts of the hurting. That is what many of our political leaders do today."},{"kind":"para","text":"Instead of providing hope for the future, they say, “Oh, if I were made judge, I could help everyone around me.\" That is not what they want to do. They are just power-hungry. How many politicians have to hurt and maim people for all of us to get the hint that they are not out for your best interests? Politicians are only out for themselves, and it's why we can never depend on them."},{"kind":"para","text":"David was set in his position of power, not because he was looking for it, but because the Lord had provided for him. Absalom is looking for power, and that is what will ultimately lead to his fall. David should be aware of this and make sure everything was up to par with Absalom, but David doesn't seem to be concerned about the happenings of his children at all..."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is not leading up to Absalom actually serving the people. What this is leading up to is a split in the house of David. This is what Absalom is essentially doing..."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"These six things doth the LORD hate: yea, seven are an abomination unto him: A proud look, a lying tongue, and hands that shed innocent blood, An heart that deviseth wicked imaginations, feet that be swift in running to mischief, A false witness that speaketh lies, and he that soweth discord among brethren.","ref":"Proverbs 6:16-19"},{"kind":"para","text":"If we look at this list (which isn't given yet; it will be Solomon who pens this after he is made king), Absalom fits this description to the letter. In fact, all our politicians fit this description. It's a sad and cruel reality, but that is what our world looks for, and that is why it's \"...an abomination unto...\" the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sadly, it's how a lot of our church leaders are as well. The Lord wants His children to be of a humble spirit;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Humble yourselves therefore under the mighty hand of God, that he may exalt you in due time:","ref":"1 Peter 5:6"},{"kind":"para","text":"A truth bearer;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But speaking the truth in love, may grow up into him in all things, which is the head, even Christ:","ref":"Ephesians 4:15"},{"kind":"para","text":"A peacemaker;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Blessed are the peacemakers: for they shall be called the children of God.","ref":"Matthew 5:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"A servant;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"If any man serve me, let him follow me; and where I am, there shall also my servant be: if any man serve me, him will my Father honour.","ref":"John 12:26"},{"kind":"para","text":"And a unifier;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Finally, be ye all of one mind, having compassion one of another, love as brethren, be pitiful, be courteous:","ref":"1 Peter 3:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"Sadly, we are bearing the marks of Absalom, and often it's due to the same reason that Absalom is doing what he is doing here. It's because we have been wronged, and we think we must do what it takes to make it right. However, it is not our duty to serve vengeance."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"O LORD God, to whom vengeance belongeth; O God, to whom vengeance belongeth, shew thyself.","ref":"Psalm 94:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"I will still give Absalom some sympathy here. I believe he saw that there were a lot of people mistreated in their ventures, and so he genuinely cares about these people and their hurts. I think that is a posture to take. Sadly, he is going about this all wrong."}]}]},{"start":7,"end":12,"title":"Absalom Devises a Takeover","entries":[{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Absalom played the long game. Forty years is nothing to wink at. He is up there in age, and he has a plan at this point. Perhaps, after hearing so many accounts of those that have been neglected, he has had enough, and so he says, \"I'm going to do something about this now. I can't take it anymore. Forty years and nothing has changed.\" So he devises a plan. Going to \"pay his vows\" to the Lord in Hebron seems like a strange request to make. Why couldn't he pay his vows right there in Zion? Wasn't that where the Lord's temple was anyway?"}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This should seem a bit off to David, but it doesn't seem to faze David at all. Absalom wasn't in Hebron. How could he make a vow there? David hasn't been very careful about his children's happenings since the beginning of all of this, so I guess why start now?"}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"At least Absalom went to where he said he was going to."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So here's the start of Absalom’s takeover (or attempted takeover anyway). He is waiting to get out of the city, and he goes to Hebron (the place where David made his start in reigning), so he can send in messengers and get the people ready to rise against David. All of these people had been hurt in one way or another, and Absalom was their go-to man for sympathy and support. I don't know if Absalom really ever did anything to help serve these people (likely not), but they liked him because he was sympathetic. We are an easy people to please..."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, the men didn't seem to understand that they were a part of a rebellion against their king. They were just following Absalom because, for the past forty years, he had been a kind soul and a loving leader. They will soon find out that they were not on the right side of history."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ahithophel is close to David. Somehow, he gets involved with the rebellion. Again, I don't think even Ahithophel knew what was going on. Absalom is playing on their emotions, and they all think what they are doing is for a good cause and that Absalom is to be trusted. Really, he was leading them into a slaughter against their own people. We sit and think, \"These people are nuts; they are crazy for thinking that Absalom is to be trusted.\" We, of course, know better because we have a top-down view. They had a horizontal view."},{"kind":"para","text":"I look at our governor today (in California) and think, \"Boy, these people who are behind him and stand up for him are a little loopy; they are standing up for all the wrong things in this world.\" Truth be told, they really are on the wrong side of history. They feel they are doing something great and making real change, but the change they are making is for the destruction of what is good, to replace it with what they deem \"better.\" Transgender children, ease of drug use, abortions at every corner, stealing of up to $2000, and laws that don't allow the criminals to be stopped. These things are not better, and it has not led to a \"better\" society. I can view his followers as nut jobs, but the truth of the matter is, they have a horizontal view. They aren't seeing the big picture, the top-down view."},{"kind":"para","text":"If we are children of God, we must view the picture from the perspective of God's Word. Just like we might put on a pair of glasses to see clearer, we need to view the world through the lens of Scripture. We should not be asking, \"What's better for me and those around me?\" We should be asking, \"What pleases the Lord here?\" We will be blessed above measure if we lead through that view."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":18,"title":"David Flees","entries":[{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems strange that David (with all his might and power) would run away from this situation. We would think that a king would stand up for his kingdom. David has found himself in a lot of hot water lately, and (as we will see in a few verses from now) he doesn't think the Lord is really on his side in this matter. Actually, he thinks that the Lord has allowed this situation specifically because of his recent sins. David seems to have lost some connection with God, and so he feels inadequate to form a fight back against Absalom. He is under the impression that God is done with him."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This gives us a little more of a clue that David was hopeful to return. He didn't think he would be gone forever. Or at least he hoped that the Lord would prove Himself in David's life and help him to return. His house will (at least) be kept in order while he's gone (however long that might be). It seems like a theme in David's life, doesn't it? He is constantly running, rather than going to the Lord first for direction. It seems better for all of us to just flee away."},{"kind":"para","text":"I know for me, I find it easier to just step back and hide in my cave. I would rather be alone with my thoughts and sorrow than to continue to make face with people and pretend like everything is okay. I have been that way for a good portion of my life. Perhaps it was the way I was raised. Maybe it has something to do with my personality. Maybe I am just not going to the Lord enough. I understand David's frustration here though, and I know his thoughts in this matter. If the Lord really wants me to continue in His ministry (that He has placed me in), then He will lead me back."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that David still had a good chunk of people following him. If we rounded these people together, he had at least six hundred people. Now, in the grand scheme of things, that might not seem like much. Six hundred is quite a large number though, and David could still do a lot with his remaining six hundred."}]}]},{"start":19,"end":23,"title":"The Weeping People","entries":[{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David says, \"Why are you here with us? Absalom has no quarrel with you. If you stay with us, he will see you as an enemy, and you don't need that on your bill for your people.\" David is giving him a leave today. He has a chance to save himself and his people, and there was really no reason for him to get involved with this issue here. But Ittai had a different thought behind it..."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This man was a loyal man. \"Ittai\" means \"With me.\" His literal name insists on loyalty. He will be a strong soldier for David later on because of this. Ittai is not just willing to remain loyal to David in \"death or life,\" he was willing to bring his whole family along for the ride."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They were leaving their comfort and their homes. They felt betrayed by Absalom. They felt rejected by the Lord. They felt abandoned and confused. There was great reason to weep and cry in their current circumstances. The Lord hears their cries. David understands this very well."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"In my distress I called upon the LORD, and cried unto my God: he heard my voice out of his temple, and my cry came before him, even into his ears.","ref":"Psalm 18:6"},{"kind":"para","text":"David knows what he deserved for his recent acts of sin and negligence, and he knows that he ALONE should be fleeing, and yet there are six hundred people willing to be afflicted and bear the load with David. That is what brothers and sisters in Christ should be doing. We should all be willing to bear the load for one another, even if it is not our own sin we are suffering for. I wish we had a heart like this in our church today."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are too many who leave the load bearing to the leaders, and there are too many leaders who neglect the load bearing because they are too busy or too forgetful. If David had borne the load of Absalom at the beginning, he would be in a very different state right now."}]}]},{"start":24,"end":29,"title":"The Ark Returned","entries":[{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The priests took the ark of God. They brought this ark with them (perhaps) thinking that they would need to build a new house for Him somewhere else. The Lord had already said it would be David's son who would build His house in that city (Zion). David knows this, and so this is the test of faith on the behalf of David and all the people that are with him."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Again, David knows what he deserves. He deserves God's wrath. He deserves to be wandering. He deserves to pay for the sins he has committed without a second thought. The Lord is gracious though, and we will see that He is very soon. At this moment, however, David is just waiting on the Lord. If God wants David to return, David will return. If He doesn't, well, that will be obvious too."},{"kind":"para","text":"Waiting is a very difficult thing to do for the child of God. It's quite stressful to wait and see what the Lord has planned. But David knows that God is always worth the wait."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Wait on the LORD: be of good courage, and he shall strengthen thine heart: wait, I say, on the LORD.","ref":"Psalm 27:14"}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Remember, seer (in this time) is the title they gave to \"prophets.\" These were people who would relay messages from the Lord. These prophets were commissioned to go back with the ark, and if the Lord gave a message to them about what to do next, they would come find David and let him know what the Lord would like to do."}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now David and his companions wander and wait. They find a very interesting place to weep and pray."}]}]},{"start":30,"end":37,"title":"Mount Olivet","entries":[{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This “Mount Olivet\" will later be known as the \"Mount of Olives,\" where Jesus spent a good portion of his time praying during his ministry. It must be a good place to pray and weep over the lost. I think there should be a “Mount of Olives\" type place for all of God's children. If you don't have a place where you can weep and pray like this, I suggest you find one out and visit it often."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This had to hurt David exceedingly. Ahithophel was a trusted man to David. I am sure David shared some deep secrets and hurts with Ahithophel. Ahithophel could use that against David to help Absalom grow in his tactics against David. This was a \"salt in the wound\" moment for sure. I speak from experience (as so many can attest to me), there is nothing worse than a trusted friend betraying you. I'd rather be shot in the foot."}]},{"start":37,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Hushai was a good friend to David, and it seems that David felt like he could trust him. Hushai was willing to weep and feel for David while he was in hurt. David didn't want that for him. So David conspires a conspiracy of his own and sends Hushai back to Jerusalem to catch up with the priests. Hushai will serve Absalom, and whatever Absalom says will be entrusted to Hushai, who can entrust that message to the priests, and the priests can give that message to their sons who will then turn and give that message to David. It was a game of telephone. Hopefully, everyone will have their best listening ears, and they won't be forgetful hearers. David wasn't about to try and take anything back. He wanted to see what the Lord would do. If Absalom was meant to be king, David was okay with the Lord's decision. If not, David needed to know what to do here."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":29},{"start":30,"end":37}]},
    "16": {"chapter":16,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 16"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: Things have not been looking very good in the life of David for some time now. In our last chapter, we saw David forced out of his kingdom by his own son. His son started a revolt against David, and David didn't want to kill his own people, so there was a small group that followed David out and now he is living in the wilderness (again), on the outskirts of Jerusalem. He sends his friend back (and the Levitical priests) so that they can keep watch on Absalom and bring back word of what should happen next."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we get a look at how David's wandering is going (as well as with the people following David), and what steps are taking place for Absalom now that David is no longer on the throne."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":4,"title":"Ziba Visits David","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This seems like a nice gesture. David is not in the best place right now (spiritually or emotionally), so for this servant to come and offer all of this while they are about seems nice. However, there was more of a purpose for it than just to be kind to David, and David seems to think there was a trick to what was happening here. David loves Mephibosheth (for the sake of his father Jonathan, remember), but David doesn't owe any allegiance to this guy, Ziba."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David wonders what it's all for, and the servant says, “Well, the donkeys are here for you to ride on, the food is there for you to eat, and the drinks are there for you to drink.\" That's obvious, but he missed the point of the question. Why did this servant bring it? I think David understands how to use these items, but he doesn't understand why Ziba is bringing these items."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Mephibosheth seems to believe that (now that the king is in dire straits) there is some land ownership that needs to be sorted out for Mephibosheth. Ziba knows that Mephibosheth belongs on the throne (he's in the lineage of Saul after all), but it is David that God has placed as king. David isn't thinking about that at the moment, and so he grants Ziba land that belongs to Mephibosheth. This is something that certainly needs to be dealt with when David gets back."}]}]},{"start":5,"end":8,"title":"Shimei Hates David","entries":[{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Shimei seems like a well-adjusted citizen of the land. Every word coming out of his mouth seems to be pleasant and controlled. I'm kidding, of course. I have walked around the streets of LA (or even here in Lancaster), and I have seen my fair share of people standing about cursing to the sky and at buildings. They seem a little out there, if I'm honest. This guy (Shimei) doesn't seem to love life at all, and it could be because David had shown up. He seems to hate David. Saul's lineage is not a fun one, and they disgrace the name of Saul every time they are brought up. Every single one of Saul's children seems to have bitterness lying within them (specifically against David). David, if you don't recall, tried to stay away from the throne, but the Lord kept leading him back. That seems to be the life of a Christian."},{"kind":"para","text":"We are called to do something, and the last thing we want to do is make enemies. We know that people will be upset about it, and we don't know how to go about it without causing some strife one way or another, so we just try to avoid it (it's better to avoid strife). When the Lord calls you into something, however, you will not have a choice but to go into it, and there won't be a lot of people who are very happy about that decision. Even the people in the church will stand firm against what the Lord has called you to do. It is quite rare to find a church family who will search out God's calling on an individual and help them proceed if it's true."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is not a fun scene. If I was David I would likely just leave. What's the point of staying at this place knowing that these people are not happy with your presence?"}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I mean... Shimei isn't exactly wrong. David (with all due respect) deserves to be in the position he currently is in. He had made some huge errors and lapses of judgement in his own family, and in the land. He has murdered people (in cold blood), he has neglected his family, stolen a wife from an innocent man... David is not in good standing at the moment to say much of anything."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":14,"title":"David Allows Shimei to Cast Stones","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There is a level of respect that you should be showing the king (even if you don't agree with him). Shimei doesn't seem to care about the king’s position at all, and disgraces it by his actions. David could easily deal with this man if he saw fit, but David also knows that he isn't wrong. David is feeling like a failure at the moment... Abishai can shut him up though, and asks permission to get him quieted."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This kind of reminds me of a scene in the New Testament."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And it came to pass, when the time was come that he should be received up, he stedfastly set his face to go to Jerusalem, And sent messengers before his face: and they went, and entered into a village of the Samaritans, to make ready for him. And they did not receive him, because his face was as though he would go to Jerusalem. And when his disciples James and John saw this, they said, Lord, wilt thou that we command fire to come down from heaven, and consume them, even as Elias did? But he turned, and rebuked them, and said, Ye know not what manner of spirit ye are of. For the Son of man is not come to destroy men's lives, but to save them. And they went to another village.","ref":"Luke 9:51-56"},{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord certainly had the power to rain fire down from Heaven to destroy these people, but He chose not to. He chose mercy over their negligence and found a different place to minister. Just because the Lord has the power to destroy, doesn't mean He always needs to use that power. Just because David had the power to kill this man quickly, doesn't mean he needed to abuse that power to do it. His words and stones were a means of strengthening David in the long run. It seems to be how the Lord strengthens His people quickly and effectively. I don't like it. I doubt anyone else reading this today likes it. But that is the Lord's way of doing it, and in the end, we are always better off for it."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Even his own son doesn't care for David at the moment. David has let too many things go, and too many sins infiltrate the kingdom. There had to be some justice for the actions (or lack thereof) in the household of David, and so here it is, all coming to a head. To be honest, I think most of this could have been avoided had David just dealt with it sooner. Small problems will always lead to bigger ones if they are not kept in check. That's a life lesson for all of us to take into consideration when dealing with problems of any sort. Especially as a leader, if we know there is something wrong (no matter how small it is), we should be checking on it and dealing with it quickly. I know my flesh is desirous to ignore the problem in hopes that it goes away, but very rarely does that take place."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That is how the Lord often works. He takes these moments of cursing and hurt to provide strength and direction. David has to be open to taking that direction, though (no matter how hard it may seem to him)."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This Shimei character is persistent. He follows them for a good, long while. \"Shimei\" means \"renowned.\" His presence was certainly well known, and they could not escape this man's continuous disgruntlement. I find it a little funny though. Some people will not relent. Some people will keep going and keep going and pushing forward to try and hurt others as much as possible. Shimei wasn't going to do anything else but throw rocks and call names. He had nothing better to do with his time. These people were growing quite tired of it as they were journeying along. They weren't even in the city anymore, and this man was still following them."},{"kind":"para","text":"I imagine this man has been traveling along for some time, and at this point, he is trying with all his might to continue to throw rocks and call names (parched and weary), because he is so upset. I think he's starting to grow tired. That's how they were able to find a time of \"refreshment.\" The stone thrower was too tired to continue, and they were able to get some relief. It might have been the greatest relief they had experienced in a long time, in fact. They had been weary far before Shimei, and now they know what genuine relief looks like."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":19,"title":"Absalom and Hushai","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So we have looked at the happenings of David and his men, and haven't really kept up with what was happening in the kingdom. Thankfully, we have some account of it, specifically because of David's friend being in the midst of the people, ready to give whatever news must come next to David."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That's actually a really good answer. He's not lying, but he also isn't giving the whole truth. He has been commissioned there by David, and so he is specifically there to keep tabs on Absalom, but he is also there because the Lord has made it clear to serve the people of Israel. That is also why we are in our place of worship today (mind you)."},{"kind":"para","text":"We are not there to get something from the leaders of a church. We are not there to (specifically) BECOME a leader in the church. We are there to serve the people. If the leader is doing something evil against the people, we continue to serve them without the help of the leader. That is my final say, and I will take that to the grave. Most people would not agree with that statement, and that is fine (we are allowed to have our own beliefs). But we should be a people of service, not a people of disservice, and there are a lot of leaders who think \"You either do it my way, or you don't do it at all.\" That is the wrong way to minister."},{"kind":"para","text":"I have no quarrels against any person of leadership (let me get that straight), but if we (for instance) were serving along our president, and he tells us \"Either serve the people my way or don't serve at all\" we would all neglect a good portion of our country. We would only focus on those that our leader wants us to focus on. This is wrong. Everyone needs service, and it's wrong to neglect it because your leader doesn't tell you to serve or tells you to serve differently than how the Lord has called you."}]}]},{"start":20,"end":23,"title":"Absalom Takes Power","entries":[{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Absalom wants the throne, and so he asks the counsellor of David what should take place to take it. Here is how Ahithophel thinks Absalom should go about taking this throne in finality..."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It certainly relays a message... This is absolutely disgusting and horrible. What a disgraceful act by any man, let alone someone who is taking the throne. This act, however, proves that Absalom has reign and power, because there isn't a single person that can stop him at this point. All of Israel will forever have this burned into their brains, and so will we (because we just read it)."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ahithophel seemed like he knew what he was talking about. They acted almost as if Ahithophel's words were greater than God's Word. We should remember that the Lord said this would happen..."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Thus saith the LORD, Behold, I will raise up evil against thee out of thine own house, and I will take thy wives before thine eyes, and give them unto thy neighbour, and he shall lie with thy wives in the sight of this sun. For thou didst it secretly: but I will do this thing before all Israel, and before the sun.","ref":"2 Samuel 12:11-12"},{"kind":"para","text":"David is now forced into a position that no father wants to find himself in. He has to find a strategy against his own son. David is good in these situations (God has proven that over and over again with the chasing of Saul), but David has to tread lightly due to the fact that this was his own son that he is up against. If you haven't noticed, the only way that David gets victory through these difficulties (over and over again) is by seeking out a relationship with the Lord. That is what David is doing right now. He is seeking out connection to God. He knows that if things are right with him vertically, everything falls into place horizontally. That is a good lesson for all of us to keep in our hearts."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":23}]},
    "17": {"chapter":17,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 17"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we see Absalom take the chance to steal the throne from David. He does some terrible things to get the people to recognize his new leadership, and David is last seen with his men in the wilderness. Not a lot of people really like David at this moment, and they really don't have many places to turn too and go."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see Absalom and the men around him come up with ideas about how to establish his throne a little further. The biggest issue they currently face is the fact that David is still alive. As long as David lives, there will always be conflict on the throne. So Absalom will recieve some advice about how he should take care of that."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":4,"title":"A Plot to Kill David","entries":[{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There is an obvious strong hate for David, and the people around Absalom were helping to influence that. Remember, Absalom is David's literal son. David let too many things go, and now there's a plan to get David killed, and in all honesty, this plan was a pretty good plan to make that happen. But really, David was already gone and already defeated. He wasn't out fighting to receive his kingdom. The Lord granted that kingdom to David, and David had forgotten that."},{"kind":"para","text":"The interesting part is that Ahithophel pled this idea that it would bring peace if he were to kill David and bring the other people back. There is no peace in the kingdom, and I don't think there really ever could be if Absalom remained king. The people, no doubt, were extremely hurt and confused, and were still reeling from the last scene they just witnessed as Absalom took the throne. There is no peace in the Middle East."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Anything that had to do with killing and destroying his father, as well as his father's legacy, was going to be intriguing to Absalom. It had nothing to do with bringing the people back. Honestly, it could have been said that Ahithophel would kill all the traitors of Israel, including David, and Absalom would have been just as pleased. As long as David was dead, he had nothing to worry about."}]}]},{"start":5,"end":14,"title":"Hushai's Advice","entries":[{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Hushai will be a voice of reason in this matter. Ahithophel wants bloodshed and wrath. Hushai will give better advice."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Hushai isn't wrong. David will be well protected by the people who are following him. It was not as if David took a bunch of random strangers with him out of Jerusalem. These people were his men of battle. David was a man of battle. He knew the strategies of war. He knew how to fight. It would likely not end well for anyone desiring to go against him, and finding him would be quite difficult. I have no doubt in my mind that David learned a thing or two about how to properly hide himself after being chased down by Saul so many times."},{"kind":"para","text":"David actually sets the proper example there. Imagine if Absalom was able to have his father killed. David could have killed Saul quite a few times, and didn't do it because he wanted the Lord to avenge, not himself. The Lord put Saul in the king’s position, and it was obvious to David that the Lord would take Saul out of it when the time came. Absalom doesn't have that mindset here, however, and so he is taking these things into his own hands. All that will do is bring judgement from the Lord."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God was using Hushai's advice. He was actually the one running the conversation. Hushai says, \"Don't let just a few thousand men go into battle for you. Why don't you lead the battle yourself, and use all of Israel?\" This sounds counterintuitive, seeing as there are now MORE men going to war, but this puts Absalom in the position of a leader, whereas before it would have just been one man going in and killing David. David never led battle that way, and a good king would not be absent when battle comes their way. They liked Hushai's idea better, but ultimately this will lead to the benefit of David more than Absalom. Absalom doesn't realize that Hushai is on David's side, and not on Absalom's side."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":20,"title":"The Priests Almost Caught","entries":[{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Hushai does what David had told him to do in a situation like this, and gets the priests to go and warn David. It seems like such a simple plan. It should be as easy as sneaking out to say a word and sneaking back in to avoid conflict."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That is quite a close one. Absalom almost caught the priests, and it would not have ended well for them. Absalom isn't stupid. He will have to watch his back in this situation. He will have to be sure that he is playing his cards right. He wants to have victory in this battle, and he very much wants to make his father pay for the sins he has caused in his life."},{"kind":"para","text":"Absalom was certainly not a man of God, and that was showing. Actually, all of Israel is seeing what life is like with a godless man on the throne. That is something they are certainly going to get in the future multiple times over. Saul was a godless man, but he was a conflicted man. Absalom is not conflicted. He is angry, and he knows exactly what his goal is."}]}]},{"start":21,"end":23,"title":"Ahithophel Dies","entries":[{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There's a fascinating correlation between passing over Jordan in this scene and passing over the Jordan in every other scene. It seems that every time the saved (that is, “Gods children”) pass over Jordan, it is a symbol of redemption. Perhaps that is why John the Baptist does most of his ministry in these waters. He sees the symbol that God has represented over and over again here."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Woah... This is very sad. I mean, this man was a cold-blooded killer, but I feel this was an extreme reaction to this situation. Simply because the king didn't listen to him, he took it to heart and thought his life was not worthy to live anymore. Maybe he suspected that David would be back in charge if his counsel wasn't followed, and he didn't want to be known as an enemy to David? Or maybe he just cared so deeply about his job, and the fact that he wasn't listened to ended his career. I don't really know. But he seems to get everything in order first, and he takes his time in this realization. I'm under the impression that he was likely thinking about this move quite deeply before he just followed through with it. That is what happens with anyone that commits suicide. They think through this decision before just making it."}]}]},{"start":24,"end":26,"title":"Captain Amasa","entries":[{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They had to have just missed each other. Of course, Absalom is under the impression that David doesn't know the battle plan and that David is still out there somewhere cowering in fear and in turmoil. But David is coming back into his land, and he will do it during a time when NOBODY is left in Israel."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's interesting that Absalom chooses Amasa to be his captain here. \"Amasa\" means \"burden.\" I wouldn't think that would be the greatest person to put in charge of anything, let alone an army. Maybe there was more to this Amasa man than meets the eye. I don't know. Absalom must have known what he was doing, however. Even still, it seems that this man was related to Joab in a way. He was Joab's aunt, daughter, and son. Second cousin? It doesn't matter; the point is, scripture points out that Absalom wants this man to be in charge of the army here, and that is a note to keep our eyes on."}]}]},{"start":27,"end":29,"title":"The Ammonites Offer","entries":[{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Ammonites were showing kindness to David. David had made plenty of enemies, but the enemies that David had made were not enemies made simply because David was a mean king and ruler. Rather, these enemies hated David because they were mean kings and rulers. They wanted to take what David had. They saw the kind of blessings that were on David's life, and so they would make war with Israel because of it. David was a man protecting his people, and the Lord was blessing that motive."},{"kind":"para","text":"The other countries around him noticed the kind of blessings David was receiving, and the might that his people were gaining, and instead of making enemies, they made peace with them. It seems the Ammonites don’t want to see what happens with Absalom on the throne, and so they give comfort and provision to David and his men here to get them back on course. Now we are left on a cliffhanger. The Bible does that sometimes. We should have a desire to read more, and so we should (to see how this dramatic familial story ends)!"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":29}]},
    "18": {"chapter":18,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 18"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: We have been watching this story unfold with David and his son Absalom, and it seems that everything has been falling apart for David. David, more than anything else, wants his son to be okay and taken care of. He doesn't care about being king. He simply cares about his sons countenance. We saw his son move out with a good portion of Israel to try and find David and his men."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, it seems that Israel finds David's men, and David has to prepare them for battle. The battle doesn't seem to last long, however, and Israel doesn't seem to know what they are doing under the leading of Absalom."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":5,"title":"David Sets Up For Battle","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have David preparing his people and seeing how much of a chance they have if his son finds out they are there to take the kingdom back. My bet (based on numbers alone) it wasn't looking too good. Absalom had the whole nation of Israel with him, and David had the remnant. Now, we could argue that David had the stronger remnant (the mightiest of men), but in all actuality, they would not stand much of a chance against everyone else (especially because these people are family fighting each other). They, ultimately (and this should be the remembrance at this point) would have to rely on God as they entered into the land."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These were David's three strongest men, and he didn't want them to go into this battle alone. Sometimes we think that if we want the job done correctly, we should do it ourselves, but that doesn't work when it comes to everything. David was a fighter. He knew exactly what to do in this situation. However, when he comes face to face with his son, I (and the people) have no doubt he will choke and hesitate."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David is the most prominent target for the other side. The enemies wouldn't care about who dies or who wins, as long as David is dead. It was smarter for David to stay out of the battle because the other side would be focused on locating David over trying to win the war."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David took counsel from those that he was leading. Ultimately, it was all up to David. David did the wise thing, though, and chose to listen to the people he was leading. They were all in the majority agreement (I am sure there wasn't a single person there who thought it was a good idea for David to enter battle with them), and so David knew that they were probably right. This is a wise leadership tactic (mind you)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Too many leaders ignore their peers, or they believe they know better than anyone and everyone else under them. I don't know how many stories I've heard of leaders mistreating a person, and that person getting out from under that leadership, and they think, \"What have I done wrong? How could I have done better? Why did I mess everything up?\" Then another person ends up leading, and they have the same story. Then another... then another, and suddenly you find out it's not the people, it's the leader. The leader is treating all the people the same way, and they are all overburdened with the problem. The worst part is, instead of the leader speaking to his peers, he just assumes everyone else is the problem and not his own doing. That's the most ungodliest way of thinking. And you want to know something? It's often the church leaders who think this way..."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For I say, through the grace given unto me, to every man that is among you, not to think of himself more highly than he ought to think; but to think soberly, according as God hath dealt to every man the measure of faith.","ref":"Romans 12:3"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For if a man think himself to be something, when he is nothing, he deceiveth himself.","ref":"Galatians 6:3"},{"kind":"para","text":"We are in great danger if we think we know better than everyone else. There is a time to listen to the Lord (that should be always), and there is a time we should listen to those that the Lord has put around you (you should always be sensitive to this). Often (especially in the church or where there is great unity), listening to the people's unified response might be the best trade."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, David didn't say \"don't kill Absalom,\" but he said, \"If it comes down to you facing him, please be courteous to him.\" This could mean to kill him quickly, or it can mean to try and convince him to turn and talk. Either way, the end goal for Absalom was to kill David, and the end goal for David was to get his son back."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":8,"title":"20,000 Killed","entries":[{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"My goodness, that's a lot of people killed over a battle for nothing. It wasn't going well for Absalom's side, it seems. That should have been a sign to Absalom that something was off, because these men shouldn't be powerful enough to take on so many of his own without being killed by them. Twenty thousand is a big number."},{"kind":"para","text":"We live near a small town out here in Lancaster called Rosamond. My wife grew up in this town, and I went to school there as well. Currently (as of 2024, and it has grown since we were younger), it has a population of twenty thousand six hundred and fifty-four people. This city has a lot of homes, but that would be nearly all of the people slaughtered in one day, leaving a total of six hundred and fifty-four people remaining (just to give you a rough idea of how crazy this battle was). This battle was not something that David and his men really wanted to do, but it was something that they knew needed to be done."},{"kind":"para","text":"Absalom wasn't going to stop, and there were people that they loved who needed to know the truth. I guarantee 90% of those twenty thousand slaughtered on this day were people who were confused about what was going on. They knew they needed to listen to Absalom (their current king) if they were going to live in this land, but they also didn't want to fight against their own people. That is the casualty of civil war..."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That is interesting to me. It seems when they fled into the woods to run away from David's army, a lot of them got lost or got cornered. The woods actually helped David's men and hurt the Israelites because they didn't know the place very well. So it was worse for them to run than it was for them to just battle in this situation because it seems that some of them likely got lost or got hurt by natural things in the woods that they fled into."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":15,"title":"Absalom Slaughtered","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So, here, we either have a clarification as to what David really meant about \"dealing gently\" with Absalom, or we have a confusion as to what David actually meant. I don't think it was a clarity thing. I think that David knew that it was likely that Absalom was going to die, but he wanted his men to try and speak to Absalom first before it happened, and if he was not willing, then to take him out quickly and as painlessly as possible. That is not what we see taking place, however."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This was straight-up murder. They were told to try and talk to Absalom, and instead they slaughtered him in his weakest state. Absalom couldn't do anything to fight back in this moment, and they all knew that. They took advantage of his weak state and murdered him. Eleven men with weapons against one without. If we don't find this cruel, there is something wrong with us (whether Absalom deserved it or not). David was hoping to reconcile with his son, and that dream was (quite literally) dashed into pieces at this very moment."}]}]},{"start":16,"end":18,"title":"The Battle Ends","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We will talk about trumpet blowing in Revelation, so I don't want to give the detailed tie-in here, but it is wise for us to pay attention when Scripture makes mention of trumpets. This trumpet sound was to gather the people back in after the battle had ended. There is great significance to that sound, and the use of the trumpet that I don't want us to lose sight of. I look forward to the final day we hear the trumpet sound and we are all gathered together again. We look forward to it... but I guarantee David's heart sank as soon as he heard that sound, because it either meant that Absalom was ready to speak, or it meant that Absalom was dead..."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They didn't even give him a proper burial. They just took his mutilated corpse and put it in a hole, placing as many stones as possible on top of him so it would take a lot of extra work to get him out if anyone even found him again. But the war was over at this point, and no one found cause or course to continue to fight."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This seems like a strange and random bit of information placed here, because nothing more is really said about it after this point. This pillar was a reminder of who he was and what he wanted. He wanted to be remembered. We could argue (since we are first introduced to Absalom in this book of scripture) that all he ever wanted was to be remembered. No body really seemed to remember him, though, and his legacy is not a very good one to remember. David's family is in turmoil and wreckage. That pillar, if anything, was only a reminder of how little Absalom actually had and how meaningless all of this fighting was..."}]}]},{"start":19,"end":23,"title":"Ahimaaz and Cushi Run","entries":[{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Tidings\" means \"to bear news.\" Usually, when someone says they are bringing you “tidings,\" it insinuates a good thing. The war being ended is certainly a good thing, but the hearing of the loss of his son will not be very encouraging to David. This will be (as scripture has made mention) the third son that David has lost in this book."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joab had to get a story together about what had happened. I believe Joab knew that David would not be happy if he heard that he just straight up killed Absalom without a conversation with him. So he had to figure out what the best way was to tell David, so that David doesn't just straight up kill Joab in return. He would certainly be a fool to lie to David."},{"kind":"para","text":"Joab knew that Absalom's death meant the end of the war, and so (I believe) that is why he acted so quickly in this matter. He knew that David was wasting his time trying to speak with Absalom, and Absalom wouldn't listen or care. Maybe Absalom would agree to meet with his father, but that would only be to try and kill David right on the spot. Either way, Absalom was not going to be convinced of anything else, and so Joab took the only opportunity he had to make sure this war ended and David was back where he belonged. Was it a good way to do it? I don't think so, but I can also understand Joab's urgency to end this."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Cushi had a head start, but Ahimaaz had a bigger desire. I assume that Cushi didn't know much of anything that had happened. He just knew that they were done battling. Ahimaaz knows that Absalom is dead. Joab doesn't want Ahimaaz to get to David before knowing the whole story, but Ahimaaz knows that the king’s business requires haste."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And David said unto Ahimelech, And is there not here under thine hand spear or sword? for I have neither brought my sword nor my weapons with me, because the king's business required haste.","ref":"1 Samuel 21:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"So Ahimaaz, somehow, outruns Cushi to get to David and tell him the news. Joab probably thought that Cushi would already be close to David, so he wouldn't have to really worry about Ahimaaz. Either way, Ahimaaz didn't know everything. He just knew that the mission was complete, and that David's son was dead."}]}]},{"start":24,"end":27,"title":"Good News?","entries":[{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So the men see one running to give the king a message. If he's alone, that means there is news to share. David is waiting here for word on what had happened, and (no doubt) in continuous prayer that things would work out well. If there was one man coming back, that means they have some news to share. If there are a multitude of men, then they are probably not coming back with news, but for a slaughter..."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Two people with news to bring, and David is hopeful that one of them will give them good news about his son."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Oof... What a sentence. Ahimaaz had a lot of good things to say and do, and it will seem that Ahimaaz has good news, but that is only because he doesn't know the whole story."}]}]},{"start":28,"end":30,"title":"The War Has Ended","entries":[{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ahimaaz blesses the king (and the king’s God, which was a smart choice) and brings good news that the war had ended. Of course, David isn't concerned about the war; he is only concerned about his son."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So David still doesn't know what has happened to his son yet. Ahimaaz doesn't know the full story. I don't even know if he fully understands that Absalom is dead; he just knows that something happened, and there was a \"great tumult” (probably the men standing around the grave of Absalom after they had laid the stones over him) and knew that the trumpet meant the war was over. So his tidings were simple. Now David is waiting for Cushi to come forward and tell his side of the story."}]}]},{"start":31,"end":33,"title":"The Real News","entries":[{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The same greeting, and David doesn't care about anything except for the news of his son..."}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is not the best way to bear news (how can you bear news like this though?) Ahimaaz didn't give all the information, but Cushi gave the information somewhat cruelly. It was evident that Absalom was dead. Now, Cushi doesn't say how it happened, he just makes it known (quite clearly) that it DID happen, and there was no way that David was going to take this well and we end the chapter on this note..."}]},{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David's heart is broken. There was no changing this situation. There was no going back and doing things differently. David CAN'T give his life for his son. He can't fix the problem anymore. So, he weeps uncontrollably, and there was no way for him to feel good about this. This scene should break our hearts. David has done a lot of really dumb things, and has let too many sins go unchecked, and so we could read through all the past circumstances and say, \"David needs to be taken down a notch.\" Well, God has taken David down a notch, and David is at a point of undoing. We see his countenance here and (should) think, \"Okay, I think David has learned, it might be time to let up a little on him...\" David's pain is a lot for one man to bear. Sin is a lot for one man to bear…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":27},{"start":28,"end":30},{"start":31,"end":33}]},
    "19": {"chapter":19,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 19"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we saw the fall of Absalom finally come to fruition. We have seen so much conflict and confusion taking place in the life and family of David and the kingdom of Israel, and no body knows what side they are on, or why they are on it. Absalom was a people pleaser, and David was quite disconnected from the people. It is not an easy thing to run a kingdom..."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, David is still in suffering over the loss of his son. He cannot collect himself, and it starts weighing heavily on all the people who are following him."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":8,"title":"Time To Recollect","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I think David always suspected the outcome would come to this. It was not (obviously) what he wanted. He didn't want to lose children. He didn't want to go to war in his own family. He didn't want so much division. This was supposed to be a moment of victory for the children of Israel, but because of the kings’ weeping, there were no dry eyes among the people. This was not a victorious war for David."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"As they are going back into Jerusalem, they are doing it as if they are strangers. They are doing it quietly because of this somber moment. David could not compose himself, and even while he's traveling through, he continues to cry. I'm sure he is looking around and thinking, \"Everything reminds me of my son. Everything reminds me of what has happened...\" This was not a good state for David (or anyone else for that matter) to be in."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joab makes a point. If the war had gone the other way around, David would not have been pleased either. If everyone else died, would he have been happier? Would David have been more pleased about his son being on the throne and running the Israelites amuck? Which outcome would he have preferred more? Would he have preferred that everyone else die so his son could live and reign unrighteously, or that his son die so that David could reign righteously?"},{"kind":"para","text":"Was there a better way that Joab could have talked about this? Probably. This was a sensitive time for David, but it was also a time for Israel to be led, and (at the moment) there was no king for them. They needed a leader, and right now David could not be that. So Joab tries to get David back into power, and help those he is leading to know that David cares about them too."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He is sitting at the gate waiting and anticipating David's next steps forward. Would they continue to mourn? Would they continue to travel in the wilderness? Would they begin to feel abandoned by their own king? Would they never go back home? Who are they going to follow? David needs to assess this situation gently and give them hope and encouragement to move forward. That is a very difficult thing to do when you are not feeling very uplifted yourself. That is usually what a pastor has to do in the most pressing times of his own life. It takes a special man to lift people up when you are personally down."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":10,"title":"A Divisive Decision","entries":[{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This was a civil war. Now that the war is over, there are still arguments about what the next plan of strategy would be. Was it wise to bring David back? Was it better to just find a new king? There were a lot who thought that David should be placed as the rightful king, and a lot who thought that David was the enemy for killing their current king. If you don't remember, Absalom led a revolt! He had plenty of people on his side of the battle."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And it was so, that when any man came nigh to him to do him obeisance, he put forth his hand, and took him, and kissed him. And on this manner did Absalom to all Israel that came to the king for judgment: so Absalom stole the hearts of the men of Israel.","ref":"2 Samuel 15:5-6"},{"kind":"para","text":"Absalom was stealing the hearts of the kingdom, and so there were plenty of people who cared deeply about Absalom because he was personable to them. David, on the other hand, seemed like he was not. He was busy in his dealings in war, and not busy in his dealings with people. That is sometimes how it is with leaders. Especially in the church."},{"kind":"para","text":"We have pastors who are great at pastoring. They are people persons. Then we have pastors who are great at preaching. They are not people persons. The best pastors can do both. That is a rare thing to find these days, and it's a rare thing to find in a king. I don't think we have really seen a leader who is capable of both except for Solomon (spoiler alert)."}]}]},{"start":11,"end":15,"title":"David Re-established","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"As we've studied out before, the elders of the city were the judges of a matter. They were (sort of) the final authority. They had to be wise and godly men. The elders have said and done nothing. The people are requesting a decision, and the elders are sitting on the prospect. David wants to come back in a rightful way, not in a forceful way. Absalom did it wrong, but David wants to do it right."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Strife and battle unite a people. It apparently unites a people together more than actual blood relatives. David says, \"We are of the same family. Do you really want to act like you don't know me and you don't know what to do?\" The people were all of one heart in this matter and knew that David should be positioned as king. If the elders (at this point) decide they don't want David back, it would hurt the people, and David would be forced to lead them in a different direction. This would not be good for either side. Especially because they are physically related to David and they are his own family. It seems that when word gets to the elders, every single person in the entirety of Judah agrees to this decision. They ALL bowed their hearts to David and his return."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Every time we see a passing through the sea of Jordan, we get a bit of a spiritual picture. David and his men have returned back to the fold. They have returned home. They are where they belong. Praise be to God that He does not leave us abandoned without a home."},{"kind":"para","text":"I have met plenty of nomads at the church. They don't have a home. They hop from one place to another without a care or compassion for anyone around them (especially without a compassion for the leaders and pastors of the church). They don't feel like they belong anywhere, and (to be honest) they really don't belong anywhere because they don't want to try and unite with those around them. That is a big problem with their hearts. Their hearts are not willing to connect to the heart of the church. Do we love the church enough to fight for a position there? Do we love it enough to stay connected?"},{"kind":"para","text":"David loved his kingdom enough to fight for a connection there. He didn't want to fight for power, he wanted to fight for unity. The people thought he was worthy enough to be placed back on the throne, and perhaps he is. He certainly has learned some lessons over the past few years."}]}]},{"start":16,"end":20,"title":"Shimei the Benjamite.","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Great. Here comes Shimei, the stone thrower. He was not happy about David being around his part of town just a few chapters back, and now he's heard about what David is about to do and comes to meet with David. But he doesn't pick up stones this time..."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Shimei took a lot of Benjamites with him to meet David. If I were David, I would think, \"Great, another war. I cannot do this right now. I have too much going on to deal with this too.\" Saul's whole house seems to be coming to meet with David, and plenty of men from Benjamin. This doesn't seem very good..."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This was unexpected. Shimei had a very long and hateful spirit towards David ever since his father and brothers died. He did not want David to be in the place of his father. Now, he's seen here on his knees pleading with David to forgive him. Why the change of heart all of a sudden? It could have been that Shimei was just drunk at that portion of his life as he was casting stones (though I don't believe that to be the case). I think Shimei realized the type of hard decisions that David has had to make and cope with from this most recent battle. He saw that David had to have his own son killed in order to bring order back, and that is not an easy decision to make."},{"kind":"para","text":"Actually, David didn't want that to be the decision made, but it was what had to happen nevertheless. So I think Shimei felt some sympathy for David, and realized that David had a lot on his plate, and he just added to the pain of it all. David also had the right to kill Shimei for his act now that he was king, but I don't think David would hold that kind of grudge against Shimei. It could have been that Shimei was just afraid of David remembering that and it coming back to him, but I think it was more that Shimei was smart enough to see what David was going through."}]}]},{"start":21,"end":23,"title":"Shimei Forgiven","entries":[{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I guess Abishai hasn't seen enough bloodshed for today. He thinks that action should take place over this insignificant and minute problem. David had a choice to make. Would he look all powerful in front of everyone, or would he connect? That is the choice every leader is faced with. Sadly, most leaders chose the former..."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I love this picture. He pardons this man's sin against him. He forgives him and offers him security. David doesn't hold a grudge against Shimei, and I believe Shimei was sincere in his apology. I think David saw that, and it was not something superficial to him. It isn't always easy to forgive others of their trespasses, but it is required that we do so."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For if ye forgive men their trespasses, your heavenly Father will also forgive you: But if ye forgive not men their trespasses, neither will your Father forgive your trespasses.","ref":"Matthew 6:14-15"},{"kind":"para","text":"How can we know the love of God, and not mimic it? If there are leaders that are not willing to forgive, you can bank on the fact that they have never been truly forgiven by the Lord. David is feeling forgiven because he knows that God has placed him back as king. He didn't fight for that position; God just gave it back to him. God was not done with him yet. He felt that, and he acknowledged that."}]}]},{"start":24,"end":30,"title":"Mephibosheth Returns","entries":[{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Mephibosheth was a man of prayer and fasting. You can see it on his face (literally). He loved David immensely, and he wanted God to work out the details. He didn't even get dressed properly when he came down to speak to David. He was so excited to hear that the Lord had answered his prayer, that he rushed over without even taking a second look at himself. The moment that David was taken out of the kingdom, Mephibosheth got on his knees and prayed. He didn't stop until this very moment. A fresh shave and haircut are going to make this man feel really good."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Mephibosheth couldn't do anything to help in the battle anyway, but he could certainly pray. I wholeheartedly believe that is what he did. So Mephibosheth says, \"If you have believed that I have done you wrong by not being with you in this battle, you can destroy me here. I deserve worse, but there was nothing more I could do to help you in this time of need. Just know that I believe in you and believe you are 'as an angel of God...'\" That part is a little dramatic, but it's obvious that he loves David."}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Mephibosheth is a humble man, that is for sure. He knows where he belongs, and I think that is what gives the king (and the Lord) so much compassion for him. God sees the humility of a man that knows where he belongs. He says, \"You are right, you belong down in the lowest valley, and that is why I want to show you my grace and power by bringing you up from there.\" The valleys are not fun places to be (that's just the honest truth). It is, however, the place where God will do the most work."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Mephibosheth didn't care about what land he was given. He only cared about the king and wanted to be by the side of King David as he reigned back on the throne. This is how we know that Mephibosheth is truly loyal to David. He never was found loyal to Absalom. He didn't want to be a part of choosing a side because his side was already chosen."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are some men (and women) who may not be physically adept to be a part of the trials and burdens of those that love and care for them. But they are spiritually adept to it. Mephibosheth wasn't there physically in the rain, wandering, and then having to fight against the others of Israel, but you can tell by his countenance that he was there with them in spirit. He came out on the other side of this battle looking the same way as them."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are people in the church who are needful in this area. I thank God that I know there are men praying for me today. I praise the Lord for those in the past who have gone through my hurt from a distance. There are people who live halfway across the country whom I know are praying for me regularly. They may not be here to physically go through the storm and trials with me, but they are here with me spiritually, and that equals the same amount of help in my eyes. I believe it equaled out to the same help in David's eyes as well."}]}]},{"start":31,"end":39,"title":"Barzillai's Offer","entries":[{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Man, there are people from all sorts of walks of life coming to this pivotal moment in David's time. Remember Barzillai helped David during this trial by giving him beds, and food."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And it came to pass, when David was come to Mahanaim, that Shobi the son of Nahash of Rabbah of the children of Ammon, and Machir the son of Ammiel of Lodebar, and Barzillai the Gileadite of Rogelim, Brought beds, and basons, and earthen vessels, and wheat, and barley, and flour, and parched corn, and beans, and lentiles, and parched pulse, And honey, and butter, and sheep, and cheese of kine, for David, and for the people that were with him, to eat: for they said, The people is hungry, and weary, and thirsty, in the wilderness.","ref":"2 Samuel 17:27-29"},{"kind":"para","text":"David loved that so much that he is offering this man and his family a place with them in Israel."}]},{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Barzillai knows that he is up there in age. All he would be (in his own eyes) is another mouth to feed. He couldn't do anything to help David or his people. He had a life where he was, and he was sufficient to stay there until his passing."}]},{"start":35,"end":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Man, to be old does not sound fun. These are all things that people strive for in this life. We live to make decisions. We live to eat and drink good food. We live to hear music and enjoy art. This man was old enough to miss all of that now. His mind isn't as sharp, his tongue has lost its taste buds, and he can barely hear anything for enjoyment, let alone for helping."}]},{"start":36,"end":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Barzillai knew that David deserved his position. That is why he helped. That was his ultimate goal (just to please the Lord in this matter). He needed David back where he belonged, and he knew it was the right thing to do. Not to mention David’s son was kind of a heinous ruler. This Barzillai didn't belong in the land either, though, and he knew it. He was not an Israelite. David wanted him to convert though. He had the privilege to do that (and perhaps he does in his own homeland, we will never know). David is forgiving to quite a few people this day. It was what was missing with his son Absalom. He knows that he will never have a chance to forgive and converse with his son again, and that has taught him to take his chances while he has them today."}]},{"start":38,"end":38,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chimham was young enough to be of service to Israel. It was an opportunity of a lifetime for this young man. He was about to have his whole life changed, and was going to be taken care of by the king."}]},{"start":39,"end":39,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Again, the crossing of Jordan is a very spiritual picture of salvation and refilling. For David, it was a refilling moment (entering back into the fold). For Barzillai, it was a moment of salvation (alife change to fulfill his days for the Lord). I believe that is the blessing that David bestows upon him. That is what he will have when he returns back to his family. He will have a spirit from the Lord to look to."}]}]},{"start":40,"end":43,"title":"Israel's Upset With Judah","entries":[{"start":41,"end":41,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, the drama isn't over yet. There is always some drama that has to be faced after such an occurrence. Thankfully, it won't be David that has to deal with this particular drama at the moment, and the men of Judah will stand up for him. But they seemed to be upset because it was Judah that brought David back, and not all of Israel. It was only one portion of the Israelites. They thought that was strange. They would have all liked to be a part of this ceremony and the decision to bring David back."}]},{"start":42,"end":42,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David comes from that tribe. He is a Judain (is that how you would say it?) They didn't do it as a bribe of any sort, they did it because they know David and he is their family."}]},{"start":43,"end":43,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ugh, these men just won't stop. David was not just a piece of prosperity to these people. All of Israel was receiving their king back; they should be content with that, even if it was just Judah making that a matter. Israel was having their issues with this decision because they respected Absalom, but they didn't respect the place of the king. If they did, they wouldn't have allowed the overthrow to happen in such a mocking way. Now they are complaining because Judah has made David king without the rest of the tribes’ permission. David was already king. Israel didn't make the decision to throw him out, and they couldn't make that decision unless they strived to kill David. That would be the only way to keep David from becoming their king again. These people just don't know when to let up…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":30},{"start":31,"end":39},{"start":40,"end":43}]},
    "20": {"chapter":20,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 20"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we see David still reeling over the loss of his son. Joab gets the kings attention and provides reason to help him move forward. There will be plenty of time to weep after David is settled back on the throne. Right now, there are pieces that still need to be picked up. Judah is in agreeance to the decision to place David back in his proper place, but the rest of Israel doesn't seem to know what to think about it. A ceremony takes place that leads David back to power, and people start to complain to Judah about this decision."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see David placed back in his proper place, but there are a vast majority of people who are not happy with the idea. One man (particularly) decides that one rebellion is not enough for these people, and commands another one on the heals of the last. David has to set up and try and end this second rebellion as quickly as possible, and he has a very small cast of characters who are on his side (namely the children of Judah alone)."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":3,"title":"Israel Doesn't Take","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If the children of Saul (who were Benjamite) can forgive David, there should be no reason that these men shouldn't forgive him as well. These people know that David just lost his son, and that was a disturbing thing to David, and yet they don't seem to consider those thoughts at all. Judah is proclaiming David back as king, but there are a great number of people who are against it."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Belial\" means \"worthlessness.\" This man was a just person that looked for trouble. He was there to cause problems, not look for solutions. I'm sorry to say this, but there are a lot of Belial men in the church today. They don't want to encourage or edify people. They want to question everything, and gossip about people. They want to prod and poke, and try and get people to rebel against the status quo. They want to stir up trouble, and all for what? So that people can be confused and hurt?"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"If any man teach otherwise, and consent not to wholesome words, even the words of our Lord Jesus Christ, and to the doctrine which is according to godliness; He is proud, knowing nothing, but doting about questions and strifes of words, whereof cometh envy, strife, railings, evil surmisings, Perverse disputings of men of corrupt minds, and destitute of the truth, supposing that gain is godliness: from such withdraw thyself.","ref":"1 Timothy 6:3-5"},{"kind":"para","text":"I will give this man (and others in the church) the benefit of the doubt and assume that they have genuine concerns that need to be addressed. This is something, however, that could be talked about in a more private setting (especially after the events that had just transpired that day). But either way, he is setting up for another revolt, and David is just starting to come back to his people..."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Oh man... It says \"every\" man of Israel. This Sheba fellow seems to have a lot of pull. \"Sheba\" means \"seven.\" It's the Lord’s perfect number, yet this man is anything but perfect. It's a familiar name, seeing as we have some places that are named after it. But this man seems to get the attention of all the people, and they all seem to agree with him. They are headed back to their tents to start another battle. Everyone, except Judah."},{"kind":"para","text":"Judah was proud of their king. They were willing to go to battle against these people. Now, before we start taking sides, I think it's important to note how David reacted to Saul when Saul was pursuing him. Instead of going up to battle Saul, he was doing everything in his power to avoid him and wait for the Lord to deal with it. That is a very proper example of a man following the Lord. We can either take vengeance into our own hands and suffer the consequences, or we can allow the Lord to judge and deal with the consequences. Every one of us has that choice in every situation. These men of Israel had that choice as well. But they are choosing to place the problem into their own hands, and that is not going to bode well with the Lord. David is God's chosen leader, whether Israel liked it or not."},{"kind":"para","text":"Today, we have chosen leaders over us. You may not like the Biden administration, but this is the man in which God has chosen for us. Rightfully so, we deserve to suffer for the way in which we have turned from following the Lord. David, however, was not making the people suffer. He was making the people prosper. He had his hangups, and his problems, but he was a man that knew how to take correction, and follow after the Lord. He has been through the grinder enough lately, and yet these people don't think he's quite beaten down plenty. So, instead of allowing the Lord to decide, they will decide for the Lord."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Not to bring back the mental picture of Absalom's sin, but these were the women in which Absalom defiled. David had put them away. He was not going to abuse them. They were not allowed to marry, and were not allowed to do much outside of the palace. David took care of them until they passed away (I assume of old age eventually), but David could not do much more with them. He was cleaning out the past here."},{"kind":"para","text":"We will notice that Israel (that is the eleven tribes pictured here) and Judah have a bit of a split in their decisions here. We will see that happen over and over again as we continue to read. I believe that's for a reason (bigger than the tribes even understood)."}]}]},{"start":4,"end":13,"title":"Amasa's Sin","entries":[{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have read that the king's business requires haste;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And David said unto Ahimelech, And is there not here under thine hand spear or sword? for I have neither brought my sword nor my weapons with me, because the king's business required haste.","ref":"1 Samuel 21:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"Yet, Amasa sure is taking his sweet time. He doesn't seem to think there is much reason to hurry in this business. I mean, it's not like Israel and Judah are going to go to war or anything... But then again, he was a man that served Absalom, not David. He liked the ruling of Absalom, so he wasn't going to take David's words seriously. Amasa was being the captain of David's army, but Amasa couldn't be trusted."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Keeping Joab in charge is the smarter move. Joab has already proved himself to be a good leader for David's army, and so he should never have tried to place any trust in Amasa. David has not said much about his feelings on any of these matters, but you can tell that this is all troubling to him. He still has not collected himself from the events that have transpired, and he is trying to work with a steady mind and speed. I feel for David."},{"kind":"para","text":"I may not have gone through much of his turmoil (losing children in the manner he did, or having my leadership defiled and stolen from my own son), but I know how it is to continue to minister and lead with a broken heart. It is not an easy task to handle. I would be praying (as I'm sure David is in his own mind) that the season would end. Just a short break. We know those are his prayers because he writes his thoughts down in song form through the book of Psalms."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Amasa was not doing his job. It seems Joab thought of Amasa as a traitor. This was no accident. This was an intentional killing from Joab because Amasa was nonchalant about every move that David desired to make. If I'm honest, I kind of side with Joab in this matter. Was this the proper way to deal with it? Probably not. If I were Joab, I would have sent Amasa back to the king. If he didn't think it worthy to go back, then perhaps there would be room to try him and kill him. It would have to be an agreement from the whole tribe, though. Joab just gets Amasa out of the way, though, and ends this quite quickly."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that the other men understood Joab’s purpose behind this as well. If you are for David, go to the battle. If you are not, stay put and away from David and his people. In seasons of war, you are either with your people or against them. There is no idleness."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don't think any of them were prepared for Joab's killing here. I hesitate to call it murder, but I think it really was. He murdered this man, Amasa. But Amasa could have very much become a threat, seeing as he was fumbling with swords and taking his time with the king’s requests. This man was not serious about David being back on the throne. He was serious when Absalom was there, and everyone knew that. Joab knew Amasa couldn't be trusted and was set as an example to the people that were standing there. The people (because of the rash decision here) are stunned and just stand there staring at this lifeless, gushing body on the floor. So the man calling the troops to order moves the body out of the way and then stands before them again, ready to go to battle, and that’s when they finally start taking this matter seriously."},{"kind":"para","text":"I can picture this moment as a bit humorous. I know it's serious and it should be taken seriously, but I think of the people all just completely dumbfounded at Amasa's body and the man standing there shouting, \"If you are for David and Joab, then follow us to battle...\" The insinuation in everyone's mind was, \"...and if not, then you will be like Amasa?\" So they just stand there staring with their mouths open, unsure of how to register this situation. The man moves the body (probably dragging it), and the men all watch as it's being taken off the road. The cloth is put over his face, and then the men watch as the announcer gets back into the street. The man looks at the soldiers and turns around and starts walking with Joab, and they all follow after, assuming they don't want to be like Amasa. This is why I love these books of Samuel so much. I try to get the readers into a mindset as if they are there in front of these people, being an eyewitness to the scene, but the writers of these two books (specifically) are great at giving the full picture."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":22,"title":"A Wise Woman","entries":[{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So, there are a few in the surrounding cities that thought it appropriate to take Sheba out. This man was trying something that would not be wise against David at this juncture, and some people understand that."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So Sheba is at Abel, trying to hide and build his forces. The quicker that Joab and his men deal with him, the quicker the rebellion can seize and war can be contained."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"A \"wise\" woman. She is a Godly woman, and she is going to give Joab some advice that will hopefully be helpful here."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joab is attentive. Perhaps they all knew that this woman was a wise, godly woman. Or maybe they are just now being told about her words. Joab does well to bend an ear here. We, too, would do well to bend an ear to wise, godly older women. Don't shun them or forsake them because they might talk a lot. Even in the middle of battle, Joab took a moment to listen to some sound advice from a woman who knows a thing or two."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"She says, \"You know Joab, I've been around for some time, and my family grew up here. Why do you think it's such a good idea to destroy my home? I'm just a mother trying to get by in this place, and you are trying to take that away.\" She's looking out for the innocent people who are about to lose their homes, their protection, and (possibly) their own lives. She doesn't want Joab to make a mess of things here. Maybe there's a better way to deal with this problem than to start destroying things and ruining their livelihoods."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joab has a heart. I think that is clear (and revealed) here. We could have questioned it before, but because of this scene, we understand that Joab is actually sympathetic towards this woman. He doesn't want to make things difficult for the innocent people here. He just wants to end another civil war before it has a chance to start."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This makes me laugh. It's not funny, but I can picture this lady saying, “Listen, don't destroy my city. Let’s be civilized about this. What is it that you are doing here?\" Joab says, “No, ma'am, I just need one person only. His name is Sheba. He doesn't even belong here. He is an enemy to the king.\" So she says, “Okay, young lad, I will see to this personally and cut this guy’s head off and throw it over the wall. You stay put.\" I am embellishing a bit, but you get the picture. Very interesting woman."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"She knew the right people to talk to that would deal with this matter. She (of course) wasn't going to fight against Sheba and believe she would win. She had help from the best people in her circle. I assume she gave plenty of advice to the people in this city, and these people were extremely grateful for her spirit and wisdom, so there were no questions asked when she requested this thing to be done. They thought, \"I mean, this is the wisest woman here. She wants someone's head cut off, let’s cut someone’s head off.\" Nobody had to lift a finger to battle. The city of Abel took care of it on their own. Perhaps more problems should be resolved with talking rather than with instinct, but I digress."}]}]},{"start":23,"end":26,"title":"David's New Team","entries":[{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joab was a serious man for the cause of the king. He was a man of action (which could be troubling), but he was certainly specific about which side of the fence he was on. His decisions might be rash, but they are guaranteed to be precise. These names may not mean too much to us at the moment, but to David they were men that were trustworthy. They were good at the positions he put them in. David knows how to assemble and lead a team that is capable. He is putting things in order so that another rebellion does not easily occur like it has these past two times."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":26}]},
    "21": {"chapter":21,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 21"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: There have been some serious drama and tedious turmoil for the Israelites (and specifically the household of David) in these past few chapters. It doesn't seem like anything is letting up. Another rebellion was beginning to rear its head in our last chapter, and Joab was there to make sure it didn't escalate. He went after the man who was leading this new rebellion and a \"wise woman\" from the city (the rebellion leader was hiding in) helped Joab get this man and kill him so that the city wouldn't be destroyed. This was the first success story in quite some time that we have seen in the kingdom of Israel."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, I wish I could say that it starts off on the heels of success and continues that vain... But it doesn't, and it seems like it's one thing after the other for David and the Israelites."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":6,"title":"The Famine","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This seems like a weird thing to bring up all of a sudden. Saul has been gone and dead for quite some time now, and God is just now beginning to bring famine. Now, we have noticed that David has gone through some heavy trials and burdens (one right after the other), and so when David has been experiencing so much pressure all around him, he likely assumes it's his own doing that the famine is there. So he doesn't question anything for two years, but the third year hits and he is starting to become confused as to why God is not letting up. Hasn't David had enough?"},{"kind":"para","text":"Well, apparently the famine was not for David. It was for Saul. What did Saul do? He killed the Gibeonites. When did this happen? Well, I don't know, but apparently it happened at some point, and David doesn't seem to question it (either because he knew about it, or because he just simply believed the Lord)."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The writer of 2 Samuel is cleaning this up a little to help us understand the context. I'm assuming the writer also understood that we don't know what is happening here. The Gibeonites were not part of the Israelites, but there was an oath between them that should not be broken, and at some point, Saul broke that oath."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Even after Saul's passing, David is still tasked with fixing his mistakes. There have been a lot of mistakes that David has had to atone for because of Saul. Saul was not a very wise man."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Gibeonites seem to be cordial. They don't request bloodshed. They are not \"eye-for-eye\" types of people. Maybe they just had a high respect for David and his people (even though Saul was a cruel man). Saul did plenty of heinous things in his time. He even killed a whole group of priests of his own people!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Abiathar shewed David that Saul had slain the LORD's priests.","ref":"1 Samuel 22:21"},{"kind":"para","text":"But David is insistent upon helping these Gibeonites, because if they don't forgive them, the Lord is not going to let up on the famine."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a very serious offer that is being made. If you don't remember the treaty that was made, we can go back to Joshua 9 and look at it. They lie about who they are to Joshua so they won't be killed by Joshua, and Joshua and his men make a promise to them."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Joshua made peace with them, and made a league with them, to let them live: and the princes of the congregation sware unto them. And it came to pass at the end of three days after they had made a league with them, that they heard that they were their neighbours, and that they dwelt among them. And the children of Israel journeyed, and came unto their cities on the third day. Now their cities were Gibeon, and Chephirah, and Beeroth, and Kirjathjearim. And the children of Israel smote them not, because the princes of the congregation had sworn unto them by the LORD God of Israel. And all the congregation murmured against the princes. But all the princes said unto all the congregation, We have sworn unto them by the LORD God of Israel: now therefore we may not touch them. This we will do to them; we will even let them live, lest wrath be upon us, because of the oath which we sware unto them. And the princes said unto them, Let them live; but let them be hewers of wood and drawers of water unto all the congregation; as the princes had promised them. And Joshua called for them, and he spake unto them, saying, Wherefore have ye beguiled us, saying, We are very far from you; when ye dwell among us? Now therefore ye are cursed, and there shall none of you be freed from being bondmen, and hewers of wood and drawers of water for the house of my God. And they answered Joshua, and said, Because it was certainly told thy servants, how that the LORD thy God commanded his servant Moses to give you all the land, and to destroy all the inhabitants of the land from before you, therefore we were sore afraid of our lives because of you, and have done this thing. And now, behold, we are in thine hand: as it seemeth good and right unto thee to do unto us, do. And so did he unto them, and delivered them out of the hand of the children of Israel, that they slew them not. And Joshua made them that day hewers of wood and drawers of water for the congregation, and for the altar of the LORD, even unto this day, in the place which he should choose.","ref":"Joshua 9:15-27"},{"kind":"para","text":"They were actually helpful to the Israelites in their beginnings. The Lord had commanded the Israelites to kill all the people in the land (because they were wicked and evil), but these people were protected because Joshua thought they came from a distant land. Saul apparently messed that up somewhere, and now his children are about to pay for his mistakes. God takes these matters seriously and obviously hasn't forgotten."}]}]},{"start":7,"end":9,"title":"Saul's Mistakes","entries":[{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Mephibosheth would be spared for a few reasons, but the main reason being that David and Jonathan made an oath to each other too. If the Lord would take the oath towards the Gibeonites seriously, then it would only stand to reason that the Lord would take the oath between David and Jonathan seriously as well. So David made sure that Mephibosheth was not mixed in with this mess."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You might be thinking to yourself \"but I thought David was sparing Mephibosheth?\" Apparently, there was another Mephibosheth in their group. He was a cousin to this other Mephibosheth. Either that or David brings Mephibosheth along just as an example? I don’t think that’s the case though. Today, Jonathan is quite a common name. Just in my church alone, there are about five Jonathans. So, when a name was liked, people often utilized it for their own families. In this case, they are part of the same family, but they are suffering for their grandfathers’ doing."},{"kind":"para","text":"Saul had quite a lot of grandchildren, it seems. Seven grandkids is a lot. I couldn't imagine what these families were going through at this time. This could not have been an easy thing for any of them to experience. I don't even think it was an easy thing for David to experience. As the leader of the nation, he had to do what he had to do, and it was better to get it done quickly than to prolong the inevitable. Saul's mistakes didn't only cost him the kingdom; it cost him his whole household."}]}]},{"start":10,"end":11,"title":"Rizpah Suffers","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"She just lost her children. She was Saul's concubine (which was already a sin in itself), but it still could not have been easy for her. These children of hers were fully grown adults, mind you, but that doesn't make it easier. I couldn't imagine being in her shoes. Perhaps when Saul had passed away, all of his children, wives, and grandchildren thought that the nightmare was over. Maybe they thought that they could just put this out of their mind and move forward."},{"kind":"para","text":"Saul's evil keeps rearing its head, and they keep having to reopen old wounds. Rizpah is undone in this moment, and she has a fast, a cry, and a breakdown before the Lord. That is sometimes what we need. She wouldn't eat until the rain came and the atonement satisfied the Lord to break them out of the famine. How long that took? I don't know."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":14,"title":"David Buries Saul and His Family","entries":[{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David finally had a funeral for this dead king and his family. It certainly was time. Some of the Israelites had already had a funeral for Saul and his people at the end of 1 Samuel:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And they took their bones, and buried them under a tree at Jabesh, and fasted seven days.","ref":"1 Samuel 31:13"},{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, David was not a part of this procession, and neither was the remainder of Saul's family. They needed a proper burial for this family, and Rizpah seems to be the spark that David needed to officially put this family to rest. He will have bones to bury from nine bodies this day, all from the same family tree. This act was the final seal to the Lord's judgement being lifted."},{"kind":"para","text":"God has been judging the land of Israel for quite some time. They have been going through the ringer, and it could not have been easy for David to discern all of these things lately. He's hoping (I believe) this might be the last of it for a while, and he could possibly get some rest now... Except the chapter isn't over..."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":17,"title":"David's Slowing","entries":[{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Unprovoked, the Philistines have come back to attack Israel once again. They chose to do this now (I assume) because they had probably gotten word that Israel was in a very weak state at this time. They had been going through a lot of different types of turmoil. Maybe they saw David's men go to get the bones of Jonathan and Saul, and that is what sparked them to return? Maybe they just heard about the flipping of ownership in the kingdom and came to rub some dirt in their eyes? I don't know. But they were a nuisance, and David was beginning to \"wax faint\" during this battle. He was usually pretty good with strategizing and accomplishing the fight, but now things are starting to look a little different."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Philistines seem to have a new giant to contend with, David. They apparently didn't learn their lesson with Goliath. I think a lot of David's problem here (now) is that he has developed in years at this point. He can't go to battle like the younger ones anymore. He has slowed down a bit and has become a little more fragile. Still a strong man, but not as nimble as he used to be."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They knew they had to protect their king. It was becoming a bit of a hazard to have David out on the field with them. David was a man of war, so this could not have been an easy decision for David. I have no doubt that David likely put up a bit of a fight against this advice, but in the end, the people were right. David had set up a wise and resilient army before him, and he would have to trust that they would do what's right by him and the kingdom. That is what he has spent most of his life preparing them for anyway. He knew he wasn't going to live forever, and at the moment, there was no one else to take his place. His oldest son would have been the one to take the kingdom, but it seems that his children are about as dysfunctional as the Philistines. David will have to figure out who is taking his place quickly, though."}]}]},{"start":18,"end":22,"title":"The Four Giants","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We are seeing the type of men that were fighting for David and Israel. \"Sibbechai\" means \"weaver.\" His name alone insinuates a valiant warrior. David had trained with (and picked) men that were good at what they did. The Philistines seem to be relentless at this time, and they are making a very hard push to hurt the Israelites. The people that David has put together to fight these wars, however, are not ones to trifle with. This man (Sibbechai) killed a son of the giants in this battle on this day (it seems the Philistines are materializing giants like they are Pez dispensers)."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Goliath had a brother! This is the first we are hearing about this. It seems that he (too) might have been a giant. \"Elhanan\" means \"God has been gracious.\" Even in the middle of all this trouble and turmoil, God's grace is somewhere in the midst."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Another giant. This time, we have a deformed giant. This giant was so massive that he had twelve fingers and twelve toes. Why does that matter? I don't know. But it's an interesting bit of information. It's like these giants were made in a lab."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have another man named Jonathan coming on the scene here. He killed this giant. It seems that the Philistines had a family of giants, and they thought that the strength and size of their giants would be sufficient to destroy the Israelites. They don't learn any lessons, though. They know that the Israelites have a God that takes care of them and fights their battles. We saw them with the ark at the beginning of 1 Samuel. We know that they know all about this God, and yet they are still ignorant of Him. They still reject the God of Israel. They still try and defy the Lord, and they lose every single time."},{"kind":"para","text":"The world has the same battle, and the same victories take place still today with God's children. All of these giants seemed to have come from Goliath (verse 22 illustrates). They were planning and preparing for a future battle after Goliath had been taken down. Someone said that David grabbed five stones because there were five giants. I don't know if I'd put any money in that, but it is certain that David could have taken down the giants had they all come to him at once. God was on David’s side. Even in this chapter, we see God is still on David's side, and (though he isn't in the middle of the battle anymore) David was being protected by the Lord in every step of the way."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":22}]},
    "22": {"chapter":22,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 22"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, David deals with some final issues in regards to Saul's past mistakes. He has to deal with Saul's family, and it's not a fun picture for David. He gives Saul a proper burial (however) and shortly after has to go into battle. During the time of battle, he realizes how old he is getting, and can't seem to keep up with his comrads in the fight (not like he used to be able to). They command him to stay behind from here on out so that he doesn't die in the middle of war. David has set up mighty men to take care of the needs, and those men were plenty skilled enough to fight the battle that David was too weak to fight."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, David takes a moment in his old age to write a song. This Psalm is useful to David because he recounts God's goodness and consistent mercy and forgiveness for David, even though David knows his sins have be great through his life. He is taking a look back through his life and seeing how God has led him every step of the way until this point. It's good for us to take a moment and look back at what God has done for us and how far He has gotten us."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":1,"title":"A Song From David","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David was good at writing down his thoughts and emotions. Perhaps that is the best way for us (as Christians) to keep our emotions under check. Having a written account out in the world will give us assurance that they are there for value, help, and remembrance. When I wrote music, they were my way of getting my feelings out of my brain and onto paper. It was me taking my thoughts and getting rid of them. I believe that is what an artist is supposed to do. If people jive with that, then that's great; you will have people who are able to relate and are able to appreciate your thoughts and music. If people don't desire to listen to it, they may not experience the same journeys as you, and that is completely okay as well. The interesting thing about the Psalms (however) is that everyone can understand and relate to what David is writing (including Jesus Himself). This is a Psalm David wrote just after his victories, and it's the 18th Psalm."}]}]},{"start":2,"end":16,"title":"Psalm 18","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I'm assuming he is looking back over his life (and especially the difficulties) and thinking about how good God has been to him. We can't forget that David is up there in age at this point. He is contemplating what life will be like for the kingdom after his death, and thinking about what the most important parts of each one of his trials were to remember. Of course, the important thing to remember was God's presence. That was what David continuously relied on, especially when he faced hard times. God was always there for him. He was David's rock and fortress."},{"kind":"para","text":"He is our rock, fortress, and deliverer too. We can depend on the Lord in the same way that David did, and we can rest assured that the Lord will uphold His children and take care of them. If we are His child, there is no better comfort and reliance we can have."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That is what each person in this world must come to terms with: The Lord is our savior. He is our salvation. He SAVES! What a beautiful thing to know that the Lord saves us. What an assurance for the believer. What a grand promise to us."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That is who we should always be looking for. Whether we are in trouble or in peace, look to the Lord. It is so easy for us to turn away from God and get comfortable with our own flesh and sin. Don't let it be our anthem. Don't let Satan sway you away from seeking and leaning on the Lord. That is what David has realized over and over again, and that is what he is remembering and writing down towards the end of his life. He can look back at all his troubles and sins and say, \"The Lord was always there. He was always providing. He was always correcting. He was always saving.\""}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now when we read this in the book of Psalms, we understand that it's depicting the thoughts and heartaches of Christ. The Psalms are a prophecy (the majority of them, if not all of them). They give the inside struggles of the Son of God, as well as the reliance He had on the Father. David was a sinner; Christ never sinned. But He certainly took our sin onto Himself and suffered for it."},{"kind":"para","text":"Because we know it's a prophecy (and that is what I mostly look into through Psalms), then I want to take this chapter to look at David. He is the one that sees the turmoil and trouble. He knows that he has messed up over and over again, and he deserves to be in hell, in the snares of death, and in distress. Yet, he also knows that when he called on God, God heard him. He listened and loved David."},{"kind":"para","text":"He listens and loves those that are struggling. Even in your sins, mind you. God could have just been done with David completely. He could have turned away from David and completely ignored him. He could have said, \"The kingdom will be ripped away from you, David; you are too much of a sinner to continue to be king.\" But He doesn't do that. Rather, He is patient with David. He corrects David and leads David. He is there to listen to our cries. He is there to feel our hurts with us. He has compassion enough to feel our woes."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord rescued and produced for David. He didn't just leave David hanging. He didn't just let him be completely defeated by the enemy. David had plenty of enemies. Including in his own lineage. His own sons were turning against him and disgracing his family name. He was not always there for his sons. He was not there like God was there for him. He knows that now."},{"kind":"para","text":"He regrets his actions, and there isn't much more he can do now that he is in his old age and his sons are going to be taking over his fortune. He has to set up what really matters, and the most important thing is that his sons remember the Lord and seek him with everything they have. That has been the theme for the people of Israel since the beginning of this book, and David realizes this at his old age. The Lord provides. He protects. He is able to fight the battles we cannot face. He is able to lift up the weakest and completely confounded spirits. He can bring up the most neglected souls, and make them joyous through the most difficult seasons. He has done it for David, He has done it for Samuel, for Joseph, for Jacob, for Isaac, for Abraham. He did it for Noah, and for Adam. He does it into the New Testament, and He does it for you and me. The Lord is always there to provide for His children."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":25,"title":"God Delivers","entries":[{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That isn't entirely true. We have read about the grave mistakes of David. What makes him so confident saying this here (other than it's also a prophecy), is that he knows and understands that the Lord forgives the repentant soul. He will not remember our sins. He doesn't want to remember our sins. He wants to forgive when we are genuine about how disturbed we are by our sins. To the readers, we see this and say, \"AHA, David is a hypocrite. He was not a perfect man like he says he is. He is lying here!\" That is how it is to the outside world with us as well..."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"To everyone else in the world, the Christian fails constantly. They see your failures and they say, \"I knew you weren't genuine. I knew you were a sinner.\" They are right to judge thus. The Lord, however, judges us quite differently. He sees our iniquities as Christians, and He forgives us in our repentance for them. We know that does not mean we will never sin again. Rather, when a Christian sins, we have Christ to defend our sins and forgive us. We are a work in progress. Those without Christ will not work on their sins and turn from them. Those with Christ will constantly be growing closer to Him and becoming more like Him."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"He must increase, but I must decrease.","ref":"John 3:30"}]}]},{"start":26,"end":37,"title":"Planting and Gathering","entries":[{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God will reward those based on the reward they seek. He will grow the seeds you plant. If you sow seeds of mercy, He will grow fruits of mercy. If you sow seeds of pride, He will grow bitter fruits... We must be cautious of the type of seeds we are throwing into the world. We must be certain it is something we want to reap later on. David understands this, and he demonstrates this here. We have seen in his life that he has made plenty of mistakes, and he understands that the trials he faced were from the seeds he planted. That is the truth for every single one of us."}]},{"start":37,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord rewards us with victory when we are pursuing Him also. Being faithful to the Lord is the best way to have God's presence. We should not seek to break God's heart. When we intentionally sin and go against the Lord, we are only breaking His heart. May that not be the seeds we sow..."}]}]},{"start":38,"end":46,"title":"Pursuing the Lord","entries":[{"start":43,"end":43,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord provided David with strength in the battle. I think if we looked back at every time David went into a fight, we can trace it to some sort of prayer in David's life. He was in constant prayer with the Lord. Not always! But more than most, I could argue. Especially during this time. David was a man that was seeking after God's desires. He was seeking after what would be the best way to please the Lord in the most diverse of situations. He knew that no matter how bleak the path may look, he could still take the road and please the Lord at the same time."},{"kind":"para","text":"Those times when he wasn't in prayer, he made some serious mistakes. He reaped the fruit of his labor, and it was not something he enjoyed. But all the trials did was bring him back to the Lord. That is what trials should do to God's children. It should bring us under full submission to God."}]},{"start":46,"end":46,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Strangers know the fame of David even still today. There are plenty of atheists and cultists who know the story of David. They know what David did to Goliath. They know that he was a man that was greatly used of God. They know he was a Jew, and in the lineage of the Israelites. They don't know his sins though. Most wouldn't be able to say what kind of father he really was. But strangers know him, and whether they want to believe in the historical accounts or not, they know that there is truth to his words and his psalms. They know that he was a man greatly used of God, and they know that he was a warrior of warriors."},{"kind":"para","text":"During this time, there were people coming from all around who tried to take David on in battle. They did not succeed, because the Lord was continuously with David. We see a direct difference in the battle between David and Saul. At the beginning, the Lord helped Saul in the battle. Saul messed it all up, and continuously rejected God's presence. Eventually God left the battle in the hands of Saul. This didn't happen with David. David was victorious over every enemy he faced. The Lord gave him a great knowledge of war strategy and tactic, and it helped keep David prospering and alive. It benefited everyone around David as well (including the strangers)."}]}]},{"start":47,"end":51,"title":"God Has Been Faithful","entries":[{"start":51,"end":51,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God has made promises to David. He has made promises to the lineage of Israel. He has made promises to the world and to His children. He has made promises that He has kept, and He has made promises that He will keep. David can look back at all of the times in his life (including the times of confusion) and say, \"God never forsook me.\" What a great view to have on the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"God has always been there for you and me. He has always provided for you. He has always remembered you and has never forsaken you through the times of difficulty. His grace is sufficient. It's complete. It's there to help you along and give you life and strength. Rely on the presence and faithfulness of the Lord. That is what David is pleading to the readers of this Psalm. That is the life that Jesus lived as well."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":25},{"start":26,"end":37},{"start":38,"end":46},{"start":47,"end":51}]},
    "23": {"chapter":23,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 23"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: Our last chapter saw the penning of David's Psalm (Psalm 18 if you want to read it). He showed and proved the goodness of God on his life through every season he faced. Even through the seasons of difficulty, God proved His goodness, and provided for David. David could not hold anything wrong agaist the Lord. God gave everything to David that David deserved, even if David thought it was bad at the time. David understands that he deserves worse."},{"kind":"para","text":"In fact, in this chapter, we see David's heart and humility. This chapter gives a deeper look at the men that David had positioned around him to help protect and guide the kingdom and people of Israel."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":7,"title":"God's Goodness","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Whoever is writing this account obviously thought highly of David here. He makes it clear that David is a man that is missed by the Israelites. Solomon, when he comes into reign (spoiler alert), makes a huge impact on Israel, but nothing like his father made. David was Israel's star pupil, and they knew that they would be missing David once he passed on. They also knew they were going to go through some serious changes with whomever was set up as their next king."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God's Spirit moved God's people to do God's Will. That was true in the Old Testament and it's true still today. We have seen a good group of people throughout history that have done some pretty astounding things by the Spirit of the Lord, and led some great revivals due to it. People have been martyred for this Spirit. They've been hated for this Spirit. Abused for this Spirit. They have also been loved (like David) for this Spirit."},{"kind":"para","text":"When David followed the Spirit's leading, the people around him prospered. That is evident still in every leader today. If our leaders do not follow the Spirit of the Lord, we cannot expect the results of their leading to end with anything fruitful or good. It doesn't matter how well-intentioned they may be. Without the Spirit, leaders are just blind people leading blind people..."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If only we had a government that saw the importance of this verse today. If we had leaders (including political ones) who ruled in \"the fear of God,\" we would have absolute direction in the way we should go. But if you look at our current leadership (that is under the Biden administration), all we have is a bunch of morally, physically, spiritually, and mentally confused people trying to direct a bunch of blind people. That is not a good direction to be heading in. That is how nations fall (that is how the Roman Empire fell, it's how the United Kingdom's reign fell, it's how the Babylonian Empire fell, the way the Egyptian ruling fell)... History has proven that leaders cannot be directed by their own opinions, but rather by a standard of something higher than themselves. And whose standard is higher than the Lord of creation?"}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"When I was growing up, I hardly ever watched the sun rise. We lived in the city, and we didn't always wake up super early to see it. Even when I lived in the desert (during my high school years), I still didn't see the sun rise. When it was rising, it was usually while I was getting ready for school. When I went to work, I usually got to the job before the sun came up. It wasn't until quite recently that I've gotten to experience the sunrise. There's nothing like it. It's such a beautiful picture of creation. Sunsets are cool and all, but there is nothing that can beat a sunrise. I've come to appreciate a good sunrise (especially on those Easter mornings before church)."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is the view of the Lord. He is like the light of the morning! That is a good reason to wake up early tomorrow if you have the chance. Take a moment to watch the sun rise. Sit and think about the splendor of God! Bring your family out if you need to, but you must personally take a time to just watch the sun come up (even if it's not very often)."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David seems to be saying that his house is not worthy of God's presence. We are not worthy to see the sun rise. We are certainly not worthy of His salvation. We are sinful people, and yet He has given us the splendor of knowing Him and seeing His beautiful creation! If you need another reason to wake up a bit earlier tomorrow, here it is! He allows you the opportunity to see His creation, and something as majestic as the sunrise."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Touching God's holiness with impure hands is not acceptable. There are a lot of people today who claim a belief in God, but they certainly don't know Him personally. They (perhaps) see the sunrise and don't find God's glory in it. In fact, they are sunset people. They see the clouds and the purple hues in the sky and they say, \"Wow, what an amazing view!\" Completely rejecting and forgetting the maker of it. Sadly, even we can sometimes be like this as God's children. When good things are given to us by the Lord, we forsake them and give Him no glory or thanks."},{"kind":"para","text":"Worse is a \"good man\" who doesn't know Christ and serving others in the church. None of us are deserving of God's good gifts, but those that don't know Him receive them and abuse them, and that's even worse than a saved sinner receiving them and utilizing them for himself. Even still, whatever we get, we should be in constant communion with the Lord, thanking Him for His good gift."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"title":"Adino","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We will see that these men that are mentioned are men that came to David during his biggest trials in life. We have Adino mentioned first. \"Adino\" means \"his ornament.\" He was an \"Tachmonite.\" \"Eznite\" means he was \"strong\" or \"sharp.\" He used a single spear against eight hundred people and killed them all."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":10,"title":"Eleazar","entries":[{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Eleazar\" means \"God has helped.” He was an \"Ahohite.\" The scene that is depicted here is an obvious sign that God has certainly helped him. He was too tired to continue on, but by some sort of providential pushing, he continued the fight and completed the course, allowing a victory for the Israelites all by himself."},{"kind":"para","text":"I imagine about halfway through the battle, he was too tired to lift his sword, and yet God protects him and gets him through to the end. I also imagine that when he was done defeating the Philistines at this day, he was probably found lying on the ground when his brothers in arms came to him. He was too tired to even stand, but he got through it with the help of God."}]}]},{"start":11,"end":12,"title":"Shammah","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Shammah\" means \"Astonishment.\" He was a \"Hararite.\" You would think that defending lentils wouldn't be that important, but during that time they were in need of food. Israel had faced several famines (including one with David), and they needed the food. So this man's act of boldness for this food was what ultimately led the Israelites into continuation. He not only defended the food, but he won a victory against the Philistines who were trying to steal it, ALL BY HIMSELF. I guarantee the men that left him there saw him after the battle and were astonished."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":17,"title":"An Unworthy Gift","entries":[{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that the Philistines were blocking the way to Bethlehem. That is David's hometown, if you don't recall. He was born and raised there, and there was no way to get there at the moment other than through the \"troop\" of Philistines that had set up a garrison blocking the path."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I assume that David loved this water. It seems strange, and you would think that all water would taste the same, but David favored this water particularly. I suppose I could get it. When I moved to Texas the first time (as a teenager), I didn't have much money. I could not afford to eat much. I didn't go out to eat hardly ever, and cereal was the best thing to live on. Thankfully, I had people who provided food for me, so I didn't starve to death. One thing I missed when I was there was In-N-Out. After the first month, I knew it was not that important. But the second, third, even fourth month, I was missing it dearly. I could not help but think about it and the taste that I missed so much. I ended up moving back to California, and the first place I went when I landed was In-N-Out. It was the best burger I ever had. When I moved to Texas the second time, they had finally put an In-N-Out burger over there. It wasn't very close by, but it was a little taste of home. Except it wasn't the same. It was In-N-Out, but it didn't taste right. I couldn't tell you what was off, but it just wasn't California's burgers. Perhaps that is how David is feeling here. Other water is just as good, but nothing beats that well's water in Bethlehem where he was raised."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I'm sorry, why? Think of the effort here. These men heard that their dying king wanted this water, and they risked their necks for it, and instead of David receiving it with gladness, he pours it on the ground. The mighty men could have easily just taken water from a water source nearby, and David would have been none the wiser to it, but they were serious about serving their king (even to the point of battling to get a specific type of water for him). This almost seems disgraceful. Let's see what his reasoning is..."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So, because these men put their lives on the line to get this water, he feels as if he is not worthy to receive it. Obviously, David felt like he didn't deserve the water, and he was willing to sacrifice his \"longing\" for this specific water because his friends were willing to sacrifice themselves to get it. It was a sign of humility on the part of David. He may never be able to drink that water again, but he certainly doesn't want to drink it unworthily."},{"kind":"para","text":"This was also a sign of sacrifice from David. He was literally sacrificing (by dumping the water out) to the Lord in thankfulness. The men that got the drink for David were not angry with David's sacrifice; they were honored even more by David's act here. They cared about their king, and this act was the exact reason they were willing to put their lives on the line for him. And their willingness to put their lives on the line for David is the exact reason David doesn't feel worthy of this gift."}]}]},{"start":18,"end":23,"title":"The Other Mighty Men","entries":[{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Abishai\" means \"My father is a gift.\" He is David's nephew, if you don't remember. We have spoken about him before already. He was the captain of the three men that were just mentioned a moment ago. He killed three hundred. His story is astounding, there's no doubt about that, but it isn't nearly as astounding as the other three men's stories. David saw him as more honorable, however, and that is why he was made captain."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Benaiah\" means \"Jehovah has built.\" This man must have been built like a beast himself. He kills two \"lionlike\" men (whatever that might mean), kills a literal lion (in the snow, mind you), and an Egyptian with the Egyptian’s own spear that the Egyptian had in his own hand. What a wild guy! He was a son of a valiant man, so he was brought up in, and (likely) trained to fight for the Israelites. Perhaps he didn't kill three hundred men, but he was certainly someone that had some strength and might. Because of it, he was stationed as a leader of the guards."}]}]},{"start":24,"end":39,"title":"The Rest of the Thirty-Seven","entries":[{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Asahel\" means \"God-made.\" \"Elhanan\" means \"God has been gracious.\" These people's names are a good reminder to David of God's provision and presence."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a different Shammah. I assume that this name is a pretty common one in the region, because it will come up again in a few verses. \"Elika\" means \"my God rejects.\" David doesn't reject him. God rejects Israel's enemies."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Helez\" means \"He has saved.\" God has used these mighty men to save the Israelites more times than they even know they deserved. While the Israelites may not have known them well, David takes very special notice of these men and puts them in prominent and trusted positions for him and the people. \"Ira\" means \"Watchful of a city.\" I wonder what his job would have been?"}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Abiezer\" means \"My father is help.\" \"Mebunnai\" means \"Building of Jehovah.\" It is quite amazing to me that these names all have a connection to the leading and provision of the Lord. Even \"Ira\" has a leading to God. God watches over the city. Abiezer's father could very well be the Lord, and the Lord is their help."}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Zalmon\" means \"Shady.\" You might say, “Well, that's not a very Godly name.\" Shady usually implies that this person does some very evil dealings. However, the Lord could easily be infused with this name as well."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The LORD is thy keeper: the LORD is thy shade upon thy right hand.","ref":"Psalm 121:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Maharai\" means \"Impetuous.\" \"Impetuous\" means \"Passion.\" Usually impulsive. The Lord has a passion for His creation, and He does whatever it takes to reach the lost."}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Heleb\" means \"Milk.\" \"Okay Jonathan, how will you reference God in this one?\" Actually this one is still easy!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"As newborn babes, desire the sincere milk of the word, that ye may grow thereby: If so be ye have tasted that the Lord is gracious.","ref":"1 Peter 2:2-3"},{"kind":"para","text":"God's Word is milk, and His Word is also Christ. \"Ittai\" means \"With me.\" That is the comfort of a man who is dealing with battles. To know God is with you gives us boldness to do difficult things."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Benaiah\" was another name that these people must have liked. \"Hiddai\" means \"For the rejoicing of Jehovah.\""}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Abialbon\" means \"God is my father.\" \"Azmaveth\" means \"strong unto death.\" The Lord must be his strength, otherwise his death would come sooner than he ventured."}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Eliahba\" means \"God hides.\""}]},{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Ahiam\" means \"mother's brother.\" Okay... so this one is not easy to reference back to the Lord. They can't all be winners. His parents were pretty direct on this name. I'm not sure I want to know the story behind it... Even still, we could link to the fact that (through Christ) we are born into the family of God and are given brother (and sister) relationships in this family. Even Mary would be labeled the sister of Christ when she came to trust Him as her Lord and Savior."}]},{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Eliphelet\" means \"God is deliverance.\" \"Eliam\" means \"God is kinsman.\""}]},{"start":35,"end":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Hezrai\" means \"enclosed.\" To be enclosed by the protection of God is all a man needs."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Yea, they shall sing in the ways of the LORD: for great is the glory of the LORD.","ref":"Psalm 138:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Paarai\" means \"gaping.\" The Lord's protection and direction is wide open for those that are willing to receive it and follow Him. His providence is available to all."}]},{"start":36,"end":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Igal\" means \"He redeems.\" \"Bani\" means \"Built.\" A lot of these men were \"built.\" If they were not built in their physical bodies, they will certainly be built in their spirits and mental capacity. David had men that were built in all ways encircling him. This is a wise thing to do. Be around people who are smarter than you, because you will learn more from them if you do."}]},{"start":37,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Zelek\" means \"Fissure.\" That is God's salvation! It's there if you are willing to search it out. \"Nahari\" means \"snorter.\" I don't know what to say about this one... Maybe he had an interesting laugh."}]},{"start":38,"end":38,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ira must be another popular name. “Gareb” means \"Scabby.\" This man must have had some sort of skin disease, because this is not a normal thing. Even still, he is beautifully and wonderfully made."}]},{"start":39,"end":39,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Uriah\" means \"Jehovah is my light.\" This is the same Uriah that David had killed. He was a mighty man... That is a very sad thing to know. Due to David's sin, he was willing to kill one of his strong men... Thankfully, the writer has memorialized this man. He was worthy of that much."},{"kind":"para","text":"Thirty-seven mighty men is a lot to reference. To find a single dedicated man in the church today is a very rare thing. To find a dedicated man in any corporation or any line of business is unheard of today. There are not many who care about their leaders enough to be dedicated without an incentive. These men were dedicated to their king because they cared very much about him. He showed care for them as well. He knew their strengths and he helped them develop into stronger warriors. He made them mightier by taking special notice of them and building upon their strengths. I want to really drive this home."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is the way in which we should be leading all of our businesses. God has given people certain strengths for certain things, and it would be a waste of our time, their time, and their building to squander those strengths and not encourage them in building those strengths up. Be watchful of what other people are building and provide for them opportunities to grow in those things that the Lord has blessed them with. Otherwise, we are nothing but a hindrance to them, and God will not let that go unnoticed."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":39}]},
    "24": {"chapter":24,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 24"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we have a bit of a lift from what we have been reading throughout this book of scripture. David has gone through quite a lot of chaos in this second half of Samuel, and it seemed like all hope was lost for David before he reclaimed his throne. Even after taking it back, there was a lot of problems that had to be dealt with and taken care of. David takes a moment to recognize God's goodness, and address some of the men that have helped him get along for this long. These men had power and triumph and were willing to go to great lengths for their king. David was assured that his people were worth quite a lot in their positions."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we would hope that (seeing as it's the last chapter of this book) it would end on a very cohesive and soft ending. It doesn't. It doesn't even feel like an ending if I'm being honest. But it's such a good chapter of scripture, and a great reminder of God's goodness, and mercy. It's a reminder of God's sacrifice for us, and that is what makes this chapter so important, and why I think this chapter ends off with a bit of a cliffhanger. David's last written task in the books of Samuel is one of immense faith and following. This is what David goes through in his old age, mind you. It doesn't matter what age we may be, we still need to grow our faith in the Lord."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":9,"title":"Numbering the People","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now David was in his years, and there was no way he could have left his throne comfortably without thinking, “Well, what happens next? Who is going to be my successor? Will the people succeed after I'm gone?\" The Israelites had had a small taste of what it's like to have bad leadership (on two occasions now), and they were prone to falling into the same mischief. David wanted to know if things were well taken care of and up to par with the Lord's people. Except, David had already received his assurance."},{"kind":"para","text":"There was no reason for David to do this here (he knows that there are far more people this time around than when he first started). He was to solemnly trust in the Lord, and in His providence and guiding, not in the number of people. But the Lord allows David to number the people. He allows David to work on his own faith. That is what faith is, by the way, it's an assurance."},{"kind":"para","text":"God has never, and will never produce faith without assurance. If you are not assured in your faith, you can simply go to God for wisdom (His Word is full of it). We are not following the Lord in \"blind faith\" by just jumping without fully trusting. He always gives us the means to trust Him, and to understand His ways. It says that the Lord’s anger was \"kindled against Israel\" when this took place. Why? Well, it was not the Lord that instructs David to do this, this time. We know this because we are told in a separate part of scripture who it is that instructs David."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Satan stood up against Israel, and provoked David to number Israel.","ref":"1 Chronicles 21:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"It is Satan leading David this way. The Lord allows it to happen and gives the permission, but He lets it be known that this was not something He wanted from David. His anger is kindled. Worse yet, David can't even do it himself. He is sending Joab to do it for him."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Not even Joab understands why this is important to David. He was thinking, \"Boy, David is old. He must be going mad. There really is no reason to do this. There are so many people that it would take quite some time to number all of them. It's sufficient to know that there are a great many people, and that should be enough.\" So he is respectful in his question, but the question is (somewhat) disrespectful to the king. The king asks something, you are to do it. There is no questioning asked. But the spiritual nature of this just didn't add up. If the king was sinning by doing this, then Joab would be sinning with him by following this command."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, it might not seem like much, but nine months and twenty days of counting is a long time for something so strangely tedious. I presume they had all the people stand in rows or lines as they got into each city. It would have been quite difficult to count people as they moved around. Maybe they made a count by household. Either way, this was a long, arduous task that didn't make much sense, and Joab and his people must have been quite weary by the end of it."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"One million and three hundred thousand, for those that are keeping score. I don't believe that was the literal number though. They may have started off with literal numbers, but they just thought \"you know what, this will take our entire lifetimes, let’s just start rounding to the nearest five, and we can go from there.\" After the eight hundred thousandth person, I'm sure the rounding grew a little more. I am assured that there were well over a million people though. It just would have been extremely rare to have such a round number like this."},{"kind":"para","text":"That's still a lot of people for one particular kingdom. They were quite large here. To us, that is a lot of people. That's plenty of people. David will feel comfort in that. That is actually the problem though. David's comfort should rely on the Lord, not the amount of people. That was the same problem that Saul faced as well. He relied on his own might and strength to pursue the Philistines. He went to battle with the Philistines in his own power, and it was this young man named David who went out with some rocks and a sling to take down one of their biggest men. It was the power and direction of the Lord, not the might of the king’s army and people. If it was based on their own might, the king and his people would have taken the Philistines without a problem. But it's not. And David knows this. He was just being led by a false voice."}]}]},{"start":10,"end":14,"title":"David is Guilty","entries":[{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So, if we stop right here, we will assume that David simply had a change of heart. The Lord was able to get his attention. No. That's not what happens exactly. David was constantly thinking about what the Lord wanted, and going back to God for direction. The Psalms are full of that. David always makes a mistake, and then turns to the Lord in pleading. The Lord is gracious. I don't think David knew that the Lord was angry at the beginning of this. It wasn't until a prophet comes to him and tells him his fault."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So God gives David a choice after his sin. He gives this choice through Gad. Gad tells David the choices, and it's in those choices that David realizes that the Lord is angry with him and pleads for God's forgiveness. I think it should be addressed that David knows that he is best off asking the Lord about all his moves before making them. Yet we see several times throughout David's life where he forgets."},{"kind":"para","text":"We are better off keeping the Lord in remembrance before making any sudden moves as well. Pray about your direction and decisions. If you do not get an answer on those prayers, it is better that you don't continue forward until you do. David doesn't make a prayer request for this, though, and so he is stuck with the consequences of his actions here. But he gets to make a choice. That doesn't happen very often with the Lord. The Lord will usually just do what needs to be done, and then it will be over. David was a gifted man, and God takes special care of David. He could not visit David personally at the moment, but He will certainly make His presence known through His children."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The famine and pestilence were God's actions specifically. All three of them (of course) were God's actions, but the famine and pestilence will require His people to lean on Him (they can't blame man for that). Running from the enemy was a greater pain according to David. He would rather the Lord be in charge of the burden than for the burden to come from people of war (especially because David can't run like he used to). There was a greater chance that the people would turn to the Lord and survive that kind of turmoil than for them to be chased by men and be attacked with brutal force."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":17,"title":"David Repents","entries":[{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"In the grand scheme of things, seventy thousand is not that much. It's more than should have been, however, and David knows that."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Does David see an angel here? Well, it says \"...when he saw the angel that smote the people...\" so it would seem quite likely. I am under the impression that he at least saw the pestilence (if not the literal angel producing this problem). He was assured that it was the Lord doing it when people started dropping. He finally goes to the Lord and says, \"God, don't take it out on them. I am the problem here. I was the one that sinned against you, take it out on me and my family. Take it out on my lineage.\" Crazy enough, it will be taken out on David's lineage in due season. But that same faith is what saves the people."},{"kind":"para","text":"Though Christ will take the punishment for our sins, it is our faith that will ultimately get us saved. He dies for us, but His death is meaningless if we do not have the faith to follow Him. God's anger is still against you without the salvation of Christ. The pestilence is still in your land if you don't have faith... I think if David truly repented with Gad, the Lord would have done this a very different way. It wasn't until now that David finally comes before God to beg for forgiveness."}]}]},{"start":18,"end":25,"title":"A Worthy Sacrifice","entries":[{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David is commanded by the Lord to make an altar to the Lord in the land of a Jebusite (not an Israelite). That is interesting to me. The location of this place is even more interesting to me."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then Solomon began to build the house of the LORD at Jerusalem in mount Moriah, where the LORD appeared unto David his father, in the place that David had prepared in the threshingfloor of Ornan the Jebusite.","ref":"2 Chronicles 3:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is the place in which Abraham went to offer his son Isaac."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he said, Take now thy son, thine only son Isaac, whom thou lovest, and get thee into the land of Moriah; and offer him there for a burnt offering upon one of the mountains which I will tell thee of.","ref":"Genesis 22:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"It's also the place in which the Lord will eventually be crucified. It's debated, but this is the place that continually shows up in Scripture as some of the greatest and most prominent sacrifices in Israel's history. We can study that better in John or Genesis. This is the place in which David was when he pleaded with the Lord, and so it will be the place in which the Lord will do a work here for him."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David is placing value in this land. The land was already valuable, and it will remain valuable. Whether or not the Israelites know the value of that land today is debatable, but it is an extremely valuable mount that holds quite a lot of history."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Araunah saw the power and strength of David, and wanted to just give everything to him. He thought, \"I certainly don't want to be on this king’s bad side. I think it would be far better to just let him have the land.\" Mind you, this land belonged to Araunah. It was not as if he had stolen the land, or was stealing it from some other individual. But he was giving it over to the king, and look at what David thought of this action next..."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Sacrifices come at a cost. There is a great price to pay for sacrifice. The cost is usually a life, and something that is of a lot of value. We could argue that life is the most valuable thing in this world. Without it, you don't hold any value. You also can't gain more value without it. If you are dead, you can't read this, you can't work for Christ, you can't support your family, or pour into the world, or grow a legacy. Dead people can't do anything except lie in a grave. The living is the only thing that can actually do anything, and that is why they are of value."},{"kind":"para","text":"All life is valuable. That includes the people who are likely reprobates, serial killers, rapists, and child abusers. Does this mean we should be sympathetic for their sins? Absolutely not. It just means that they have value to change, and be a part of a bigger change than themselves. They can come to know Christ and live for Him just like anyone else in this world. Christ can direct them in a better direction than they ever thought was possible. They hold value in our society. They can put value in our society if they so choose (most don't)."},{"kind":"para","text":"May we be valuable Christians reading this today. We are so prone to try and get free land (something that someone else has worked on), because it is our Christian right to have the land (or the church, or the ministry, or whatever good thing you want to replace \"land\" with). It is better to add value into it than to take it for free. We, Christians (of all people), should understand that whatever your passion and desire is, is worth something. There is value placed in the land. There is value placed in your church, and in the ministry. There is value placed in the family you are leading. Put more value in it. Make it worth more to you if the Lord has granted you access to it. If the Lord has called you into something, don't do it without pouring your faith and value into it. Don't give the Lord worthless sacrifices."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It is this same act that will save humanity. A sacrifice will be made on this mount for the whole world. It is David's faith that saves the Israelites. It is Abraham's faith that saves his son Isaac. It will be our faith in the sacrifice of Christ that will save us. What a powerful and valuable lesson learned in this chapter. This chapter may have seemed bleak (especially at the start), but it ends with a very important reminder. The Lord is looking to save His people. He has a means to do it, and we must trust in Him by faith that He will keep to His promises, and do whatsoever He pleases. Those things He chooses to do are always good. It can't be anything else."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":25}]},
  },
};
